[[:db/cas 17592186094250 :answer/value "OT^(→) = a + \\frac{1}{2}b" "\\overrightarrow{OT} = \\mathbf{a} + \\frac{1}{2}\\mathbf{b}"] [:db/cas 17592186094251 :answer/value "OT^(→) = \\frac{2}{3}a + \\frac{1}{3}b" "\\overrightarrow{OT} = \\frac{2}{3}\\mathbf{a} + \\frac{1}{3}\\mathbf{b}"] [:db/cas 17592186094252 :answer/value "OT^(→) = \\frac{1}{3}a + \\frac{2}{3}b" "\\overrightarrow{OT} = \\frac{1}{3}\\mathbf{a} + \\frac{2}{3}\\mathbf{b}"] [:db/cas 17592186094253 :answer/value "OT^(→) = \\frac{1}{2}a + b" "\\overrightarrow{OT} = \\frac{1}{2}\\mathbf{a} + \\mathbf{b}"] [:db/cas 17592186094254 :answer/value "OT^(→) = \\frac{1}{2}a + \\frac{1}{2}b" "\\overrightarrow{OT} = \\frac{1}{2}\\mathbf{a} + \\frac{1}{2}\\mathbf{b}"] [:db/cas 17592186095365 :answer/value "OR^(→) = \\frac{3}{4}p + \\frac{1}{4}q" "\\overrightarrow{OR} = \\frac{3}{4}\\mathbf{p} + \\frac{1}{4}\\mathbf{q}"] [:db/cas 17592186095366 :answer/value "OR^(→) = \\frac{2}{3}p + \\frac{1}{3}q" "\\overrightarrow{OR} = \\frac{2}{3}\\mathbf{p} + \\frac{1}{3}\\mathbf{q}"] [:db/cas 17592186095367 :answer/value "OR^(→) = \\frac{1}{2}p + \\frac{1}{2}q" "\\overrightarrow{OR} = \\frac{1}{2}\\mathbf{p} + \\frac{1}{2}\\mathbf{q}"] [:db/cas 17592186095368 :answer/value "OR^(→) = \\frac{1}{3}p + \\frac{2}{3}q" "\\overrightarrow{OR} = \\frac{1}{3}\\mathbf{p} + \\frac{2}{3}\\mathbf{q}"] [:db/cas 17592186095369 :answer/value "OR^(→) = \\frac{1}{4}p + \\frac{3}{4}q" "\\overrightarrow{OR} = \\frac{1}{4}\\mathbf{p} + \\frac{3}{4}\\mathbf{q}"] [:db/cas 17592186096451 :question/worked-solution "![](images/76/76b9867a7c1ef484922241ebe44272f0104e406c31310bf36227a13850f50d8b.png)\n\nIn $\\bigtriangleup ONM$ shown above, we have:\n\n- $ON=2|\\mathbf{n}|$;\n\n- $\\overrightarrow{ON}$ and $\\mathbf{n}$ have the same direction\n\nHence, $\\overrightarrow{ON}=2\\mathbf{n}$.\n\nSimilarly:\n\n- $NM=2|\\mathbf{k}|$\n\n- $\\overrightarrow{NM}$ and $\\mathbf{k}$ have the same direction\n\nHence, $\\overrightarrow{NM}=2\\mathbf{k}$.\n\nAdditionally, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & =\\overset{\\to }{ON}+\\overset{\\to }{NM} \\\\  & =2n+2k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $\\mathbf{m}=\\lambda\\mathbf{n}+\\mu\\mathbf{k}=2\\mathbf{n}+2\\mathbf{k}$. \n\nSince $\\mathbf{n} \\nparallel \\mathbf{k},$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients of $\\mathbf{n}$ and $\\mathbf{k}$, we get:\n\n- $\\lambda=2$ and $\\mu=2$" "![](images/76/76b9867a7c1ef484922241ebe44272f0104e406c31310bf36227a13850f50d8b.png)\n\nIn $\\bigtriangleup ONM$ shown above, we have:\n\n- $ON=2|\\mathbf{n}|$;\n\n- $\\overrightarrow{ON}$ and $\\mathbf{n}$ have the same direction\n\nHence, $\\overrightarrow{ON}=2\\mathbf{n}$.\n\nSimilarly:\n\n- $NM=2|\\mathbf{k}|$\n\n- $\\overrightarrow{NM}$ and $\\mathbf{k}$ have the same direction\n\nHence, $\\overrightarrow{NM}=2\\mathbf{k}$.\n\nAdditionally, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{m} & =\\overset{\\to }{ON}+\\overset{\\to }{NM} \\\\  & =2\\mathbf{n}+2\\mathbf{k}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $\\mathbf{m}=\\lambda\\mathbf{n}+\\mu\\mathbf{k}=2\\mathbf{n}+2\\mathbf{k}$. \n\nSince $\\mathbf{n} \\nparallel \\mathbf{k},$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients of $\\mathbf{n}$ and $\\mathbf{k}$, we get:\n\n- $\\lambda=2$ and $\\mu=2$"] [:db/cas 17592186096452 :question/worked-solution "From the diagram, we see that\n\n$$\n\\overrightarrow{OX} = \\overrightarrow{OA}+\\overrightarrow{AX}.\n$$\n\nWe're told that $\\overrightarrow{OA} = \\mathbf{a},$ so we just need to work out $\\overrightarrow{AX}.$\n\nFirst, from $\\triangle OAB,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =\\overset{\\to }{AO}+\\overset{\\to }{OB} \\\\  & =-\\overset{\\to }{OA}+\\overset{\\to }{OB} \\\\  & =-a+b \\\\  & =b-a.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $AX:XB=3:1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\overrightarrow{AX}=\\dfrac{3}{4}\\overrightarrow{AB}=\\dfrac{3}{4}(\\mathbf{b}-\\mathbf{a}).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{OX} & =\\overset{\\to }{OA}+\\overset{\\to }{AX} \\\\  & =a+\\frac{3}{4}(b-a) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}a+\\frac{3}{4}b.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "From the diagram, we see that\n\n$$\n\\overrightarrow{OX} = \\overrightarrow{OA}+\\overrightarrow{AX}.\n$$\n\nWe're told that $\\overrightarrow{OA} = \\mathbf{a},$ so we just need to work out $\\overrightarrow{AX}.$\n\nFirst, from $\\triangle OAB,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =\\overset{\\to }{AO}+\\overset{\\to }{OB} \\\\  & =-\\overset{\\to }{OA}+\\overset{\\to }{OB} \\\\  & =-\\mathbf{a}+\\mathbf{b} \\\\  & =\\mathbf{b}-\\mathbf{a}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $AX:XB=3:1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\overrightarrow{AX}=\\dfrac{3}{4}\\overrightarrow{AB}=\\dfrac{3}{4}(\\mathbf{b}-\\mathbf{a}).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{OX} & =\\overset{\\to }{OA}+\\overset{\\to }{AX} \\\\  & =\\mathbf{a}+\\frac{3}{4}(\\mathbf{b}-\\mathbf{a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\mathbf{a}+\\frac{3}{4}\\mathbf{b}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186097046 :question/problem "![](images/c2/c2406e6f3cb76f50bfcc73a348b38d93b7a24da8e4314e0b3523bb4cbf143364.png)\n\nConsider the diagram above, where $\\overset{\\to }{OA}=a,$ $\\overset{\\to }{OB}=b$, and $T$ divides $\\overset{―}{AB}$ in the ratio $AT:TB=1:1.$ Find $\\overset{\\to }{OT}.$" "![](images/c2/c2406e6f3cb76f50bfcc73a348b38d93b7a24da8e4314e0b3523bb4cbf143364.png)\n\nConsider the diagram above, where $\\overset{\\to }{OA}=\\mathbf{a},$ $\\overset{\\to }{OB}=\\mathbf{b}$, and $T$ divides $\\overline{AB}$ in the ratio $AT:TB=1:1.$ Find $\\overset{\\to }{OT}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186097046 :question/worked-solution "From the diagram, we see that\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{OT}=\\overset{\\to }{OA}+\\overset{\\to }{AT}.\n$$\n\nWe're told that $\\overset{\\to }{OA}=a,$ so we just need to work out $\\overset{\\to }{AT}.$\n\nFirst, from $△OAB,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =\\overset{\\to }{AO}+\\overset{\\to }{OB} \\\\  & =-\\overset{\\to }{OA}+\\overset{\\to }{OB} \\\\  & =-a+b \\\\  & =b-a.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $AT:TB=1:1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{AT}=\\frac{1}{2}\\overset{\\to }{AB}=\\frac{1}{2}(b-a).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{OT} & =\\overset{\\to }{OA}+\\overset{\\to }{AT} \\\\  & =a+\\frac{1}{2}(b-a) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}a+\\frac{1}{2}b.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "From the diagram, we see that\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{OT}=\\overset{\\to }{OA}+\\overset{\\to }{AT}.\n$$\n\nWe're told that $\\overset{\\to }{OA}=\\mathbf{a},$ so we just need to work out $\\overset{\\to }{AT}.$\n\nFirst, from $△OAB,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =\\overset{\\to }{AO}+\\overset{\\to }{OB} \\\\  & =-\\overset{\\to }{OA}+\\overset{\\to }{OB} \\\\  & =-\\mathbf{a}+\\mathbf{b} \\\\  & =\\mathbf{b}-\\mathbf{a}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $AT:TB=1:1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{AT}=\\frac{1}{2}\\overset{\\to }{AB}=\\frac{1}{2}(\\mathbf{b}-\\mathbf{a}).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{OT} & =\\overset{\\to }{OA}+\\overset{\\to }{AT} \\\\  & =\\mathbf{a}+\\frac{1}{2}(\\mathbf{b}-\\mathbf{a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\mathbf{a}+\\frac{1}{2}\\mathbf{b}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186097164 :question/problem "![](images/81/816c4ecbfe49321bc6b2bc0d72eb61d6ddd1aeae7e76a275df0975439dde5865.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. Given that $c=a+\\lambda b,$ find $\\lambda .$" "![](images/81/816c4ecbfe49321bc6b2bc0d72eb61d6ddd1aeae7e76a275df0975439dde5865.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. Given that $\\mathbf{c}=\\mathbf{a}+\\lambda \\mathbf{b},$ find $\\lambda .$"] [:db/cas 17592186097164 :question/worked-solution "To find $\\lambda ,$ we want to match the expression\n\n$$\nc=a+\\lambda b.\n$$\n\nWe are given that one of the components is $a.$ So, we begin with the vector $a,$ and then find the remaining vector needed to reach the point $C.$\n\n![](images/67/6782f2fa59056afd9022edbaf69c44a991bf12a7ac6a190bea3cc08fc7a4996c.png)\n\nNote that in $△OAC,$ we have:\n\n- $AC=2.5|b|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{AC}$ and $b$ have the same direction\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{AC}=2.5b.$\n\nAdditionally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c & =\\overset{\\to }{OA}+\\overset{\\to }{AC} \\\\  & =a+2.5b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $c=a+\\lambda b=a+2.5b.$\n\nSince $a∦b,$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients of $b,$ we get:\n\n$$\n\\lambda =2.5\n$$" "To find $\\lambda ,$ we want to match the expression\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{c}=\\mathbf{a}+\\lambda \\mathbf{b}.\n$$\n\nWe are given that one of the components is $\\mathbf{a}.$ So, we begin with the vector $\\mathbf{a},$ and then find the remaining vector needed to reach the point $C.$\n\n![](images/67/6782f2fa59056afd9022edbaf69c44a991bf12a7ac6a190bea3cc08fc7a4996c.png)\n\nNote that in $△OAC,$ we have:\n\n- $AC=2.5|\\mathbf{b}|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{AC}$ and $\\mathbf{b}$ have the same direction\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{AC}=2.5\\mathbf{b}.$\n\nAdditionally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{c} & =\\overset{\\to }{OA}+\\overset{\\to }{AC} \\\\  & =\\mathbf{a}+2.5\\mathbf{b}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $\\mathbf{c}=\\mathbf{a}+\\lambda \\mathbf{b}=\\mathbf{a}+2.5\\mathbf{b}.$\n\nSince $\\mathbf{a}∦\\mathbf{b},$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients of $\\mathbf{b},$ we get:\n\n$$\n\\lambda =2.5\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186097165 :question/problem "![](images/a1/a1e251e00a99520cc392d031dbd05646ce07b65319d829e2cd8fc6f80e6e29a2.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. Given that $c=\\lambda a+\\mu b$, find $\\lambda $ and $\\mu .$" "![](images/a1/a1e251e00a99520cc392d031dbd05646ce07b65319d829e2cd8fc6f80e6e29a2.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. Given that $\\mathbf{c}=\\lambda \\mathbf{a}+\\mu \\mathbf{b}$, find $\\lambda $ and $\\mu .$"] [:db/cas 17592186097165 :question/worked-solution "![](images/60/601e049067de307ef2321ae9b516d1c7306d837ca0758c080074446a8c54389d.png)\n\nIn $△OAC$ shown above, we have:\n\n- $OA=2|a|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{OA}$ and $a$ have the same direction\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{OA}=2a.$\n\nSimilarly:\n\n- $AC=2|b|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{AC}$ and $b$ have the same direction\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{AC}=2b.$\n\nAdditionally, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c & =\\overset{\\to }{OA}+\\overset{\\to }{AC} \\\\  & =2a+2b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $c=\\lambda a+\\mu b=2a+2b.$ \n\nSince $a∦b,$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients, we get:\n\n- $\\lambda =2$ and $\\mu =2$" "![](images/60/601e049067de307ef2321ae9b516d1c7306d837ca0758c080074446a8c54389d.png)\n\nIn $△OAC$ shown above, we have:\n\n- $OA=2|\\mathbf{a}|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{OA}$ and $\\mathbf{a}$ have the same direction\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{OA}=2\\mathbf{a}.$\n\nSimilarly:\n\n- $AC=2|\\mathbf{b}|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{AC}$ and $\\mathbf{b}$ have the same direction\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{AC}=2\\mathbf{b}.$\n\nAdditionally, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{c} & =\\overset{\\to }{OA}+\\overset{\\to }{AC} \\\\  & =2\\mathbf{a}+2\\mathbf{b}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $\\mathbf{c}=\\lambda \\mathbf{a}+\\mu \\mathbf{b}=2\\mathbf{a}+2\\mathbf{b}.$ \n\nSince $\\mathbf{a}∦\\mathbf{b},$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients, we get:\n\n- $\\lambda =2$ and $\\mu =2$"] [:db/cas 17592186097181 :question/problem "![](images/4b/4b7c8c679171c1dcb6ef44b33696ba2ccce87b3f690643af979476d8dfb285ce.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. Given that $c=\\lambda a+3b$, find $\\lambda .$" "![](images/4b/4b7c8c679171c1dcb6ef44b33696ba2ccce87b3f690643af979476d8dfb285ce.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. Given that $\\mathbf{c}=\\lambda \\mathbf{a}+3\\mathbf{b}$, find $\\lambda .$"] [:db/cas 17592186097181 :question/worked-solution "To find $\\lambda ,$ we want to match the expression\n\n$$\nc=\\lambda a+3b.\n$$\n\nWe are given that one of the components is $3b.$ So, we first draw the vector $3b$ from the origin. Then we complete the path to the point $C.$\n\n![](images/f6/f67af4512a7ab61f862932a60dafc25ef62bc46335b7d7df323fe889f8728e6b.png)\n\nLet $P$ be the endpoint of the vector $3b.$ Then\n\n$$\nc=\\overset{\\to }{OP}+\\overset{\\to }{PC}.\n$$\n\nIn $△OPC,$ we have:\n\n- $PC=\\frac{1}{2}|a|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{PC}$ and $a$ have the same direction\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{PC}=\\frac{1}{2}a.$\n\nAdditionally, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c & =\\overset{\\to }{OP}+\\overset{\\to }{PC} \\\\  & =3b+\\frac{1}{2}a.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $c=\\lambda a+3b=\\frac{1}{2}a+3b.$\n\nSince $a∦b,$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients of $a,$ we get:\n\n$$\n\\lambda =\\frac{1}{2}\n$$" "To find $\\lambda ,$ we want to match the expression\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{c}=\\lambda \\mathbf{a}+3\\mathbf{b}.\n$$\n\nWe are given that one of the components is $3\\mathbf{b}.$ So, we first draw the vector $3\\mathbf{b}$ from the origin. Then we complete the path to the point $C.$\n\n![](images/f6/f67af4512a7ab61f862932a60dafc25ef62bc46335b7d7df323fe889f8728e6b.png)\n\nLet $P$ be the endpoint of the vector $3\\mathbf{b}.$ Then\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{c}=\\overset{\\to }{OP}+\\overset{\\to }{PC}.\n$$\n\nIn $△OPC,$ we have:\n\n- $PC=\\frac{1}{2}|\\mathbf{a}|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{PC}$ and $\\mathbf{a}$ have the same direction\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{PC}=\\frac{1}{2}\\mathbf{a}.$\n\nAdditionally, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{c} & =\\overset{\\to }{OP}+\\overset{\\to }{PC} \\\\  & =3\\mathbf{b}+\\frac{1}{2}\\mathbf{a}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $\\mathbf{c}=\\lambda \\mathbf{a}+3\\mathbf{b}=\\frac{1}{2}\\mathbf{a}+3\\mathbf{b}.$\n\nSince $\\mathbf{a}∦\\mathbf{b},$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients of $\\mathbf{a},$ we get:\n\n$$\n\\lambda =\\frac{1}{2}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186097240 :question/problem "![](images/fb/fb31653c9fc5031372c4b08e8fba0ec809c7e58a7a2321a8a7eb027b8eacd48a.png)\n\nConsider the diagram above, where $OP^(→) = p$, $OQ^(→) = q$ and $R$ divides $PQ^(―)$ in the ratio $PR: RQ = 1: 2$. Find $OR^(→)$." "![](images/fb/fb31653c9fc5031372c4b08e8fba0ec809c7e58a7a2321a8a7eb027b8eacd48a.png)\n\nConsider the diagram above, where $\\overrightarrow{OP} = \\mathbf{p}$, $\\overrightarrow{OQ} = \\mathbf{q}$ and $R$ divides $\\overline{PQ}$ in the ratio $PR: RQ = 1: 2$. Find $\\overrightarrow{OR}$."] [:db/cas 17592186097241 :question/problem "![](images/01/01940c9fa894d635300a49867da35d483b873a45d2a75204c20c58e4cb84c883.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. Given that $t=\\lambda r+\\mu s$, find $\\lambda $ and $\\mu .$" "![](images/01/01940c9fa894d635300a49867da35d483b873a45d2a75204c20c58e4cb84c883.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. Given that $\\mathbf{t}=\\lambda \\mathbf{r}+\\mu \\mathbf{s}$, find $\\lambda $ and $\\mu .$"] [:db/cas 17592186097241 :question/worked-solution "![](images/a0/a0d9fa1c366945e5e789b6361f929357af163db2e9be4a3ccc9023c12486aca6.png)\n\nIn $△ORT$ shown above, we have:\n\n- $OR=2|r|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{OR}$ has the opposite direction to $r$\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{OR}=-2r.$\n\nSimilarly:\n\n- $RT=2|s|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{RT}$ has the opposite direction to $s$\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{RT}=-2s.$\n\nAdditionally, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t & =\\overset{\\to }{OR}+\\overset{\\to }{RT} \\\\  & =-2r-2s.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $t=\\lambda r+\\mu s=-2r-2s.$ \n\nSince $s∦r,$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients, we get:\n\n- $\\lambda =-2$ and $\\mu =-2$" "![](images/a0/a0d9fa1c366945e5e789b6361f929357af163db2e9be4a3ccc9023c12486aca6.png)\n\nIn $△ORT$ shown above, we have:\n\n- $OR=2|\\mathbf{r}|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{OR}$ has the opposite direction to $\\mathbf{r}$\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{OR}=-2\\mathbf{r}.$\n\nSimilarly:\n\n- $RT=2|\\mathbf{s}|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{RT}$ has the opposite direction to $\\mathbf{s}$\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{RT}=-2\\mathbf{s}.$\n\nAdditionally, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{t} & =\\overset{\\to }{OR}+\\overset{\\to }{RT} \\\\  & =-2\\mathbf{r}-2\\mathbf{s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $\\mathbf{t}=\\lambda \\mathbf{r}+\\mu \\mathbf{s}=-2\\mathbf{r}-2\\mathbf{s}.$ \n\nSince $\\mathbf{s}∦\\mathbf{r},$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients, we get:\n\n- $\\lambda =-2$ and $\\mu =-2$"] [:db/cas 17592186235343 :question/problem "![](images/68/6882323d2c8017e95100a0ab9d1091fdf149e884ef27d2d2bf59ab7609b1a960.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. Given that $c=\\lambda a+2b,$ find $\\lambda .$" "![](images/68/6882323d2c8017e95100a0ab9d1091fdf149e884ef27d2d2bf59ab7609b1a960.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. Given that $\\mathbf{c}=\\lambda \\mathbf{a}+2\\mathbf{b},$ find $\\lambda .$"] [:db/cas 17592186235343 :question/worked-solution "To find $\\lambda ,$ we want to match the expression\n\n$$\nc=\\lambda a+2b.\n$$\n\nWe are given that one of the components is $2b.$ So, we first draw the vector $2b$ from the origin. Then we complete the path to the point $C.$\n\n![](images/a8/a8309dc45ac43c6642010e744de5a062d6575d48ba85bd642df39dd261f47422.png)\n\nLet $P$ be the endpoint of the vector $2b.$ Then\n\n$$\nc=\\overset{\\to }{OP}+\\overset{\\to }{PC}.\n$$\n\nIn $△OPC,$ we have:\n\n- $PC=2|a|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{PC}$ and $a$ have opposite directions\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{PC}=-2a.$\n\nAdditionally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c & =\\overset{\\to }{OP}+\\overset{\\to }{PC} \\\\  & =2b-2a.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $c=\\lambda a+2b=-2a+2b.$\n\nSince $a∦b,$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients of $a,$ we get:\n\n$$\n\\lambda =-2\n$$" "To find $\\lambda ,$ we want to match the expression\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{c}=\\lambda \\mathbf{a}+2\\mathbf{b}.\n$$\n\nWe are given that one of the components is $2\\mathbf{b}.$ So, we first draw the vector $2\\mathbf{b}$ from the origin. Then we complete the path to the point $C.$\n\n![](images/a8/a8309dc45ac43c6642010e744de5a062d6575d48ba85bd642df39dd261f47422.png)\n\nLet $P$ be the endpoint of the vector $2\\mathbf{b}.$ Then\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{c}=\\overset{\\to }{OP}+\\overset{\\to }{PC}.\n$$\n\nIn $△OPC,$ we have:\n\n- $PC=2|\\mathbf{a}|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{PC}$ and $\\mathbf{a}$ have opposite directions\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{PC}=-2\\mathbf{a}.$\n\nAdditionally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{c} & =\\overset{\\to }{OP}+\\overset{\\to }{PC} \\\\  & =2\\mathbf{b}-2\\mathbf{a}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $\\mathbf{c}=\\lambda \\mathbf{a}+2\\mathbf{b}=-2\\mathbf{a}+2\\mathbf{b}.$\n\nSince $\\mathbf{a}∦\\mathbf{b},$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients of $\\mathbf{a},$ we get:\n\n$$\n\\lambda =-2\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186235344 :question/problem "![](images/55/551316d1e36175ca302c5c472d4109a9bc9cadf32cc85d2a1751424a386070cc.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. Given that $c=\\lambda a-2b,$ find $\\lambda .$" "![](images/55/551316d1e36175ca302c5c472d4109a9bc9cadf32cc85d2a1751424a386070cc.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. Given that $\\mathbf{c}=\\lambda \\mathbf{a}-2\\mathbf{b},$ find $\\lambda .$"] [:db/cas 17592186235344 :question/worked-solution "To find $\\lambda ,$ we want to match the expression\n\n$$\nc=\\lambda a-2b.\n$$\n\nWe are given that one of the components is $-2b.$ So, we first draw the vector $-2b$ from the origin. Then we complete the path to the point $C.$\n\n![](images/40/40a0137d6227212db9b7ef9f349029838f3f51398790ec5e1c304e12c3743a0f.png)\n\nLet $P$ be the endpoint of the vector $-2b.$ Then\n\n$$\nc=\\overset{\\to }{OP}+\\overset{\\to }{PC}.\n$$\n\nIn $△OPC,$ we have:\n\n- $PC=\\frac{1}{2}|a|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{PC}$ and $a$ have opposite directions\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{PC}=-\\frac{1}{2}a.$\n\nAdditionally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c & =\\overset{\\to }{OP}+\\overset{\\to }{PC} \\\\  & =-2b-\\frac{1}{2}a.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $c=\\lambda a-2b=-\\frac{1}{2}a-2b.$\n\nSince $a∦b,$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients of $a,$ we get:\n\n$$\n\\lambda =-\\frac{1}{2}\n$$" "To find $\\lambda ,$ we want to match the expression\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{c}=\\lambda \\mathbf{a}-2\\mathbf{b}.\n$$\n\nWe are given that one of the components is $-2\\mathbf{b}.$ So, we first draw the vector $-2\\mathbf{b}$ from the origin. Then we complete the path to the point $C.$\n\n![](images/40/40a0137d6227212db9b7ef9f349029838f3f51398790ec5e1c304e12c3743a0f.png)\n\nLet $P$ be the endpoint of the vector $-2\\mathbf{b}.$ Then\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{c}=\\overset{\\to }{OP}+\\overset{\\to }{PC}.\n$$\n\nIn $△OPC,$ we have:\n\n- $PC=\\frac{1}{2}|\\mathbf{a}|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{PC}$ and $\\mathbf{a}$ have opposite directions\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{PC}=-\\frac{1}{2}\\mathbf{a}.$\n\nAdditionally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{c} & =\\overset{\\to }{OP}+\\overset{\\to }{PC} \\\\  & =-2\\mathbf{b}-\\frac{1}{2}\\mathbf{a}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $\\mathbf{c}=\\lambda \\mathbf{a}-2\\mathbf{b}=-\\frac{1}{2}\\mathbf{a}-2\\mathbf{b}.$\n\nSince $\\mathbf{a}∦\\mathbf{b},$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients of $\\mathbf{a},$ we get:\n\n$$\n\\lambda =-\\frac{1}{2}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186235345 :question/problem "![](images/73/73e0ac67304b5e7682abddb59fcd01a9e8aca88831a303bf5b31a72ff772228d.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. Given that $c=\\lambda a-3b,$ find $\\lambda .$" "![](images/73/73e0ac67304b5e7682abddb59fcd01a9e8aca88831a303bf5b31a72ff772228d.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. Given that $\\mathbf{c}=\\lambda \\mathbf{a}-3\\mathbf{b},$ find $\\lambda .$"] [:db/cas 17592186235345 :question/worked-solution "To find $\\lambda ,$ we want to match the expression\n\n$$\nc=\\lambda a-3b.\n$$\n\nWe are given that one of the components is $-3b.$ So, we first draw the vector $-3b$ from the origin. Then we complete the path to the point $C.$\n\n![](images/24/2484cf9fdda85c5c516babe6814f34c369ebdd91d6f100f0ce0fe4102e3c90a6.png)\n\nLet $P$ be the endpoint of the vector $-3b.$ Then\n\n$$\nc=\\overset{\\to }{OP}+\\overset{\\to }{PC}.\n$$\n\nIn $△OPC,$ we have:\n\n- $PC=3|a|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{PC}$ and $a$ have opposite directions\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{PC}=-3a.$\n\nAdditionally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c & =\\overset{\\to }{OP}+\\overset{\\to }{PC} \\\\  & =-3b-3a.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $c=\\lambda a-3b=-3a-3b.$\n\nSince $a∦b,$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients of $a,$ we get:\n\n$$\n\\lambda =-3\n$$" "To find $\\lambda ,$ we want to match the expression\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{c}=\\lambda \\mathbf{a}-3\\mathbf{b}.\n$$\n\nWe are given that one of the components is $-3\\mathbf{b}.$ So, we first draw the vector $-3\\mathbf{b}$ from the origin. Then we complete the path to the point $C.$\n\n![](images/24/2484cf9fdda85c5c516babe6814f34c369ebdd91d6f100f0ce0fe4102e3c90a6.png)\n\nLet $P$ be the endpoint of the vector $-3\\mathbf{b}.$ Then\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{c}=\\overset{\\to }{OP}+\\overset{\\to }{PC}.\n$$\n\nIn $△OPC,$ we have:\n\n- $PC=3|\\mathbf{a}|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{PC}$ and $\\mathbf{a}$ have opposite directions\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{PC}=-3\\mathbf{a}.$\n\nAdditionally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{c} & =\\overset{\\to }{OP}+\\overset{\\to }{PC} \\\\  & =-3\\mathbf{b}-3\\mathbf{a}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $\\mathbf{c}=\\lambda \\mathbf{a}-3\\mathbf{b}=-3\\mathbf{a}-3\\mathbf{b}.$\n\nSince $\\mathbf{a}∦\\mathbf{b},$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients of $\\mathbf{a},$ we get:\n\n$$\n\\lambda =-3\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186235565 :question/problem "![](images/ba/bae20e625c529ef2157faa4d534a19529c605c9002ed750e56723ff29e265226.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. If $c=\\lambda a-4b,$ then\n\n$\\lambda =$\n{{field-1}}." "![](images/ba/bae20e625c529ef2157faa4d534a19529c605c9002ed750e56723ff29e265226.png)\n\nCo