 can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients of $\\mathbf{a},$ we get:\n\n$$\n\\lambda =-3.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186235566 :question/problem "![](images/8e/8e6ccf522b5325c3b592ca9470810a95c5491f0b68f000ea774f6555cee0f47f.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. If $c=\\lambda a-2b,$ then\n\n$\\lambda =$\n{{field-1}}." "![](images/8e/8e6ccf522b5325c3b592ca9470810a95c5491f0b68f000ea774f6555cee0f47f.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. If $\\mathbf{c}=\\lambda \\mathbf{a}-2\\mathbf{b},$ then\n\n$\\lambda =$\n{{field-1}}."] [:db/cas 17592186235566 :question/worked-solution "To find $\\lambda ,$ we want to match the expression\n\n$$\nc=\\lambda a-2b.\n$$\n\nWe are given that one of the components is $-2b.$ So, we first draw the vector $-2b$ from the origin. Then we complete the path to the point $C.$\n\n![](images/64/646b8f1322f815e786f82eb8cfb3fa9e84a3c8d588c94910b0b702b607bfb2d8.png)\n\nLet $P$ be the endpoint of the vector $-2b.$ Then\n\n$$\nc=\\overset{\\to }{OP}+\\overset{\\to }{PC}.\n$$\n\nIn $△OPC,$ we have:\n\n- $PC=\\frac{3}{2}|a|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{PC}$ and $a$ have opposite directions\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{PC}=-\\frac{3}{2}a.$\n\nAdditionally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c & =\\overset{\\to }{OP}+\\overset{\\to }{PC} \\\\  & =-2b-\\frac{3}{2}a.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $c=\\lambda a-2b=-\\frac{3}{2}a-2b.$\n\nSince $a∦b,$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients of $a,$ we get:\n\n$$\n\\lambda =-\\frac{3}{2}\n$$" "To find $\\lambda ,$ we want to match the expression\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{c}=\\lambda \\mathbf{a}-2\\mathbf{b}.\n$$\n\nWe are given that one of the components is $-2\\mathbf{b}.$ So, we first draw the vector $-2\\mathbf{b}$ from the origin. Then we complete the path to the point $C.$\n\n![](images/64/646b8f1322f815e786f82eb8cfb3fa9e84a3c8d588c94910b0b702b607bfb2d8.png)\n\nLet $P$ be the endpoint of the vector $-2\\mathbf{b}.$ Then\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{c}=\\overset{\\to }{OP}+\\overset{\\to }{PC}.\n$