ly,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c & =\\overset{\\to }{OA}+\\overset{\\to }{AC} \\\\  & =a+2b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $c=a+\\lambda b=a+2b.$\n\nSince $a∦b,$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients of $b,$ we get:\n\n$$\n\\lambda =2\n$$" "To find $\\lambda ,$ we want to match the expression\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{c}=\\mathbf{a}+\\lambda \\mathbf{b}.\n$$\n\nWe are given that one of the components is $\\mathbf{a}.$ So, we begin with the vector $\\mathbf{a},$ and then find the remaining vector needed to reach the point $C.$\n\n![](images/0c/0c785e189beebb0a803bceba272db156af6d3b47c690ee9527876f77c65d1891.png)\n\nNote that in $△OAC,$ we have:\n\n- $AC=2|\\mathbf{b}|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{AC}$ and $\\mathbf{b}$ have the same direction\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{AC}=2\\mathbf{b}.$\n\nAdditionally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{c} & =\\overset{\\to }{OA}+\\overset{\\to }{AC} \\\\  & =\\mathbf{a}+2\\mathbf{b}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $\\mathbf{c}=\\mathbf{a}+\\lambda \\mathbf{b}=\\mathbf{a}+2\\mathbf{b}.$\n\nSince $\\mathbf{a}∦\\mathbf{b},$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients of $\\mathbf{b},$ we get:\n\n$$\n\\lambda =2\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186256371 :question/problem "![](images/58/58901401bfe81e040e22da6e07d543423db58a845009a80525eae131752874d4.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. Given that $c=\\lambda a+3b$, find $\\lambda .$" "![](images/58/58901401bfe81e040e22da6e07d543423db58a845009a80525eae131752874d4.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. Given that $\\mathbf{c}=\\lambda \\mathbf{a}+3\\mathbf{b}$, find $\\lambda .$"] [:db/cas 17592186256371 :question/worked-solution "To find $\\lambda ,$ we want to match the expression\n\n$$\nc=\\lambda a+3b.\n$$\n\nWe are given that one of the components is $3b.$ So, we first draw the vector $3b$ from the origin. Then we complete the path to the point $C.$\n\n![](images/3e/3effb654f4db5e1f207c671116baf648cb70f90781882a1fd758f32b610aefca.png)\n\nLet $P$ be the endpoint of the vector $3b.$ Then\n\n$$\nc=\\overset{\\to }{OP}+\\overset{\\to }{PC}.\n$$\n\nIn $△OPC,$ we have:\n\n- $PC=2|a|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{PC}$ and $a$ have the same direction\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{PC}=2a.$\n\nAdditionally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c & =\\overset{\\to }{OP}+\\overset{\\to }{PC} \\\\  & =3b+2a.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $c=\\lambda a+3b=2a+3b.$\n\nSince $a∦b,$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients of $a,$ we get:\n\n$$\n\\lambda =2\n$$" "To find $\\lambda ,$ we want to match the expression\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{c}=\\lambda \\mathbf{a}+3\\mathbf{b}.\n$$\n\nWe are given that one of the components is $3\\mathbf{b}.$ So, we first draw the vector $3\\mathbf{b}$ from the origin. Then we complete the path to the point $C.$\n\n![](images/3e/3effb654f4db5e1f207c671116baf648cb70f90781882a1fd758f32b610aefca.png)\n\nLet $P$ be the endpoint of the vector $3\\mathbf{b}.$ Then\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{c}=\\overset{\\to }{OP}+\\overset{\\to }{PC}.\n$$\n\nIn $△OPC,$ we have:\n\n- $PC=2|\\mathbf{a}|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{PC}$ and $\\mathbf{a}$ have the same direction\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{PC}=2\\mathbf{a}.$\n\nAdditionally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{c} & =\\overset{\\to }{OP}+\\overset{\\to }{PC} \\\\  & =3\\mathbf{b}+2\\mathbf{a}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $\\mathbf{c}=\\lambda \\mathbf{a}+3\\mathbf{b}=2\\mathbf{a}+3\\mathbf{b}.$\n\nSince $\\mathbf{a}∦\\mathbf{b},$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients of $\\mathbf{a},$ we get:\n\n$$\n\\lambda =2\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186256375 :question/problem "![](images/25/25717d521feeec25f7b4b722ca7b8a4ee9b5fdc8423bac06953e5f2adc8eef3d.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. Given that $t=\\lambda r+\\mu s$, find $\\lambda $ and $\\mu .$" "![](images/25/25717d521feeec25f7b4b722ca7b8a4ee9b5fdc8423bac06953e5f2adc8eef3d.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. Given that $\\mathbf{t}=\\lambda \\mathbf{r}+\\mu \\mathbf{s}$, find $\\lambda $ and $\\mu .$"] [:db/cas 17592186256375 :question/worked-solution "![](images/d5/d585c949d7de8cc3fa2d62bfb60990af12242c32ef3390e563f7e627844c5a88.png)\n\nIn $△ORT$ shown above, we have:\n\n- $OR=3|r|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{OR}$ and $r$ have the same direction\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{OR}=3r.$\n\nSimilarly:\n\n- $RT=2|s|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{RT}$ has the opposite direction to $s$\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{RT}=-2s.$\n\nAdditionally, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t & =\\overset{\\to }{OR}+\\overset{\\to }{RT} \\\\  & =3r-2s.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $t=\\lambda r+\\mu s=3r-2s.$ \n\nSince $s∦r,$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients, we get:\n\n- $\\lambda =3$ and $\\mu =-2$" "![](images/d5/d585c949d7de8cc3fa2d62bfb60990af12242c32ef3390e563f7e627844c5a88.png)\n\nIn $△ORT$ shown above, we have:\n\n- $OR=3|\\mathbf{r}|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{OR}$ and $\\mathbf{r}$ have the same direction\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{OR}=3\\mathbf{r}.$\n\nSimilarly:\n\n- $RT=2|\\mathbf{s}|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{RT}$ has the opposite direction to $\\mathbf{s}$\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{RT}=-2\\mathbf{s}.$\n\nAdditionally, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{t} & =\\overset{\\to }{OR}+\\overset{\\to }{RT} \\\\  & =3\\mathbf{r}-2\\mathbf{s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $\\mathbf{t}=\\lambda \\mathbf{r}+\\mu \\mathbf{s}=3\\mathbf{r}-2\\mathbf{s}.$ \n\nSince $\\mathbf{s}∦\\mathbf{r},$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients, we get:\n\n- $\\lambda =3$ and $\\mu =-2$"] [:db/cas 17592186256377 :question/problem "![](images/c9/c991e8d6cc9cb869db0a0a663830f255f33dd32756167a5e665b2e4318ae7014.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. Given that $r=\\lambda p+\\mu q$, find $\\lambda $ and $\\mu .$" "![](images/c9/c991e8d6cc9cb869db0a0a663830f255f33dd32756167a5e665b2e4318ae7014.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. Given that $\\mathbf{r}=\\lambda \\mathbf{p}+\\mu \\mathbf{q}$, find $\\lambda $ and $\\mu .$"] [:db/cas 17592186256377 :question/worked-solution "![](images/a9/a9dcb3e3cd825cf24103f3636b79418531f8b8a14a5f6c83b9b94780fdf8739b.png)\n\nIn $△OPR$ shown above, we have \n\n- $OP=4|p|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{OP}$ and $p$ have the same direction\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{OP}=4p.$\n\nSimilarly:\n\n- $PR=2|q|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{PR}$ has the opposite direction to $q$\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{PR}=-2q.$\n\nAdditionally, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\overset{\\to }{OP}+\\overset{\\to }{PR} \\\\  & =4p-2q.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $r=\\lambda p+\\mu q=4p-2q.$\n\nSince $p∦q,$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients, we get:\n\n- $\\lambda =4$ and $\\mu =-2$" "![](images/a9/a9dcb3e3cd825cf24103f3636b79418531f8b8a14a5f6c83b9b94780fdf8739b.png)\n\nIn $△OPR$ shown above, we have \n\n- $OP=4|\\mathbf{p}|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{OP}$ and $\\mathbf{p}$ have the same direction\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{OP}=4\\mathbf{p}.$\n\nSimilarly:\n\n- $PR=2|\\mathbf{q}|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{PR}$ has the opposite direction to $\\mathbf{q}$\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{PR}=-2\\mathbf{q}.$\n\nAdditionally, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{r} & =\\overset{\\to }{OP}+\\overset{\\to }{PR} \\\\  & =4\\mathbf{p}-2\\mathbf{q}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $\\mathbf{r}=\\lambda \\mathbf{p}+\\mu \\mathbf{q}=4\\mathbf{p}-2\\mathbf{q}.$\n\nSince $\\mathbf{p}∦\\mathbf{q},$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients, we get:\n\n- $\\lambda =4$ and $\\mu =-2$"] [:db/cas 17592186256378 :question/problem "![](images/fc/fc7d37da99173b7fa99f2f5f0bbf1478a54505628ed827488a18b3d01535f81f.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. Given that $l=\\lambda j+\\mu k$, find $\\lambda $ and $\\mu .$" "![](images/fc/fc7d37da99173b7fa99f2f5f0bbf1478a54505628ed827488a18b3d01535f81f.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. Given that $\\mathbf{l}=\\lambda \\mathbf{j}+\\mu \\mathbf{k}$, find $\\lambda $ and $\\mu .$"] [:db/cas 17592186256378 :question/worked-solution "![](images/6c/6c9273c0b89d4c06c8a8591b8e8e94b0c83b2d1d4bd04b64ed8cb0a185619b5f.png)\n\nIn $△OJL$ shown above, we have \n\n- $OJ=|j|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{OJ}$ has the opposite direction to $j$\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{OJ}=-j.$\n\nSimilarly:\n\n- $JL=2|k|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{JL}$ has the opposite direction to $k$\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{JL}=-2k.$\n\nAdditionally, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l & =\\overset{\\to }{OJ}+\\overset{\\to }{JL} \\\\  & =-j-2k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $l=\\lambda j+\\mu k=-j-2k.$\n\nSince $j∦k,$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients, we get:\n\n- $\\lambda =-1$ and $\\mu =-2$" "![](images/6c/6c9273c0b89d4c06c8a8591b8e8e94b0c83b2d1d4bd04b64ed8cb0a185619b5f.png)\n\nIn $△OJL$ shown above, we have \n\n- $OJ=|\\mathbf{j}|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{OJ}$ has the opposite direction to $\\mathbf{j}$\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{OJ}=-\\mathbf{j}.$\n\nSimilarly:\n\n- $JL=2|\\mathbf{k}|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{JL}$ has the opposite direction to $\\mathbf{k}$\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{JL}=-2\\mathbf{k}.$\n\nAdditionally, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{l} & =\\overset{\\to }{OJ}+\\overset{\\to }{JL} \\\\  & =-\\mathbf{j}-2\\mathbf{k}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $\\mathbf{l}=\\lambda \\mathbf{j}+\\mu \\mathbf{k}=-\\mathbf{j}-2\\mathbf{k}.$\n\nSince $\\mathbf{j}∦\\mathbf{k},$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients, we get:\n\n- $\\lambda =-1$ and $\\mu =-2$"] [:db/cas 17592186256379 :question/problem "![](images/41/41a40c03043152177c5bf6d7f56b51c09e57352480599b508d376018d87de1b3.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. Given that $c=\\lambda a+\\mu b$, find $\\lambda $ and $\\mu .$" "![](images/41/41a40c03043152177c5bf6d7f56b51c09e57352480599b508d376018d87de1b3.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. Given that $\\mathbf{c}=\\lambda \\mathbf{a}+\\mu \\mathbf{b}$, find $\\lambda $ and $\\mu .$"] [:db/cas 17592186256379 :question/worked-solution "![](images/cc/ccc52cbcbf17db97ad3849f157b5d6ab3e10f59846e2cd4b75507fc0a37c3c98.png)\n\nIn $△OAC$ shown above, we have:\n\n- $OA=3|a|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{OA}$ and $a$ have opposite directions\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{OA}=-3a.$\n\nSimilarly:\n\n- $AC=2|b|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{AC}$ and $b$ have opposite directions\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{AC}=-2b.$\n\nAdditionally, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c & =\\overset{\\to }{OA}+\\overset{\\to }{AC} \\\\  & =-3a-2b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $c=\\lambda a+\\mu b=-3a-2b.$ \n\nSince $a∦b,$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients, we get:\n\n- $\\lambda =-3$ and $\\mu =-2$" "![](images/cc/ccc52cbcbf17db97ad3849f157b5d6ab3e10f59846e2cd4b75507fc0a37c3c98.png)\n\nIn $△OAC$ shown above, we have:\n\n- $OA=3|\\mathbf{a}|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{OA}$ and $\\mathbf{a}$ have opposite directions\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{OA}=-3\\mathbf{a}.$\n\nSimilarly:\n\n- $AC=2|\\mathbf{b}|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{AC}$ and $\\mathbf{b}$ have opposite directions\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{AC}=-2\\mathbf{b}.$\n\nAdditionally, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{c} & =\\overset{\\to }{OA}+\\overset{\\to }{AC} \\\\  & =-3\\mathbf{a}-2\\mathbf{b}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $\\mathbf{c}=\\lambda \\mathbf{a}+\\mu \\mathbf{b}=-3\\mathbf{a}-2\\mathbf{b}.$ \n\nSince $\\mathbf{a}∦\\mathbf{b},$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients, we get:\n\n- $\\lambda =-3$ and $\\mu =-2$"] [:db/cas 17592186256381 :question/problem "![](images/c4/c4d26b3db6446f586ca47634d9f9a3a004968a0081b8859ce46b4b4af799d066.png)\n\nConsider the diagram above, where $\\overset{\\to }{OH}=h,$ $\\overset{\\to }{OI}=i$ and $J$ divides $\\overset{―}{HI}$ in the ratio $HJ:JI=3:7.$ Find $\\overset{\\to }{OJ}.$" "![](images/c4/c4d26b3db6446f586ca47634d9f9a3a004968a0081b8859ce46b4b4af799d066.png)\n\nConsider the diagram above, where $\\overset{\\to }{OH}=\\mathbf{h},$ $\\overset{\\to }{OI}=\\mathbf{i}$ and $J$ divides $\\overline{HI}$ in the ratio $HJ:JI=3:7.$ Find $\\overset{\\to }{OJ}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186256381 :question/worked-solution "From the diagram, we see that\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{OJ}=\\overset{\\to }{OH}+\\overset{\\to }{HJ}.\n$$\n\nWe're told that $\\overset{\\to }{OH}=h,$ so we just need to work out $\\overset{\\to }{HJ}.$\n\nFirst, from $△OHI,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{HI} & =\\overset{\\to }{HO}+\\overset{\\to }{OI} \\\\  & =-\\overset{\\to }{OH}+\\overset{\\to }{OI} \\\\  & =-h+i \\\\  & =i-h.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $HJ:JI=3:7,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{HJ}=\\frac{3}{10}\\overset{\\to }{HI}=\\frac{3}{10}(i-h).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{OJ} & =\\overset{\\to }{OH}+\\overset{\\to }{HJ} \\\\  & =h+\\frac{3}{10}(i-h) \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{10}h+\\frac{3}{10}i.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "From the diagram, we see that\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{OJ}=\\overset{\\to }{OH}+\\overset{\\to }{HJ}.\n$$\n\nWe're told that $\\overset{\\to }{OH}=\\mathbf{h},$ so we just need to work out $\\overset{\\to }{HJ}.$\n\nFirst, from $△OHI,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{HI} & =\\overset{\\to }{HO}+\\overset{\\to }{OI} \\\\  & =-\\overset{\\to }{OH}+\\overset{\\to }{OI} \\\\  & =-\\mathbf{h}+\\mathbf{i} \\\\  & =\\mathbf{i}-\\mathbf{h}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $HJ:JI=3:7,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{HJ}=\\frac{3}{10}\\overset{\\to }{HI}=\\frac{3}{10}(\\mathbf{i}-\\mathbf{h}).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{OJ} & =\\overset{\\to }{OH}+\\overset{\\to }{HJ} \\\\  & =\\mathbf{h}+\\frac{3}{10}(\\mathbf{i}-\\mathbf{h}) \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{10}\\mathbf{h}+\\frac{3}{10}\\mathbf{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186256383 :question/problem "![](images/70/707beca1d856414e25874cc0b56c6654fbb41d699f01106b6749d49408b39aee.png)\n\nConsider the diagram above, where $\\overset{\\to }{OU}=u,$ $\\overset{\\to }{OV}=v$ and $W$ divides $\\overset{―}{UV}$ in the ratio $UW:WV=7:2.$ Find $\\overset{\\to }{OW}.$" "![](images/70/707beca1d856414e25874cc0b56c6654fbb41d699f01106b6749d49408b39aee.png)\n\nConsider the diagram above, where $\\overset{\\to }{OU}=\\mathbf{u},$ $\\overset{\\to }{OV}=\\mathbf{v}$ and $W$ divides $\\overline{UV}$ in the ratio $UW:WV=7:2.$ Find $\\overset{\\to }{OW}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186256383 :question/worked-solution "From the diagram, we see that\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{OW}=\\overset{\\to }{OU}+\\overset{\\to }{UW}.\n$$\n\nWe're told that $\\overset{\\to }{OU}=u,$ so we just need to work out $\\overset{\\to }{UW}.$\n\nFirst, from $△OUV,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{UV} & =\\overset{\\to }{UO}+\\overset{\\to }{OV} \\\\  & =-\\overset{\\to }{OU}+\\overset{\\to }{OV} \\\\  & =-u+v \\\\  & =v-u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $UW:WV=7:2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{UW}=\\frac{7}{9}\\overset{\\to }{UV}=\\frac{7}{9}(v-u).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{OW} & =\\overset{\\to }{OU}+\\overset{\\to }{UW} \\\\  & =u+\\frac{7}{9}(v-u) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{9}u+\\frac{7}{9}v.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "From the diagram, we see that\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{OW}=\\overset{\\to }{OU}+\\overset{\\to }{UW}.\n$$\n\nWe're told that $\\overset{\\to }{OU}=\\mathbf{u},$ so we just need to work out $\\overset{\\to }{UW}.$\n\nFirst, from $△OUV,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{UV} & =\\overset{\\to }{UO}+\\overset{\\to }{OV} \\\\  & =-\\overset{\\to }{OU}+\\overset{\\to }{OV} \\\\  & =-\\mathbf{u}+\\mathbf{v} \\\\  & =\\mathbf{v}-\\mathbf{u}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $UW:WV=7:2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{UW}=\\frac{7}{9}\\overset{\\to }{UV}=\\frac{7}{9}(\\mathbf{v}-\\mathbf{u}).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{OW} & =\\overset{\\to }{OU}+\\overset{\\to }{UW} \\\\  & =\\mathbf{u}+\\frac{7}{9}(\\mathbf{v}-\\mathbf{u}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{9}\\mathbf{u}+\\frac{7}{9}\\mathbf{v}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186256386 :question/problem "![](images/50/50b87e86bd2985e54ce0a8fe6b3e23986f7e1946a5284d0ae297d96b9cbc60f6.png)\n\nConsider the diagram above, where $\\overset{\\to }{OM}=m,$ $\\overset{\\to }{ON}=n$ and $P$ divides $\\overset{―}{MN}$ in the ratio $MP:PN=2:1.$ Find $\\overset{\\to }{OP}.$" "![](images/50/50b87e86bd2985e54ce0a8fe6b3e23986f7e1946a5284d0ae297d96b9cbc60f6.png)\n\nConsider the diagram above, where $\\overset{\\to }{OM}=\\mathbf{m},$ $\\overset{\\to }{ON}=\\mathbf{n}$ and $P$ divides $\\overline{MN}$ in the ratio $MP:PN=2:1.$ Find $\\overset{\\to }{OP}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186256386 :question/worked-solution "From the diagram, we see that\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{OP}=\\overset{\\to }{OM}+\\overset{\\to }{MP}.\n$$\n\nWe're told that $\\overset{\\to }{OM}=m,$ so we just need to work out $\\overset{\\to }{MP}.$\n\nFirst, from $△OMN,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{MN} & =\\overset{\\to }{MO}+\\overset{\\to }{ON} \\\\  & =-\\overset{\\to }{OM}+\\overset{\\to }{ON} \\\\  & =-m+n \\\\  & =n-m.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $MP:PN=2:1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{MP}=\\frac{2}{3}\\overset{\\to }{MN}=\\frac{2}{3}(n-m).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{OP} & =\\overset{\\to }{OM}+\\overset{\\to }{MP} \\\\  & =m+\\frac{2}{3}(n-m) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}m+\\frac{2}{3}n.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "From the diagram, we see that\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{OP}=\\overset{\\to }{OM}+\\overset{\\to }{MP}.\n$$\n\nWe're told that $\\overset{\\to }{OM}=\\mathbf{m},$ so we just need to work out $\\overset{\\to }{MP}.$\n\nFirst, from $△OMN,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{MN} & =\\overset{\\to }{MO}+\\overset{\\to }{ON} \\\\  & =-\\overset{\\to }{OM}+\\overset{\\to }{ON} \\\\  & =-\\mathbf{m}+\\mathbf{n} \\\\  & =\\mathbf{n}-\\mathbf{m}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $MP:PN=2:1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{MP}=\\frac{2}{3}\\overset{\\to }{MN}=\\frac{2}{3}(\\mathbf{n}-\\mathbf{m}).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{OP} & =\\overset{\\to }{OM}+\\overset{\\to }{MP} \\\\  & =\\mathbf{m}+\\frac{2}{3}(\\mathbf{n}-\\mathbf{m}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\mathbf{m}+\\frac{2}{3}\\mathbf{n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186256582 :question/problem "![](images/42/4205d878409776be6c18735910a2f8ece019418452df83cb75d197531f057532.png)\n\nConsider the diagram above, where $\\overset{\\to }{OA}=a,$ $\\overset{\\to }{OB}=b$ and $T$ divides $\\overset{―}{AB}$ in the ratio $AT:TB=1:6.$ Find $\\overset{\\to }{OT}.$" "![](images/42/4205d878409776be6c18735910a2f8ece019418452df83cb75d197531f057532.png)\n\nConsider the diagram above, where $\\overset{\\to }{OA}=\\mathbf{a},$ $\\overset{\\to }{OB}=\\mathbf{b}$ and $T$ divides $\\overline{AB}$ in the ratio $AT:TB=1:6.$ Find $\\overset{\\to }{OT}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186256582 :question/worked-solution "From the diagram, we see that\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{OT}=\\overset{\\to }{OA}+\\overset{\\to }{AT}.\n$$\n\nWe're told that $\\overset{\\to }{OA}=a,$ so we just need to work out $\\overset{\\to }{AT}.$\n\nFirst, from $△OAB,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =\\overset{\\to }{AO}+\\overset{\\to }{OB} \\\\  & =-\\overset{\\to }{OA}+\\overset{\\to }{OB} \\\\  & =-a+b \\\\  & =b-a.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $AT:TB=1:6,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{AT}=\\frac{1}{7}\\overset{\\to }{AB}=\\frac{1}{7}(b-a).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{OT} & =\\overset{\\to }{OA}+\\overset{\\to }{AT} \\\\  & =a+\\frac{1}{7}(b-a) \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{7}a+\\frac{1}{7}b.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "From the diagram, we see that\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{OT}=\\overset{\\to }{OA}+\\overset{\\to }{AT}.\n$$\n\nWe're told that $\\overset{\\to }{OA}=\\mathbf{a},$ so we just need to work out $\\overset{\\to }{AT}.$\n\nFirst, from $△OAB,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =\\overset{\\to }{AO}+\\overset{\\to }{OB} \\\\  & =-\\overset{\\to }{OA}+\\overset{\\to }{OB} \\\\  & =-\\mathbf{a}+\\mathbf{b} \\\\  & =\\mathbf{b}-\\mathbf{a}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $AT:TB=1:6,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{AT}=\\frac{1}{7}\\overset{\\to }{AB}=\\frac{1}{7}(\\mathbf{b}-\\mathbf{a}).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{OT} & =\\overset{\\to }{OA}+\\overset{\\to }{AT} \\\\  & =\\mathbf{a}+\\frac{1}{7}(\\mathbf{b}-\\mathbf{a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{7}\\mathbf{a}+\\frac{1}{7}\\mathbf{b}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186256611 :question/problem "![](images/2f/2f0d9df3316615646dfbd994a378293dacf937050514080b5fe66af9794ae9e3.png)\n\nConsider the diagram above, where $\\overset{\\to }{OX}=a,$ $\\overset{\\to }{OY}=b$ and $Z$ divides $\\overset{―}{XY}$ in the ratio $XZ:ZY=5:2.$ Find $\\overset{\\to }{OZ}.$" "![](images/2f/2f0d9df3316615646dfbd994a378293dacf937050514080b5fe66af9794ae9e3.png)\n\nConsider the diagram above, where $\\overset{\\to }{OX}=\\mathbf{a},$ $\\overset{\\to }{OY}=\\mathbf{b}$ and $Z$ divides $\\overline{XY}$ in the ratio $XZ:ZY=5:2.$ Find $\\overset{\\to }{OZ}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186256611 :question/worked-solution "From the diagram, we see that\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{OZ}=\\overset{\\to }{OX}+\\overset{\\to }{XZ}.\n$$\n\nWe're told that $\\overset{\\to }{OX}=a,$ so we just need to work out $\\overset{\\to }{XZ}.$\n\nFirst, from $△OXY,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{XY} & =\\overset{\\to }{XO}+\\overset{\\to }{OY} \\\\  & =-\\overset{\\to }{OX}+\\overset{\\to }{OY} \\\\  & =-a+b \\\\  & =b-a.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $XZ:ZY=5:2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{XZ}=\\frac{5}{7}\\overset{\\to }{XY}=\\frac{5}{7}(b-a).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{OZ} & =\\overset{\\to }{OX}+\\overset{\\to }{XZ} \\\\  & =a+\\frac{5}{7}(b-a) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{7}a+\\frac{5}{7}b.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "From the diagram, we see that\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{OZ}=\\overset{\\to }{OX}+\\overset{\\to }{XZ}.\n$$\n\nWe're told that $\\overset{\\to }{OX}=\\mathbf{a},$ so we just need to work out $\\overset{\\to }{XZ}.$\n\nFirst, from $△OXY,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{XY} & =\\overset{\\to }{XO}+\\overset{\\to }{OY} \\\\  & =-\\overset{\\to }{OX}+\\overset{\\to }{OY} \\\\  & =-\\mathbf{a}+\\mathbf{b} \\\\  & =\\mathbf{b}-\\mathbf{a}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $XZ:ZY=5:2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{XZ}=\\frac{5}{7}\\overset{\\to }{XY}=\\frac{5}{7}(\\mathbf{b}-\\mathbf{a}).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{OZ} & =\\overset{\\to }{OX}+\\overset{\\to }{XZ} \\\\  & =\\mathbf{a}+\\frac{5}{7}(\\mathbf{b}-\\mathbf{a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{7}\\mathbf{a}+\\frac{5}{7}\\mathbf{b}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186256624 :question/problem "![](images/45/45110269c8a1ca9ad80cabc2f6c44592dca781afde7347d9d0a7e3363a76563a.png)\n\nConsider the diagram above, where $\\overset{\\to }{OA}=a,$ $\\overset{\\to }{OB}=b$ and $C$ divides $\\overset{―}{AB}$ in the ratio $AC:CB=5:1.$ Find $\\overset{\\to }{OC}.$" "![](images/45/45110269c8a1ca9ad80cabc2f6c44592dca781afde7347d9d0a7e3363a76563a.png)\n\nConsider the diagram above, where $\\overset{\\to }{OA}=\\mathbf{a},$ $\\overset{\\to }{OB}=\\mathbf{b}$ and $C$ divides $\\overline{AB}$ in the ratio $AC:CB=5:1.$ Find $\\overset{\\to }{OC}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186256624 :question/worked-solution "From the diagram, we see that\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{OC}=\\overset{\\to }{OA}+\\overset{\\to }{AC}.\n$$\n\nWe're told that $\\overset{\\to }{OA}=a,$ so we just need to work out $\\overset{\\to }{AC}.$\n\nFirst, from $△OAB,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =\\overset{\\to }{AO}+\\overset{\\to }{OB} \\\\  & =-\\overset{\\to }{OA}+\\overset{\\to }{OB} \\\\  & =-a+b \\\\  & =b-a.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $AC:CB=5:1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{AC}=\\frac{5}{6}\\overset{\\to }{AB}=\\frac{5}{6}(b-a).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{OC} & =\\overset{\\to }{OA}+\\overset{\\to }{AC} \\\\  & =a+\\frac{5}{6}(b-a) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}a+\\frac{5}{6}b.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "From the diagram, we see that\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{OC}=\\overset{\\to }{OA}+\\overset{\\to }{AC}.\n$$\n\nWe're told that $\\overset{\\to }{OA}=\\mathbf{a},$ so we just need to work out $\\overset{\\to }{AC}.$\n\nFirst, from $△OAB,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =\\overset{\\to }{AO}+\\overset{\\to }{OB} \\\\  & =-\\overset{\\to }{OA}+\\overset{\\to }{OB} \\\\  & =-\\mathbf{a}+\\mathbf{b} \\\\  & =\\mathbf{b}-\\mathbf{a}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $AC:CB=5:1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{AC}=\\frac{5}{6}\\overset{\\to }{AB}=\\frac{5}{6}(\\mathbf{b}-\\mathbf{a}).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{OC} & =\\overset{\\to }{OA}+\\overset{\\to }{AC} \\\\  & =\\mathbf{a}+\\frac{5}{6}(\\mathbf{b}-\\mathbf{a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\mathbf{a}+\\frac{5}{6}\\mathbf{b}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186292806 :question/problem "![](images/1d/1d637a841739052b4e2d26f89bbcfd92eefefcebf6000a46a10a45b7e53ecf15.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. If $c=\\lambda a+3b,$ then$\\,\\lambda =$\n{{field-1}}." "![](images/1d/1d637a841739052b4e2d26f89bbcfd92eefefcebf6000a46a10a45b7e53ecf15.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. If $\\mathbf{c}=\\lambda \\mathbf{a}+3\\mathbf{b},$ then$\\,\\lambda =$\n{{field-1}}."] [:db/cas 17592186292806 :question/worked-solution "To find $\\lambda ,$ we want to match the expression\n\n$$\nc=\\lambda a+3b.\n$$\n\nWe are given that one of the components is $3b.$ So, we first draw the vector $3b$ from the origin. Then we complete the path to the point $C.$\n\n![](images/33/33a5661bc10830c7e52e2ba7f27779a28c6e5f9cf39630e60d0634f3b6f7ccdd.png)\n\nLet $P$ be the endpoint of the vector $3b.$ Then\n\n$$\nc=\\overset{\\to }{OP}+\\overset{\\to }{PC}.\n$$\n\nIn $△OPC,$ we have:\n\n- $PC=3|a|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{PC}$ and $a$ have the same direction\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{PC}=3a.$\n\nAdditionally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c & =\\overset{\\to }{OP}+\\overset{\\to }{PC} \\\\  & =3b+3a.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $c=\\lambda a+3b=3a+3b.$\n\nSince $a∦b,$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients of $a,$ we get:\n\n$$\n\\lambda =3.\n$$" "To find $\\lambda ,$ we want to match the expression\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{c}=\\lambda \\mathbf{a}+3\\mathbf{b}.\n$$\n\nWe are given that one of the components is $3\\mathbf{b}.$ So, we first draw the vector $3\\mathbf{b}$ from the origin. Then we complete the path to the point $C.$\n\n![](images/33/33a5661bc10830c7e52e2ba7f27779a28c6e5f9cf39630e60d0634f3b6f7ccdd.png)\n\nLet $P$ be the endpoint of the vector $3\\mathbf{b}.$ Then\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{c}=\\overset{\\to }{OP}+\\overset{\\to }{PC}.\n$$\n\nIn $△OPC,$ we have:\n\n- $PC=3|\\mathbf{a}|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{PC}$ and $\\mathbf{a}$ have the same direction\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{PC}=3\\mathbf{a}.$\n\nAdditionally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{c} & =\\overset{\\to }{OP}+\\overset{\\to }{PC} \\\\  & =3\\mathbf{b}+3\\mathbf{a}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $\\mathbf{c}=\\lambda \\mathbf{a}+3\\mathbf{b}=3\\mathbf{a}+3\\mathbf{b}.$\n\nSince $\\mathbf{a}∦\\mathbf{b},$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients of $\\mathbf{a},$ we get:\n\n$$\n\\lambda =3.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186292807 :question/problem "![](images/99/992be4e67316804381ffc38860d2eaf23fd4a93cfe22ec4918c30c41e34ade57.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. If $t=\\lambda r+\\mu s$, then$\\,\\lambda =$\n\n$-\\frac{1}{2}$\n\nand \n$\\mu =$\n\n$-\\frac{5}{2}$\n\n." "![](images/99/992be4e67316804381ffc38860d2eaf23fd4a93cfe22ec4918c30c41e34ade57.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. If $\\mathbf{t}=\\lambda \\mathbf{r}+\\mu \\mathbf{s}$, then$\\,\\lambda =$\n\n$-\\frac{1}{2}$\n\nand \n$\\mu =$\n\n$-\\frac{5}{2}$\n\n."] [:db/cas 17592186292807 :question/worked-solution "![](images/c0/c0f7f4df52373ca602aff41c97a5b8413f510353874540b76147c527a2f4b18c.png)\n\nIn $△ORT$ shown above, we have:\n\n- $OR=\\frac{1}{2}|r|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{OR}$ has the opposite direction to $r$\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{OR}=-\\frac{1}{2}r.$\n\nSimilarly:\n\n- $RT=\\frac{5}{2}|s|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{RT}$ has the opposite direction to $s$\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{RT}=-\\frac{5}{2}s.$\n\nAdditionally, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t & =\\overset{\\to }{OR}+\\overset{\\to }{RT} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}r-\\frac{5}{2}s.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $$\n\\displaystyle t=\\lambda r+\\mu s=-\\frac{1}{2}r-\\frac{5}{2}s.\n$$ \n\nSince $s∦r,$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients, we get:\n\n- $\\lambda =-\\frac{1}{2}$ and $\\mu =-\\frac{5}{2}$" "![](images/c0/c0f7f4df52373ca602aff41c97a5b8413f510353874540b76147c527a2f4b18c.png)\n\nIn $△ORT$ shown above, we have:\n\n- $OR=\\frac{1}{2}|\\mathbf{r}|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{OR}$ has the opposite direction to $\\mathbf{r}$\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{OR}=-\\frac{1}{2}\\mathbf{r}.$\n\nSimilarly:\n\n- $RT=\\frac{5}{2}|\\mathbf{s}|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{RT}$ has the opposite direction to $\\mathbf{s}$\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{RT}=-\\frac{5}{2}\\mathbf{s}.$\n\nAdditionally, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{t} & =\\overset{\\to }{OR}+\\overset{\\to }{RT} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\mathbf{r}-\\frac{5}{2}\\mathbf{s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $$\n\\displaystyle \\mathbf{t}=\\lambda \\mathbf{r}+\\mu \\mathbf{s}=-\\frac{1}{2}\\mathbf{r}-\\frac{5}{2}\\mathbf{s}.\n$$ \n\nSince $\\mathbf{s}∦\\mathbf{r},$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients, we get:\n\n- $\\lambda =-\\frac{1}{2}$ and $\\mu =-\\frac{5}{2}$"] [:db/cas 17592186293042 :answer/value "\\overset{\\to }{OP}=f+\\frac{8}{5}g" "\\overset{\\to }{OP}=\\mathbf{f}+\\frac{8}{5}\\mathbf{g}"] [:db/cas 17592186293149 :answer/value "\\overset{\\to }{OP}=\\frac{5}{8}f+\\frac{1}{8}g" "\\overset{\\to }{OP}=\\frac{5}{8}\\mathbf{f}+\\frac{1}{8}\\mathbf{g}"] [:db/cas 17592186293384 :answer/value "\\overset{\\to }{OP}=\\frac{5}{13}f+\\frac{8}{13}g" "\\overset{\\to }{OP}=\\frac{5}{13}\\mathbf{f}+\\frac{8}{13}\\mathbf{g}"] [:db/cas 17592186293407 :answer/value "\\overset{\\to }{OP}=\\frac{1}{8}f+\\frac{1}{5}g" "\\overset{\\to }{OP}=\\frac{1}{8}\\mathbf{f}+\\frac{1}{5}\\mathbf{g}"] [:db/cas 17592186293882 :answer/value "\\overset{\\to }{OP}=\\frac{8}{13}f+\\frac{5}{13}g" "\\overset{\\to }{OP}=\\frac{8}{13}\\mathbf{f}+\\frac{5}{13}\\mathbf{g}"] [:db/cas 17592186294966 :question/problem "![](images/c2/c26ec7e1fccc5ada4c43eef55faf9e4fa3e17ed896b1546f472edf744404da1a.png)\n\nConsider the diagram above, where $\\overset{\\to }{OD}=d,$ $\\overset{\\to }{OE}=e,$ and $F$ divides $\\overset{―}{DE}$ in the ratio $DF:FE=2:3.$ Express $\\overset{\\to }{OF}$ via the vectors $d$ and $e.$\n\n$\\,\\overset{\\to }{OF}=$\n{{field-1}}\n$d+$\n{{field-2}}\n$e$" "![](images/c2/c26ec7e1fccc5ada4c43eef55faf9e4fa3e17ed896b1546f472edf744404da1a.png)\n\nConsider the diagram above, where $\\overset{\\to }{OD}=\\mathbf{d},$ $\\overset{\\to }{OE}=\\mathbf{e},$ and $F$ divides $\\overline{DE}$ in the ratio $DF:FE=2:3.$ Express $\\overset{\\to }{OF}$ via the vectors $\\mathbf{d}$ and $\\mathbf{e}.$\n\n$\\,\\overset{\\to }{OF}=$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\mathbf{d}+$\n{{field-2}}\n$\\mathbf{e}$"] [:db/cas 17592186294966 :question/worked-solution "From the diagram, we see that\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{OF}=\\overset{\\to }{OD}+\\overset{\\to }{DF}.\n$$\n\nWe're told that $\\overset{\\to }{OD}=d,$ so we just need to work out $\\overset{\\to }{DF}.$\n\nFirst, from $△ODE,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{DE} & =\\overset{\\to }{DO}+\\overset{\\to }{OE} \\\\  & =-\\overset{\\to }{OD}+\\overset{\\to }{OE} \\\\  & =-d+e \\\\  & =e-d.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $DF:FE=2:3,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{DF}=\\frac{2}{5}\\overset{\\to }{DE}=\\frac{2}{5}(e-d).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{OF} & =\\overset{\\to }{OD}+\\overset{\\to }{DF} \\\\  & =d+\\frac{2}{5}(e-d) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{5}d+\\frac{2}{5}e.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "From the diagram, we see that\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{OF}=\\overset{\\to }{OD}+\\overset{\\to }{DF}.\n$$\n\nWe're told that $\\overset{\\to }{OD}=\\mathbf{d},$ so we just need to work out $\\overset{\\to }{DF}.$\n\nFirst, from $△ODE,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{DE} & =\\overset{\\to }{DO}+\\overset{\\to }{OE} \\\\  & =-\\overset{\\to }{OD}+\\overset{\\to }{OE} \\\\  & =-\\mathbf{d}+\\mathbf{e} \\\\  & =\\mathbf{e}-\\mathbf{d}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $DF:FE=2:3,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{DF}=\\frac{2}{5}\\overset{\\to }{DE}=\\frac{2}{5}(\\mathbf{e}-\\mathbf{d}).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{OF} & =\\overset{\\to }{OD}+\\overset{\\to }{DF} \\\\  & =\\mathbf{d}+\\frac{2}{5}(\\mathbf{e}-\\mathbf{d}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{5}\\mathbf{d}+\\frac{2}{5}\\mathbf{e}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186294974 :question/problem "![](images/36/36aeda6b6f5ce46552f55f458da60ef46a646d737c452d586389c03c47d1c9cb.png)\n\nConsider the diagram above, where $\\overset{\\to }{OF}=f,$ $\\overset{\\to }{OG}=g$ and $P$ divides $\\overset{―}{FG}$ in the ratio $FP:PG=8:5.$ Find $\\overset{\\to }{OP}.$" "![](images/36/36aeda6b6f5ce46552f55f458da60ef46a646d737c452d586389c03c47d1c9cb.png)\n\nConsider the diagram above, where $\\overset{\\to }{OF}=\\mathbf{f},$ $\\overset{\\to }{OG}=\\mathbf{g}$ and $P$ divides $\\overline{FG}$ in the ratio $FP:PG=8:5.$ Find $\\overset{\\to }{OP}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186294974 :question/worked-solution "From the diagram, we see that\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{OP}=\\overset{\\to }{OF}+\\overset{\\to }{FP}.\n$$\n\nWe're told that $\\overset{\\to }{OF}=f,$ so we just need to work out $\\overset{\\to }{FP}.$\n\nFirst, from $△OFG,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{FG} & =\\overset{\\to }{FO}+\\overset{\\to }{OG} \\\\  & =-\\overset{\\to }{OF}+\\overset{\\to }{OG} \\\\  & =-f+g \\\\  & =g-f.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $FP:PG=8:5,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{FP}=\\frac{8}{13}\\overset{\\to }{FG}=\\frac{8}{13}(g-f).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{OP} & =\\overset{\\to }{OF}+\\overset{\\to }{FP} \\\\  & =f+\\frac{8}{13}(g-f) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{13}f+\\frac{8}{13}g.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "From the diagram, we see that\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{OP}=\\overset{\\to }{OF}+\\overset{\\to }{FP}.\n$$\n\nWe're told that $\\overset{\\to }{OF}=\\mathbf{f},$ so we just need to work out $\\overset{\\to }{FP}.$\n\nFirst, from $△OFG,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{FG} & =\\overset{\\to }{FO}+\\overset{\\to }{OG} \\\\  & =-\\overset{\\to }{OF}+\\overset{\\to }{OG} \\\\  & =-\\mathbf{f}+\\mathbf{g} \\\\  & =\\mathbf{g}-\\mathbf{f}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $FP:PG=8:5,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{FP}=\\frac{8}{13}\\overset{\\to }{FG}=\\frac{8}{13}(\\mathbf{g}-\\mathbf{f}).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{OP} & =\\overset{\\to }{OF}+\\overset{\\to }{FP} \\\\  & =\\mathbf{f}+\\frac{8}{13}(\\mathbf{g}-\\mathbf{f}) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{13}\\mathbf{f}+\\frac{8}{13}\\mathbf{g}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186302041 :question/problem "![](images/6f/6fe3721ff3ab63e75be934c79b03d4ca601513c2e08b987a0bafd94c74856e90.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. If $c=a+\\lambda b$, then$\\,\\lambda =$\n{{field-1}}." "![](images/6f/6fe3721ff3ab63e75be934c79b03d4ca601513c2e08b987a0bafd94c74856e90.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. If $\\mathbf{c}=\\mathbf{a}+\\lambda \\mathbf{b}$, then$\\,\\lambda =$\n{{field-1}}."] [:db/cas 17592186302041 :question/worked-solution "To find $\\lambda ,$ we want to match the expression\n\n$$\nc=a+\\lambda b.\n$$\n\nWe are given that one of the components is $a.$ So, we begin with the vector $a,$ and then find the remaining vector needed to reach the point $C.$\n\n![](images/9c/9c8d67718152bc1bc9569213369580b6fc6f5bc1fe0c63b637f14c9f659dfe27.png)\n\nNote that in $△OAC,$ we have:\n\n- $AC=\\frac{2}{3}|b|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{AC}$ and $b$ have the same direction\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{AC}=\\frac{2}{3}b.$\n\nAdditionally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c & =\\overset{\\to }{OA}+\\overset{\\to }{AC} \\\\  & =a+\\frac{2}{3}b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $c=a+\\lambda b=a+\\frac{2}{3}b.$\n\nSince $a∦b,$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients of $b,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\lambda =\\frac{2}{3}.\n$$" "To find $\\lambda ,$ we want to match the expression\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{c}=\\mathbf{a}+\\lambda \\mathbf{b}.\n$$\n\nWe are given that one of the components is $\\mathbf{a}.$ So, we begin with the vector $\\mathbf{a},$ and then find the remaining vector needed to reach the point $C.$\n\n![](images/9c/9c8d67718152bc1bc9569213369580b6fc6f5bc1fe0c63b637f14c9f659dfe27.png)\n\nNote that in $△OAC,$ we have:\n\n- $AC=\\frac{2}{3}|\\mathbf{b}|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{AC}$ and $\\mathbf{b}$ have the same direction\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{AC}=\\frac{2}{3}\\mathbf{b}.$\n\nAdditionally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{c} & =\\overset{\\to }{OA}+\\overset{\\to }{AC} \\\\  & =\\mathbf{a}+\\frac{2}{3}\\mathbf{b}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $\\mathbf{c}=\\mathbf{a}+\\lambda \\mathbf{b}=\\mathbf{a}+\\frac{2}{3}\\mathbf{b}.$\n\nSince $\\mathbf{a}∦\\mathbf{b},$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients of $\\mathbf{b},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\lambda =\\frac{2}{3}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186302111 :question/problem "![](images/7e/7e92a09f8448f4d320f86656bc0774e6167b96156041c32eb7a64ba5e8820128.png)\n\nConsider the diagram above, where $\\overset{\\to }{OD}=d,$ $\\overset{\\to }{OE}=e,$ and $F$ divides $\\overset{―}{DE}$ in the ratio $DF:FE=3:4.$ Express $\\overset{\\to }{OF}$ via the vectors $d$ and $e.$\n\n$\\,\\overset{\\to }{OF}=$\n{{field-1}}\n$d+$\n{{field-2}}\n$e$" "![](images/7e/7e92a09f8448f4d320f86656bc0774e6167b96156041c32eb7a64ba5e8820128.png)\n\nConsider the diagram above, where $\\overset{\\to }{OD}=\\mathbf{d},$ $\\overset{\\to }{OE}=\\mathbf{e},$ and $F$ divides $\\overline{DE}$ in the ratio $DF:FE=3:4.$ Express $\\overset{\\to }{OF}$ via the vectors $\\mathbf{d}$ and $\\mathbf{e}.$\n\n$\\,\\overset{\\to }{OF}=$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\mathbf{d}+$\n{{field-2}}\n$\\mathbf{e}$"] [:db/cas 17592186302111 :question/worked-solution "From the diagram, we see that\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{OF}=\\overset{\\to }{OD}+\\overset{\\to }{DF}.\n$$\n\nWe're told that $\\overset{\\to }{OD}=d,$ so we just need to work out $\\overset{\\to }{DF}.$\n\nFirst, from $△ODE,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{DE} & =\\overset{\\to }{DO}+\\overset{\\to }{OE} \\\\  & =-\\overset{\\to }{OD}+\\overset{\\to }{OE} \\\\  & =-d+e \\\\  & =e-d.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $DF:FE=3:4,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{DF}=\\frac{3}{7}\\overset{\\to }{DE}=\\frac{3}{7}(e-d).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{OF} & =\\overset{\\to }{OD}+\\overset{\\to }{DF} \\\\  & =d+\\frac{3}{7}(e-d) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{7}d+\\frac{3}{7}e.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "From the diagram, we see that\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{OF}=\\overset{\\to }{OD}+\\overset{\\to }{DF}.\n$$\n\nWe're told that $\\overset{\\to }{OD}=\\mathbf{d},$ so we just need to work out $\\overset{\\to }{DF}.$\n\nFirst, from $△ODE,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{DE} & =\\overset{\\to }{DO}+\\overset{\\to }{OE} \\\\  & =-\\overset{\\to }{OD}+\\overset{\\to }{OE} \\\\  & =-\\mathbf{d}+\\mathbf{e} \\\\  & =\\mathbf{e}-\\mathbf{d}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $DF:FE=3:4,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{DF}=\\frac{3}{7}\\overset{\\to }{DE}=\\frac{3}{7}(\\mathbf{e}-\\mathbf{d}).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{OF} & =\\overset{\\to }{OD}+\\overset{\\to }{DF} \\\\  & =\\mathbf{d}+\\frac{3}{7}(\\mathbf{e}-\\mathbf{d}) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{7}\\mathbf{d}+\\frac{3}{7}\\mathbf{e}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186302112 :question/problem "![](images/2a/2a9cacfc6787e60229076c4abd2854709d51ac718c8d519256a61dbe92852b05.png)\n\nConsider the diagram above, where $\\overset{\\to }{OG}=g,$ $\\overset{\\to }{OH}=h,$ and $J$ divides $\\overset{―}{GH}$ in the ratio $GJ:JH=6:7.$ Express $\\overset{\\to }{OJ}$ via the vectors $g$ and $h.$\n\n$\\,\\overset{\\to }{OJ}=$\n{{field-1}}\n$g+$\n{{field-2}}\n$h$" "![](images/2a/2a9cacfc6787e60229076c4abd2854709d51ac718c8d519256a61dbe92852b05.png)\n\nConsider the diagram above, where $\\overset{\\to }{OG}=\\mathbf{g},$ $\\overset{\\to }{OH}=\\mathbf{h},$ and $J$ divides $\\overline{GH}$ in the ratio $GJ:JH=6:7.$ Express $\\overset{\\to }{OJ}$ via the vectors $\\mathbf{g}$ and $\\mathbf{h}.$\n\n$\\,\\overset{\\to }{OJ}=$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\mathbf{g}+$\n{{field-2}}\n$\\mathbf{h}$"] [:db/cas 17592186302112 :question/worked-solution "From the diagram, we see that\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{OJ}=\\overset{\\to }{OG}+\\overset{\\to }{GJ}.\n$$\n\nWe're told that $\\overset{\\to }{OG}=g,$ so we just need to work out $\\overset{\\to }{GJ}.$\n\nFirst, from $△OGH,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{GH} & =\\overset{\\to }{GO}+\\overset{\\to }{OH} \\\\  & =-\\overset{\\to }{OG}+\\overset{\\to }{OH} \\\\  & =-g+h \\\\  & =h-g.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $GJ:JH=6:7,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{GJ}=\\frac{6}{13}\\overset{\\to }{GH}=\\frac{6}{13}(h-g).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{OJ} & =\\overset{\\to }{OG}+\\overset{\\to }{GJ} \\\\  & =g+\\frac{6}{13}(h-g) \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{13}g+\\frac{6}{13}h.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "From the diagram, we see that\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{OJ}=\\overset{\\to }{OG}+\\overset{\\to }{GJ}.\n$$\n\nWe're told that $\\overset{\\to }{OG}=\\mathbf{g},$ so we just need to work out $\\overset{\\to }{GJ}.$\n\nFirst, from $△OGH,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{GH} & =\\overset{\\to }{GO}+\\overset{\\to }{OH} \\\\  & =-\\overset{\\to }{OG}+\\overset{\\to }{OH} \\\\  & =-\\mathbf{g}+\\mathbf{h} \\\\  & =\\mathbf{h}-\\mathbf{g}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $GJ:JH=6:7,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{GJ}=\\frac{6}{13}\\overset{\\to }{GH}=\\frac{6}{13}(\\mathbf{h}-\\mathbf{g}).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{OJ} & =\\overset{\\to }{OG}+\\overset{\\to }{GJ} \\\\  & =\\mathbf{g}+\\frac{6}{13}(\\mathbf{h}-\\mathbf{g}) \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{13}\\mathbf{g}+\\frac{6}{13}\\mathbf{h}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186302113 :question/problem "![](images/d9/d9448f9f05666f9806975b37a73f09c492eb73139b6f9395b619f077f3a584c9.png)\n\nConsider the diagram above, where $\\overset{\\to }{OP}=p,$ $\\overset{\\to }{OQ}=q,$ and $R$ divides $\\overset{―}{PQ}$ in the ratio $PR:RQ=4:9.$ Express $\\overset{\\to }{OR}$ via the vectors $p$ and $q.$\n\n$\\,\\overset{\\to }{OR}=$\n{{field-1}}\n$p+$\n{{field-2}}\n$q$" "![](images/d9/d9448f9f05666f9806975b37a73f09c492eb73139b6f9395b619f077f3a584c9.png)\n\nConsider the diagram above, where $\\overset{\\to }{OP}=\\mathbf{p},$ $\\overset{\\to }{OQ}=\\mathbf{q},$ and $R$ divides $\\overline{PQ}$ in the ratio $PR:RQ=4:9.$ Express $\\overset{\\to }{OR}$ via the vectors $\\mathbf{p}$ and $\\mathbf{q}.$\n\n$\\,\\overset{\\to }{OR}=$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\mathbf{p}+$\n{{field-2}}\n$\\mathbf{q}$"] [:db/cas 17592186302113 :question/worked-solution "From the diagram, we see that\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{OR}=\\overset{\\to }{OP}+\\overset{\\to }{PR}.\n$$\n\nWe're told that $\\overset{\\to }{OP}=p,$ so we just need to work out $\\overset{\\to }{PR}.$\n\nFirst, from $△OPQ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{PQ} & =\\overset{\\to }{PO}+\\overset{\\to }{OQ} \\\\  & =-\\overset{\\to }{OP}+\\overset{\\to }{OQ} \\\\  & =-p+q \\\\  & =q-p.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $PR:RQ=4:9,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{PR}=\\frac{4}{13}\\overset{\\to }{PQ}=\\frac{4}{13}(q-p).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{OR} & =\\overset{\\to }{OP}+\\overset{\\to }{PR} \\\\  & =p+\\frac{4}{13}(q-p) \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{13}p+\\frac{4}{13}q.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "From the diagram, we see that\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{OR}=\\overset{\\to }{OP}+\\overset{\\to }{PR}.\n$$\n\nWe're told that $\\overset{\\to }{OP}=\\mathbf{p},$ so we just need to work out $\\overset{\\to }{PR}.$\n\nFirst, from $△OPQ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{PQ} & =\\overset{\\to }{PO}+\\overset{\\to }{OQ} \\\\  & =-\\overset{\\to }{OP}+\\overset{\\to }{OQ} \\\\  & =-\\mathbf{p}+\\mathbf{q} \\\\  & =\\mathbf{q}-\\mathbf{p}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $PR:RQ=4:9,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{PR}=\\frac{4}{13}\\overset{\\to }{PQ}=\\frac{4}{13}(\\mathbf{q}-\\mathbf{p}).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{OR} & =\\overset{\\to }{OP}+\\overset{\\to }{PR} \\\\  & =\\mathbf{p}+\\frac{4}{13}(\\mathbf{q}-\\mathbf{p}) \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{13}\\mathbf{p}+\\frac{4}{13}\\mathbf{q}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186302114 :question/problem "![](images/36/36742531a5f9f5d77c5f7e90980120479f4142cecaf6e84937d76d938688409d.png)\n\nConsider the diagram above, where $\\overset{\\to }{OM}=d,$ $\\overset{\\to }{ON}=e,$ and $T$ divides $\\overset{―}{MN}$ in the ratio $MT:TN=8:3.$ Express $\\overset{\\to }{OT}$ via the vectors $d$ and $e.$\n\n$\\,\\overset{\\to }{OT}=$\n{{field-1}}\n$d+$\n{{field-2}}\n$e$" "![](images/36/36742531a5f9f5d77c5f7e90980120479f4142cecaf6e84937d76d938688409d.png)\n\nConsider the diagram above, where $\\overset{\\to }{OM}=\\mathbf{d},$ $\\overset{\\to }{ON}=\\mathbf{e},$ and $T$ divides $\\overline{MN}$ in the ratio $MT:TN=8:3.$ Express $\\overset{\\to }{OT}$ via the vectors $\\mathbf{d}$ and $\\mathbf{e}.$\n\n$\\,\\overset{\\to }{OT}=$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\mathbf{d}+$\n{{field-2}}\n$\\mathbf{e}$"] [:db/cas 17592186302114 :question/worked-solution "From the diagram, we see that\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{OT}=\\overset{\\to }{OM}+\\overset{\\to }{MT}.\n$$\n\nWe're told that $\\overset{\\to }{OM}=d,$ so we just need to work out $\\overset{\\to }{MT}.$\n\nFirst, from $△OMN,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{MN} & =\\overset{\\to }{MO}+\\overset{\\to }{ON} \\\\  & =-\\overset{\\to }{OM}+\\overset{\\to }{ON} \\\\  & =-d+e \\\\  & =e-d.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $MT:TN=8:3,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{MT}=\\frac{8}{11}\\overset{\\to }{MN}=\\frac{8}{11}(e-d).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{OT} & =\\overset{\\to }{OM}+\\overset{\\to }{MT} \\\\  & =d+\\frac{8}{11}(e-d) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{11}d+\\frac{8}{11}e.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "From the diagram, we see that\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{OT}=\\overset{\\to }{OM}+\\overset{\\to }{MT}.\n$$\n\nWe're told that $\\overset{\\to }{OM}=\\mathbf{d},$ so we just need to work out $\\overset{\\to }{MT}.$\n\nFirst, from $△OMN,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{MN} & =\\overset{\\to }{MO}+\\overset{\\to }{ON} \\\\  & =-\\overset{\\to }{OM}+\\overset{\\to }{ON} \\\\  & =-\\mathbf{d}+\\mathbf{e} \\\\  & =\\mathbf{e}-\\mathbf{d}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $MT:TN=8:3,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{MT}=\\frac{8}{11}\\overset{\\to }{MN}=\\frac{8}{11}(\\mathbf{e}-\\mathbf{d}).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{OT} & =\\overset{\\to }{OM}+\\overset{\\to }{MT} \\\\  & =\\mathbf{d}+\\frac{8}{11}(\\mathbf{e}-\\mathbf{d}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{11}\\mathbf{d}+\\frac{8}{11}\\mathbf{e}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186302115 :question/problem "![](images/de/de33644bcad663e67dc19c687dc0ef959310a50ddb83203031e66b951700510b.png)\n\nConsider the diagram above, where $\\overset{\\to }{OU}=u,$ $\\overset{\\to }{OV}=v,$ and $W$ divides $\\overset{―}{UV}$ in the ratio $UW:WV=9:8.$ Express $\\overset{\\to }{OW}$ via the vectors $u$ and $v.$\n\n$\\,\\overset{\\to }{OW}=$\n{{field-1}}\n$u+$\n{{field-2}}\n$v$" "![](images/de/de33644bcad663e67dc19c687dc0ef959310a50ddb83203031e66b951700510b.png)\n\nConsider the diagram above, where $\\overset{\\to }{OU}=\\mathbf{u},$ $\\overset{\\to }{OV}=\\mathbf{v},$ and $W$ divides $\\overline{UV}$ in the ratio $UW:WV=9:8.$ Express $\\overset{\\to }{OW}$ via the vectors $\\mathbf{u}$ and $\\mathbf{v}.$\n\n$\\,\\overset{\\to }{OW}=$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\mathbf{u}+$\n{{field-2}}\n$\\mathbf{v}$"] [:db/cas 17592186302115 :question/worked-solution "From the diagram, we see that\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{OW}=\\overset{\\to }{OU}+\\overset{\\to }{UW}.\n$$\n\nWe're told that $\\overset{\\to }{OU}=u,$ so we just need to work out $\\overset{\\to }{UW}.$\n\nFirst, from $△OUV,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{UV} & =\\overset{\\to }{UO}+\\overset{\\to }{OV} \\\\  & =-\\overset{\\to }{OU}+\\overset{\\to }{OV} \\\\  & =-u+v \\\\  & =v-u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $UW:WV=9:8,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{UW}=\\frac{9}{17}\\overset{\\to }{UV}=\\frac{9}{17}(v-u).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{OW} & =\\overset{\\to }{OU}+\\overset{\\to }{UW} \\\\  & =u+\\frac{9}{17}(v-u) \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{17}u+\\frac{9}{17}v.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "From the diagram, we see that\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{OW}=\\overset{\\to }{OU}+\\overset{\\to }{UW}.\n$$\n\nWe're told that $\\overset{\\to }{OU}=\\mathbf{u},$ so we just need to work out $\\overset{\\to }{UW}.$\n\nFirst, from $△OUV,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{UV} & =\\overset{\\to }{UO}+\\overset{\\to }{OV} \\\\  & =-\\overset{\\to }{OU}+\\overset{\\to }{OV} \\\\  & =-\\mathbf{u}+\\mathbf{v} \\\\  & =\\mathbf{v}-\\mathbf{u}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $UW:WV=9:8,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{UW}=\\frac{9}{17}\\overset{\\to }{UV}=\\frac{9}{17}(\\mathbf{v}-\\mathbf{u}).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{OW} & =\\overset{\\to }{OU}+\\overset{\\to }{UW} \\\\  & =\\mathbf{u}+\\frac{9}{17}(\\mathbf{v}-\\mathbf{u}) \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{17}\\mathbf{u}+\\frac{9}{17}\\mathbf{v}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186302116 :question/problem "![](images/84/84ba52159416b3b420daa1ebd82dccac2411aee420201b9b9604937995d9553c.png)\n\nConsider the diagram above, where $\\overset{\\to }{OS}=s,$ $\\overset{\\to }{OT}=t,$ and $L$ divides $\\overset{―}{ST}$ in the ratio $SL:LT=11:4.$ Express $\\overset{\\to }{OL}$ via the vectors $s$ and $t.$\n\n$\\,\\overset{\\to }{OL}=$\n{{field-1}}\n$s+$\n{{field-2}}\n$t$" "![](images/84/84ba52159416b3b420daa1ebd82dccac2411aee420201b9b9604937995d9553c.png)\n\nConsider the diagram above, where $\\overset{\\to }{OS}=\\mathbf{s},$ $\\overset{\\to }{OT}=\\mathbf{t},$ and $L$ divides $\\overline{ST}$ in the ratio $SL:LT=11:4.$ Express $\\overset{\\to }{OL}$ via the vectors $\\mathbf{s}$ and $\\mathbf{t}.$\n\n$\\,\\overset{\\to }{OL}=$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\mathbf{s}+$\n{{field-2}}\n$\\mathbf{t}$"] [:db/cas 17592186302116 :question/worked-solution "From the diagram, we see that\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{OL}=\\overset{\\to }{OS}+\\overset{\\to }{SL}.\n$$\n\nWe're told that $\\overset{\\to }{OS}=s,$ so we just need to work out $\\overset{\\to }{SL}.$\n\nFirst, from $△OST,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{ST} & =\\overset{\\to }{SO}+\\overset{\\to }{OT} \\\\  & =-\\overset{\\to }{OS}+\\overset{\\to }{OT} \\\\  & =-s+t \\\\  & =t-s.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $SL:LT=11:4,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{SL}=\\frac{11}{15}\\overset{\\to }{ST}=\\frac{11}{15}(t-s).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{OL} & =\\overset{\\to }{OS}+\\overset{\\to }{SL} \\\\  & =s+\\frac{11}{15}(t-s) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{15}s+\\frac{11}{15}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "From the diagram, we see that\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{OL}=\\overset{\\to }{OS}+\\overset{\\to }{SL}.\n$$\n\nWe're told that $\\overset{\\to }{OS}=\\mathbf{s},$ so we just need to work out $\\overset{\\to }{SL}.$\n\nFirst, from $△OST,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{ST} & =\\overset{\\to }{SO}+\\overset{\\to }{OT} \\\\  & =-\\overset{\\to }{OS}+\\overset{\\to }{OT} \\\\  & =-\\mathbf{s}+\\mathbf{t} \\\\  & =\\mathbf{t}-\\mathbf{s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $SL:LT=11:4,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{SL}=\\frac{11}{15}\\overset{\\to }{ST}=\\frac{11}{15}(\\mathbf{t}-\\mathbf{s}).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{OL} & =\\overset{\\to }{OS}+\\overset{\\to }{SL} \\\\  & =\\mathbf{s}+\\frac{11}{15}(\\mathbf{t}-\\mathbf{s}) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{15}\\mathbf{s}+\\frac{11}{15}\\mathbf{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186302117 :question/problem "![](images/76/76ddb750ca447fd7f73f2481ecd46bb53eba12e392e81e8b64c2c23589e11694.png)\n\nConsider the diagram above, where $\\overset{\\to }{OK}=k,$ $\\overset{\\to }{OZ}=z,$ and $Y$ divides $\\overset{―}{KZ}$ in the ratio $KY:YZ=10:7.$ Express $\\overset{\\to }{OY}$ via the vectors $k$ and $z.$\n\n$\\,\\overset{\\to }{OY}=$\n{{field-1}}\n$k+$\n{{field-2}}\n$z$" "![](images/76/76ddb750ca447fd7f73f2481ecd46bb53eba12e392e81e8b64c2c23589e11694.png)\n\nConsider the diagram above, where $\\overset{\\to }{OK}=\\mathbf{k},$ $\\overset{\\to }{OZ}=\\mathbf{z},$ and $Y$ divides $\\overline{KZ}$ in the ratio $KY:YZ=10:7.$ Express $\\overset{\\to }{OY}$ via the vectors $\\mathbf{k}$ and $\\mathbf{z}.$\n\n$\\,\\overset{\\to }{OY}=$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\mathbf{k}+$\n{{field-2}}\n$\\mathbf{z}$"] [:db/cas 17592186302117 :question/worked-solution "From the diagram, we see that\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{OY}=\\overset{\\to }{OK}+\\overset{\\to }{KY}.\n$$\n\nWe're told that $\\overset{\\to }{OK}=k,$ so we just need to work out $\\overset{\\to }{KY}.$\n\nFirst, from $△OKZ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{KZ} & =\\overset{\\to }{KO}+\\overset{\\to }{OZ} \\\\  & =-\\overset{\\to }{OK}+\\overset{\\to }{OZ} \\\\  & =-k+z \\\\  & =z-k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $KY:YZ=10:7,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{KY}=\\frac{10}{17}\\overset{\\to }{KZ}=\\frac{10}{17}(z-k).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{OY} & =\\overset{\\to }{OK}+\\overset{\\to }{KY} \\\\  & =k+\\frac{10}{17}(z-k) \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{17}k+\\frac{10}{17}z.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "From the diagram, we see that\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{OY}=\\overset{\\to }{OK}+\\overset{\\to }{KY}.\n$$\n\nWe're told that $\\overset{\\to }{OK}=\\mathbf{k},$ so we just need to work out $\\overset{\\to }{KY}.$\n\nFirst, from $△OKZ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{KZ} & =\\overset{\\to }{KO}+\\overset{\\to }{OZ} \\\\  & =-\\overset{\\to }{OK}+\\overset{\\to }{OZ} \\\\  & =-\\mathbf{k}+\\mathbf{z} \\\\  & =\\mathbf{z}-\\mathbf{k}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $KY:YZ=10:7,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{KY}=\\frac{10}{17}\\overset{\\to }{KZ}=\\frac{10}{17}(\\mathbf{z}-\\mathbf{k}).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{OY} & =\\overset{\\to }{OK}+\\overset{\\to }{KY} \\\\  & =\\mathbf{k}+\\frac{10}{17}(\\mathbf{z}-\\mathbf{k}) \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{17}\\mathbf{k}+\\frac{10}{17}\\mathbf{z}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186302118 :question/problem "![](images/d0/d0e71a97dc559536c25b63fda50805875d4651166a9442ae0d3d081704edebb2.png)\n\nConsider the diagram above, where $\\overset{\\to }{OA}=a,$ $\\overset{\\to }{OQ}=q,$ and $N$ divides $\\overset{―}{AQ}$ in the ratio $AN:NQ=12:7.$ Express $\\overset{\\to }{ON}$ via the vectors $a$ and $q.$\n\n$\\,\\overset{\\to }{ON}=$\n{{field-1}}\n$a+$\n{{field-2}}\n$q$" "![](images/d0/d0e71a97dc559536c25b63fda50805875d4651166a9442ae0d3d081704edebb2.png)\n\nConsider the diagram above, where $\\overset{\\to }{OA}=\\mathbf{a},$ $\\overset{\\to }{OQ}=\\mathbf{q},$ and $N$ divides $\\overline{AQ}$ in the ratio $AN:NQ=12:7.$ Express $\\overset{\\to }{ON}$ via the vectors $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{q}.$\n\n$\\,\\overset{\\to }{ON}=$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\mathbf{a}+$\n{{field-2}}\n$\\mathbf{q}$"] [:db/cas 17592186302118 :question/worked-solution "From the diagram, we see that\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{ON}=\\overset{\\to }{OA}+\\overset{\\to }{AN}.\n$$\n\nWe're told that $\\overset{\\to }{OA}=a,$ so we just need to work out $\\overset{\\to }{AN}.$\n\nFirst, from $△OAQ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AQ} & =\\overset{\\to }{AO}+\\overset{\\to }{OQ} \\\\  & =-\\overset{\\to }{OA}+\\overset{\\to }{OQ} \\\\  & =-a+q \\\\  & =q-a.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $AN:NQ=12:7,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{AN}=\\frac{12}{19}\\overset{\\to }{AQ}=\\frac{12}{19}(q-a).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{ON} & =\\overset{\\to }{OA}+\\overset{\\to }{AN} \\\\  & =a+\\frac{12}{19}(q-a) \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{19}a+\\frac{12}{19}q.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "From the diagram, we see that\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{ON}=\\overset{\\to }{OA}+\\overset{\\to }{AN}.\n$$\n\nWe're told that $\\overset{\\to }{OA}=\\mathbf{a},$ so we just need to work out $\\overset{\\to }{AN}.$\n\nFirst, from $△OAQ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AQ} & =\\overset{\\to }{AO}+\\overset{\\to }{OQ} \\\\  & =-\\overset{\\to }{OA}+\\overset{\\to }{OQ} \\\\  & =-\\mathbf{a}+\\mathbf{q} \\\\  & =\\mathbf{q}-\\mathbf{a}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $AN:NQ=12:7,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{AN}=\\frac{12}{19}\\overset{\\to }{AQ}=\\frac{12}{19}(\\mathbf{q}-\\mathbf{a}).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{ON} & =\\overset{\\to }{OA}+\\overset{\\to }{AN} \\\\  & =\\mathbf{a}+\\frac{12}{19}(\\mathbf{q}-\\mathbf{a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{19}\\mathbf{a}+\\frac{12}{19}\\mathbf{q}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186302119 :question/problem "![](images/1d/1deea2da05b0d12c7e9afbb12f0340bdbfef18ff37dc49c506929028bfeb67c4.png)\n\nConsider the diagram above, where $\\overset{\\to }{OL}=l,$ $\\overset{\\to }{OP}=p,$ and $Q$ divides $\\overset{―}{LP}$ in the ratio $LQ:QP=7:9.$ Express $\\overset{\\to }{OQ}$ via the vectors $l$ and $p.$\n\n$\\,\\overset{\\to }{OQ}=$\n{{field-1}}\n$l+$\n{{field-2}}\n$p$" "![](images/1d/1deea2da05b0d12c7e9afbb12f0340bdbfef18ff37dc49c506929028bfeb67c4.png)\n\nConsider the diagram above, where $\\overset{\\to }{OL}=\\mathbf{l},$ $\\overset{\\to }{OP}=\\mathbf{p},$ and $Q$ divides $\\overline{LP}$ in the ratio $LQ:QP=7:9.$ Express $\\overset{\\to }{OQ}$ via the vectors $\\mathbf{l}$ and $\\mathbf{p}.$\n\n$\\,\\overset{\\to }{OQ}=$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\mathbf{l}+$\n{{field-2}}\n$\\mathbf{p}$"] [:db/cas 17592186302119 :question/worked-solution "From the diagram, we see that\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{OQ}=\\overset{\\to }{OL}+\\overset{\\to }{LQ}.\n$$\n\nWe're told that $\\overset{\\to }{OL}=l,$ so we just need to work out $\\overset{\\to }{LQ}.$\n\nFirst, from $△OLP,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{LP} & =\\overset{\\to }{LO}+\\overset{\\to }{OP} \\\\  & =-\\overset{\\to }{OL}+\\overset{\\to }{OP} \\\\  & =-l+p \\\\  & =p-l.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $LQ:QP=7:9,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{LQ}=\\frac{7}{16}\\overset{\\to }{LP}=\\frac{7}{16}(p-l).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{OQ} & =\\overset{\\to }{OL}+\\overset{\\to }{LQ} \\\\  & =l+\\frac{7}{16}(p-l) \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{16}l+\\frac{7}{16}p.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "From the diagram, we see that\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{OQ}=\\overset{\\to }{OL}+\\overset{\\to }{LQ}.\n$$\n\nWe're told that $\\overset{\\to }{OL}=\\mathbf{l},$ so we just need to work out $\\overset{\\to }{LQ}.$\n\nFirst, from $△OLP,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{LP} & =\\overset{\\to }{LO}+\\overset{\\to }{OP} \\\\  & =-\\overset{\\to }{OL}+\\overset{\\to }{OP} \\\\  & =-\\mathbf{l}+\\mathbf{p} \\\\  & =\\mathbf{p}-\\mathbf{l}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $LQ:QP=7:9,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{LQ}=\\frac{7}{16}\\overset{\\to }{LP}=\\frac{7}{16}(\\mathbf{p}-\\mathbf{l}).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{OQ} & =\\overset{\\to }{OL}+\\overset{\\to }{LQ} \\\\  & =\\mathbf{l}+\\frac{7}{16}(\\mathbf{p}-\\mathbf{l}) \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{16}\\mathbf{l}+\\frac{7}{16}\\mathbf{p}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186302254 :question/problem "![](images/58/586fb01cd796c9d4e04aaeb393ea7f5fadd6c836d0b39d36c9c668447c490bd1.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. If $t=\\lambda u+\\mu v$, then$\\,\\lambda =$\n{{field-1}}\nand \n$\\mu =$\n{{field-2}}." "![](images/58/586fb01cd796c9d4e04aaeb393ea7f5fadd6c836d0b39d36c9c668447c490bd1.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. If $\\mathbf{t}=\\lambda \\mathbf{u}+\\mu \\mathbf{v}$, then$\\,\\lambda =$\n{{field-1}}\nand \n$\\mu =$\n{{field-2}}."] [:db/cas 17592186302254 :question/worked-solution "![](images/2d/2d3a5c31af776cc49c2af118edc7256ca533320c7d53245593e5de6649ce03cf.png)\n\nIn $△OXT$ shown above, we have:\n\n- $OX=|u|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{OX}$ has the opposite direction to $u$\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{OX}=-u.$\n\nSimilarly:\n\n- $XT=2|v|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{XT}$ has the opposite direction to $v$\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{XT}=-2v.$\n\nAdditionally,\n\n$$\nt=\\overset{\\to }{OX}+\\overset{\\to }{XT}=-u-2v.\n$$\n\nConsequently, $t=\\lambda u+\\mu v=-1u-2v.$\n\nSince $v∦u,$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients, we get:\n\n- $\\lambda =-1$ and $\\mu =-2$" "![](images/2d/2d3a5c31af776cc49c2af118edc7256ca533320c7d53245593e5de6649ce03cf.png)\n\nIn $△OXT$ shown above, we have:\n\n- $OX=|\\mathbf{u}|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{OX}$ has the opposite direction to $\\mathbf{u}$\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{OX}=-\\mathbf{u}.$\n\nSimilarly:\n\n- $XT=2|\\mathbf{v}|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{XT}$ has the opposite direction to $\\mathbf{v}$\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{XT}=-2\\mathbf{v}.$\n\nAdditionally,\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{t}=\\overset{\\to }{OX}+\\overset{\\to }{XT}=-\\mathbf{u}-2\\mathbf{v}.\n$$\n\nConsequently, $\\mathbf{t}=\\lambda \\mathbf{u}+\\mu \\mathbf{v}=-1\\mathbf{u}-2\\mathbf{v}.$\n\nSince $\\mathbf{v}∦\\mathbf{u},$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients, we get:\n\n- $\\lambda =-1$ and $\\mu =-2$"] [:db/cas 17592186302255 :question/problem "![](images/de/de5905301a41f02237a48257a79e457f11ad74186942ca5bcf0002ab041aff91.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. If $t=\\lambda j+\\mu k$, then$\\,\\lambda =$\n{{field-1}}\nand \n$\\mu =$\n{{field-2}}." "![](images/de/de5905301a41f02237a48257a79e457f11ad74186942ca5bcf0002ab041aff91.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. If $\\mathbf{t}=\\lambda \\mathbf{j}+\\mu \\mathbf{k}$, then$\\,\\lambda =$\n{{field-1}}\nand \n$\\mu =$\n{{field-2}}."] [:db/cas 17592186302255 :question/worked-solution "![](images/1b/1be50e101b386f53ea5a2f1474f2e1d2debb8f98380d49a346dda32b37db70f1.png)\n\nIn $△OXT$ shown above, we have:\n\n- $OX=2|j|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{OX}$ has the opposite direction to $j$\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{OX}=-2j.$\n\nSimilarly:\n\n- $XT=\\frac{1}{2}|k|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{XT}$ has the opposite direction to $k$\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{XT}=-\\frac{1}{2}k.$\n\nAdditionally,\n\n$$\nt=\\overset{\\to }{OX}+\\overset{\\to }{XT}=-2j-\\frac{1}{2}k.\n$$\n\nConsequently, $t=\\lambda j+\\mu k=-2j-\\frac{1}{2}k.$\n\nSince $k∦j,$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients, we get:\n\n- $\\lambda =-2$ and $\\mu =-\\frac{1}{2}$" "![](images/1b/1be50e101b386f53ea5a2f1474f2e1d2debb8f98380d49a346dda32b37db70f1.png)\n\nIn $△OXT$ shown above, we have:\n\n- $OX=2|\\mathbf{j}|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{OX}$ has the opposite direction to $\\mathbf{j}$\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{OX}=-2\\mathbf{j}.$\n\nSimilarly:\n\n- $XT=\\frac{1}{2}|\\mathbf{k}|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{XT}$ has the opposite direction to $\\mathbf{k}$\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{XT}=-\\frac{1}{2}\\mathbf{k}.$\n\nAdditionally,\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{t}=\\overset{\\to }{OX}+\\overset{\\to }{XT}=-2\\mathbf{j}-\\frac{1}{2}\\mathbf{k}.\n$$\n\nConsequently, $\\mathbf{t}=\\lambda \\mathbf{j}+\\mu \\mathbf{k}=-2\\mathbf{j}-\\frac{1}{2}\\mathbf{k}.$\n\nSince $\\mathbf{k}∦\\mathbf{j},$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients, we get:\n\n- $\\lambda =-2$ and $\\mu =-\\frac{1}{2}$"] [:db/cas 17592186302256 :question/problem "![](images/0c/0c6dc7566d3ef4b0c047f048738aee6a204e0ba0e40cc48e83867456c6a1d67f.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. If $r=\\lambda p+\\mu q$, then$\\,\\lambda =$\n{{field-1}}\nand \n$\\mu =$\n{{field-2}}." "![](images/0c/0c6dc7566d3ef4b0c047f048738aee6a204e0ba0e40cc48e83867456c6a1d67f.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. If $\\mathbf{r}=\\lambda \\mathbf{p}+\\mu \\mathbf{q}$, then$\\,\\lambda =$\n{{field-1}}\nand \n$\\mu =$\n{{field-2}}."] [:db/cas 17592186302256 :question/worked-solution "![](images/56/563522ad9e5cf8e134a6669364986de592c7d63da244b050ef466e9f6c971a39.png)\n\nIn $△OPR$ shown above, we have \n\n- $OP=3|p|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{OP}$ and $p$ have the same direction\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{OP}=3p.$\n\nSimilarly:\n\n- $PR=2|q|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{PR}$ and $q$ have the same direction\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{PR}=2q.$\n\nAdditionally, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\overset{\\to }{OP}+\\overset{\\to }{PR} \\\\  & =3p+2q.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $r=\\lambda p+\\mu q=3p+2q.$\n\nSince $p∦q,$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients, we get:\n\n- $\\lambda =3$ and $\\mu =2$" "![](images/56/563522ad9e5cf8e134a6669364986de592c7d63da244b050ef466e9f6c971a39.png)\n\nIn $△OPR$ shown above, we have \n\n- $OP=3|\\mathbf{p}|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{OP}$ and $\\mathbf{p}$ have the same direction\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{OP}=3\\mathbf{p}.$\n\nSimilarly:\n\n- $PR=2|\\mathbf{q}|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{PR}$ and $\\mathbf{q}$ have the same direction\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{PR}=2\\mathbf{q}.$\n\nAdditionally, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{r} & =\\overset{\\to }{OP}+\\overset{\\to }{PR} \\\\  & =3\\mathbf{p}+2\\mathbf{q}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $\\mathbf{r}=\\lambda \\mathbf{p}+\\mu \\mathbf{q}=3\\mathbf{p}+2\\mathbf{q}.$\n\nSince $\\mathbf{p}∦\\mathbf{q},$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients, we get:\n\n- $\\lambda =3$ and $\\mu =2$"] [:db/cas 17592186302257 :question/problem "![](images/99/99d63eb31fba0757bde11dda3653e3791fcddb0b74b5325e20afcede4f3ea849.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. If $r=\\lambda a+\\mu b$, then$\\,\\lambda =$\n{{field-1}}\nand \n$\\mu =$\n{{field-2}}." "![](images/99/99d63eb31fba0757bde11dda3653e3791fcddb0b74b5325e20afcede4f3ea849.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. If $\\mathbf{r}=\\lambda \\mathbf{a}+\\mu \\mathbf{b}$, then$\\,\\lambda =$\n{{field-1}}\nand \n$\\mu =$\n{{field-2}}."] [:db/cas 17592186302257 :question/worked-solution "![](images/f7/f7acd51dcca9ad48f4aa7ec026beaeb67ddc9b1e88ab9b5042016674ba6ea542.png)\n\nIn $△OPR$ shown above, we have \n\n- $OP=|a|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{OP}$ and $a$ have the same direction\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{OP}=a.$\n\nSimilarly:\n\n- $PR=4|b|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{PR}$ and $b$ have the same direction\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{PR}=4b.$\n\nAdditionally, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\overset{\\to }{OP}+\\overset{\\to }{PR} \\\\  & =a+4b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $r=\\lambda a+\\mu b=1a+4b.$\n\nSince $a∦b,$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients, we get:\n\n- $\\lambda =1$ and $\\mu =4$" "![](images/f7/f7acd51dcca9ad48f4aa7ec026beaeb67ddc9b1e88ab9b5042016674ba6ea542.png)\n\nIn $△OPR$ shown above, we have \n\n- $OP=|\\mathbf{a}|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{OP}$ and $\\mathbf{a}$ have the same direction\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{OP}=\\mathbf{a}.$\n\nSimilarly:\n\n- $PR=4|\\mathbf{b}|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{PR}$ and $\\mathbf{b}$ have the same direction\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{PR}=4\\mathbf{b}.$\n\nAdditionally, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{r} & =\\overset{\\to }{OP}+\\overset{\\to }{PR} \\\\  & =\\mathbf{a}+4\\mathbf{b}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $\\mathbf{r}=\\lambda \\mathbf{a}+\\mu \\mathbf{b}=1\\mathbf{a}+4\\mathbf{b}.$\n\nSince $\\mathbf{a}∦\\mathbf{b},$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients, we get:\n\n- $\\lambda =1$ and $\\mu =4$"] [:db/cas 17592186302311 :question/problem "![](images/3d/3d34b42eb1874894af9675944e97e403d67db92bf96449fd8f87fbb14063a10e.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. If $c=a+\\lambda b,$ then$\\,\\lambda =$\n{{field-1}}." "![](images/3d/3d34b42eb1874894af9675944e97e403d67db92bf96449fd8f87fbb14063a10e.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. If $\\mathbf{c}=\\mathbf{a}+\\lambda \\mathbf{b},$ then$\\,\\lambda =$\n{{field-1}}."] [:db/cas 17592186302311 :question/worked-solution "To find $\\lambda ,$ we want to match the expression\n\n$$\nc=a+\\lambda b.\n$$\n\nWe are given that one of the components is $a.$ So, we begin with the vector $a,$ and then find the remaining vector needed to reach the point $C.$\n\n![](images/c0/c074e514a4ca52868a5c01d7a29182d62493f2cc955fd0582e52c78bdde2bf7d.png)\n\nNote that in $△OAC,$ we have:\n\n- $AC=2|b|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{AC}$ and $b$ have the same direction\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{AC}=2b.$\n\nAdditionally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c & =\\overset{\\to }{OA}+\\overset{\\to }{AC} \\\\  & =a+2b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $c=a+\\lambda b=a+2b.$\n\nSince $a∦b,$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients of $b,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\lambda =2\n$$" "To find $\\lambda ,$ we want to match the expression\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{c}=\\mathbf{a}+\\lambda \\mathbf{b}.\n$$\n\nWe are given that one of the components is $\\mathbf{a}.$ So, we begin with the vector $\\mathbf{a},$ and then find the remaining vector needed to reach the point $C.$\n\n![](images/c0/c074e514a4ca52868a5c01d7a29182d62493f2cc955fd0582e52c78bdde2bf7d.png)\n\nNote that in $△OAC,$ we have:\n\n- $AC=2|\\mathbf{b}|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{AC}$ and $\\mathbf{b}$ have the same direction\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{AC}=2\\mathbf{b}.$\n\nAdditionally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{c} & =\\overset{\\to }{OA}+\\overset{\\to }{AC} \\\\  & =\\mathbf{a}+2\\mathbf{b}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $\\mathbf{c}=\\mathbf{a}+\\lambda \\mathbf{b}=\\mathbf{a}+2\\mathbf{b}.$\n\nSince $\\mathbf{a}∦\\mathbf{b},$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients of $\\mathbf{b},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\lambda =2\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186302312 :question/problem "![](images/a4/a43139fc26d5778c40db020a7ebc9122c2fd54c0dcd4eb44cfd1671936a4055a.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. If $c=\\lambda a+2b,$ then$\\,\\lambda =$\n{{field-1}}." "![](images/a4/a43139fc26d5778c40db020a7ebc9122c2fd54c0dcd4eb44cfd1671936a4055a.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. If $\\mathbf{c}=\\lambda \\mathbf{a}+2\\mathbf{b},$ then$\\,\\lambda =$\n{{field-1}}."] [:db/cas 17592186302312 :question/worked-solution "To find $\\lambda ,$ we want to match the expression\n\n$$\nc=\\lambda a+2b.\n$$\n\nWe are given that one of the components is $2b.$ So, we first draw the vector $2b$ from the origin. Then we complete the path to the point $C.$\n\n![](images/1d/1d266a8324d18191f5bc8560be6539d54d1a11916f33ead7432d9f528769fc22.png)\n\nLet $P$ be the endpoint of the vector $2b.$ Then\n\n$$\nc=\\overset{\\to }{OP}+\\overset{\\to }{PC}.\n$$\n\nIn $△OPC,$ we have:\n\n- $PC=\\frac{1}{2}|a|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{PC}$ and $a$ have the same direction\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{PC}=\\frac{1}{2}a.$\n\nAdditionally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c & =\\overset{\\to }{OP}+\\overset{\\to }{PC} \\\\  & =2b+\\frac{1}{2}a.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $c=\\lambda a+2b=\\frac{1}{2}a+2b.$\n\nSince $a∦b,$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients of $a,$ we get:\n\n$$\n\\lambda =\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$" "To find $\\lambda ,$ we want to match the expression\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{c}=\\lambda \\mathbf{a}+2\\mathbf{b}.\n$$\n\nWe are given that one of the components is $2\\mathbf{b}.$ So, we first draw the vector $2\\mathbf{b}$ from the origin. Then we complete the path to the point $C.$\n\n![](images/1d/1d266a8324d18191f5bc8560be6539d54d1a11916f33ead7432d9f528769fc22.png)\n\nLet $P$ be the endpoint of the vector $2\\mathbf{b}.$ Then\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{c}=\\overset{\\to }{OP}+\\overset{\\to }{PC}.\n$$\n\nIn $△OPC,$ we have:\n\n- $PC=\\frac{1}{2}|\\mathbf{a}|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{PC}$ and $\\mathbf{a}$ have the same direction\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{PC}=\\frac{1}{2}\\mathbf{a}.$\n\nAdditionally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{c} & =\\overset{\\to }{OP}+\\overset{\\to }{PC} \\\\  & =2\\mathbf{b}+\\frac{1}{2}\\mathbf{a}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $\\mathbf{c}=\\lambda \\mathbf{a}+2\\mathbf{b}=\\frac{1}{2}\\mathbf{a}+2\\mathbf{b}.$\n\nSince $\\mathbf{a}∦\\mathbf{b},$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients of $\\mathbf{a},$ we get:\n\n$$\n\\lambda =\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186302315 :question/problem "![](images/c8/c8cbd5b93633e4e4899a1cde0da618f3be792bdbbd77db650c32814187b222f8.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. If $r=\\lambda p+\\mu q$, then$\\,\\lambda =$\n{{field-1}}\nand \n$\\mu =$\n{{field-2}}." "![](images/c8/c8cbd5b93633e4e4899a1cde0da618f3be792bdbbd77db650c32814187b222f8.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. If $\\mathbf{r}=\\lambda \\mathbf{p}+\\mu \\mathbf{q}$, then$\\,\\lambda =$\n{{field-1}}\nand \n$\\mu =$\n{{field-2}}."] [:db/cas 17592186302315 :question/worked-solution "![](images/67/6708537b79154a7778fe52e8d82b6577f93d40a06d3af0267e3e4b7eaa6468a9.png)\n\nIn $△OMR$ shown above, we have \n\n- $OM=3|q|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{OM}$ and $q$ have the opposite direction\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{OM}=-3q.$\n\nSimilarly:\n\n- $MR=2|p|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{MR}$ and $p$ have the same direction\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{MR}=2p.$\n\nAdditionally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\overset{\\to }{OM}+\\overset{\\to }{MR} \\\\  & =-3q+2p.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $r=\\lambda p+\\mu q=2p-3q.$\n\nSince $p∦q,$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients, we get:\n\n- $\\lambda =2$ and $\\mu =-3$" "![](images/67/6708537b79154a7778fe52e8d82b6577f93d40a06d3af0267e3e4b7eaa6468a9.png)\n\nIn $△OMR$ shown above, we have \n\n- $OM=3|\\mathbf{q}|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{OM}$ and $\\mathbf{q}$ have the opposite direction\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{OM}=-3\\mathbf{q}.$\n\nSimilarly:\n\n- $MR=2|\\mathbf{p}|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{MR}$ and $\\mathbf{p}$ have the same direction\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{MR}=2\\mathbf{p}.$\n\nAdditionally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{r} & =\\overset{\\to }{OM}+\\overset{\\to }{MR} \\\\  & =-3\\mathbf{q}+2\\mathbf{p}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $\\mathbf{r}=\\lambda \\mathbf{p}+\\mu \\mathbf{q}=2\\mathbf{p}-3\\mathbf{q}.$\n\nSince $\\mathbf{p}∦\\mathbf{q},$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients, we get:\n\n- $\\lambda =2$ and $\\mu =-3$"] [:db/cas 17592186302316 :question/problem "![](images/e6/e69402e97668c50f91258ca7e8dbb5206d1cd681e73bb9333303777adc866b70.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. If $i=\\lambda j+\\mu k$, then$\\,\\lambda =$\n{{field-1}}\nand \n$\\mu =$\n{{field-2}}." "![](images/e6/e69402e97668c50f91258ca7e8dbb5206d1cd681e73bb9333303777adc866b70.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. If $\\mathbf{i}=\\lambda \\mathbf{j}+\\mu \\mathbf{k}$, then$\\,\\lambda =$\n{{field-1}}\nand \n$\\mu =$\n{{field-2}}."] [:db/cas 17592186302316 :question/worked-solution "![](images/cb/cb8209592ec6d5be435622999c1b3eb86e1756cbe9852be0efdbbf41d41777ff.png)\n\nIn $△OJL$ shown above, we have  \n\n- $OJ=2|j|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{OJ}$ and $j$ have the opposite direction\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{OJ}=-2j.$  \n\nSimilarly:  \n\n- $JL=|k|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{JL}$ and $k$ have the same direction\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{JL}=k.$  \n\nAdditionally, \n\n$$\ni=\\overset{\\to }{OJ}+\\overset{\\to }{JL}=-2j+k.\n$$\n\nConsequently, $i=\\lambda j+\\mu k=-2j+k.$  \n\nSince $j∦k,$ the linear combination can only be written in one way.  Equating the coefficients, we get \n\n$$\n\\lambda =-2,\\,\\mu =1.\n$$" "![](images/cb/cb8209592ec6d5be435622999c1b3eb86e1756cbe9852be0efdbbf41d41777ff.png)\n\nIn $△OJL$ shown above, we have  \n\n- $OJ=2|\\mathbf{j}|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{OJ}$ and $\\mathbf{j}$ have the opposite direction\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{OJ}=-2\\mathbf{j}.$  \n\nSimilarly:  \n\n- $JL=|\\mathbf{k}|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{JL}$ and $\\mathbf{k}$ have the same direction\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{JL}=\\mathbf{k}.$  \n\nAdditionally, \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{i}=\\overset{\\to }{OJ}+\\overset{\\to }{JL}=-2\\mathbf{j}+\\mathbf{k}.\n$$\n\nConsequently, $\\mathbf{i}=\\lambda \\mathbf{j}+\\mu \\mathbf{k}=-2\\mathbf{j}+\\mathbf{k}.$  \n\nSince $\\mathbf{j}∦\\mathbf{k},$ the linear combination can only be written in one way.  Equating the coefficients, we get \n\n$$\n\\lambda =-2,\\,\\mu =1.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186302317 :question/problem "![](images/4c/4c1a8cde010abbeaf318a64e6c9f3b6cbd3e7bac84babbcda4c1ebfb483fa2b2.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. If $s=\\lambda m+\\mu n$, then$\\,\\lambda =$\n{{field-1}}\nand \n$\\mu =$\n{{field-2}}." "![](images/4c/4c1a8cde010abbeaf318a64e6c9f3b6cbd3e7bac84babbcda4c1ebfb483fa2b2.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. If $\\mathbf{s}=\\lambda \\mathbf{m}+\\mu \\mathbf{n}$, then$\\,\\lambda =$\n{{field-1}}\nand \n$\\mu =$\n{{field-2}}."] [:db/cas 17592186302317 :question/worked-solution "![](images/e0/e018cc5b4760b578d9ca514f9de6641e823bf5f6a8e17f019ebf24fc6ccb2248.png)\n\nIn $△OTS$ shown above, we have \n\n- $OT=4|m|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{OT}$ and $m$ have the opposite direction\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{OT}=-4m.$\n\nSimilarly:\n\n- $TS=2|n|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{TS}$ and $n$ have the same direction\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{TS}=2n.$\n\nAdditionally, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}s & =\\overset{\\to }{OT}+\\overset{\\to }{TS} \\\\  & =-4m+2n.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $s=\\lambda m+\\mu n=-4m+2n.$\n\nSince $m∦n,$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients, we get:\n\n- $\\lambda =-4$ and $\\mu =2$" "![](images/e0/e018cc5b4760b578d9ca514f9de6641e823bf5f6a8e17f019ebf24fc6ccb2248.png)\n\nIn $△OTS$ shown above, we have \n\n- $OT=4|\\mathbf{m}|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{OT}$ and $\\mathbf{m}$ have the opposite direction\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{OT}=-4\\mathbf{m}.$\n\nSimilarly:\n\n- $TS=2|\\mathbf{n}|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{TS}$ and $\\mathbf{n}$ have the same direction\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{TS}=2\\mathbf{n}.$\n\nAdditionally, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{s} & =\\overset{\\to }{OT}+\\overset{\\to }{TS} \\\\  & =-4\\mathbf{m}+2\\mathbf{n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $\\mathbf{s}=\\lambda \\mathbf{m}+\\mu \\mathbf{n}=-4\\mathbf{m}+2\\mathbf{n}.$\n\nSince $\\mathbf{m}∦\\mathbf{n},$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients, we get:\n\n- $\\lambda =-4$ and $\\mu =2$"] [:db/cas 17592186327054 :answer/value "\\overset{\\to }{OW}=\\frac{1}{3}u+\\frac{2}{3}v" "\\overset{\\to }{OW}=\\frac{1}{3}\\mathbf{u}+\\frac{2}{3}\\mathbf{v}"] [:db/cas 17592186327095 :answer/value "\\overset{\\to }{OW}=\\frac{7}{9}v-\\frac{2}{9}u" "\\overset{\\to }{OW}=\\frac{7}{9}\\mathbf{v}-\\frac{2}{9}\\mathbf{u}"] [:db/cas 17592186327189 :answer/value "\\overset{\\to }{OW}=\\frac{7}{9}u+\\frac{2}{9}v" "\\overset{\\to }{OW}=\\frac{7}{9}\\mathbf{u}+\\frac{2}{9}\\mathbf{v}"] [:db/cas 17592186328732 :answer/value "\\overset{\\to }{OW}=\\frac{2}{3}u+\\frac{1}{3}v" "\\overset{\\to }{OW}=\\frac{2}{3}\\mathbf{u}+\\frac{1}{3}\\mathbf{v}"] [:db/cas 17592186329092 :answer/value "\\overset{\\to }{OW}=-\\frac{1}{3}v+\\frac{2}{3}u" "\\overset{\\to }{OW}=-\\frac{1}{3}\\mathbf{v}+\\frac{2}{3}\\mathbf{u}"] [:db/cas 17592186329675 :question/problem "![](images/dc/dc708da9adb4f61161ee1face89403bf1a6ce9762905e8a59b4b3ed9031c817a.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. Given that $r=\\lambda p+\\mu q$, find $\\lambda $ and $\\mu .$" "![](images/dc/dc708da9adb4f61161ee1face89403bf1a6ce9762905e8a59b4b3ed9031c817a.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. Given that $\\mathbf{r}=\\lambda \\mathbf{p}+\\mu \\mathbf{q}$, find $\\lambda $ and $\\mu .$"] [:db/cas 17592186329675 :question/worked-solution "![](images/84/84f8951c4921eca20b00132996dc7b2dfafe047d58574e1822da795e1444c861.png)\n\nIn $△OPR$ shown above, we have \n\n- $OP=3|p|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{OP}$ has the opposite direction to $p$\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{OP}=-3p.$\n\nSimilarly:\n\n- $PR=2|q|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{PR}$ has the opposite direction to $q$\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{PR}=-2q.$\n\nAdditionally, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\overset{\\to }{OP}+\\overset{\\to }{PR} \\\\  & =-3p-2q.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $r=\\lambda p+\\mu q=-3p-2q.$\n\nSince $p∦q,$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients, we get:\n\n- $\\lambda =-3$ and $\\mu =-2$" "![](images/84/84f8951c4921eca20b00132996dc7b2dfafe047d58574e1822da795e1444c861.png)\n\nIn $△OPR$ shown above, we have \n\n- $OP=3|\\mathbf{p}|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{OP}$ has the opposite direction to $\\mathbf{p}$\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{OP}=-3\\mathbf{p}.$\n\nSimilarly:\n\n- $PR=2|\\mathbf{q}|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{PR}$ has the opposite direction to $\\mathbf{q}$\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{PR}=-2\\mathbf{q}.$\n\nAdditionally, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{r} & =\\overset{\\to }{OP}+\\overset{\\to }{PR} \\\\  & =-3\\mathbf{p}-2\\mathbf{q}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $\\mathbf{r}=\\lambda \\mathbf{p}+\\mu \\mathbf{q}=-3\\mathbf{p}-2\\mathbf{q}.$\n\nSince $\\mathbf{p}∦\\mathbf{q},$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients, we get:\n\n- $\\lambda =-3$ and $\\mu =-2$"] [:db/cas 17592186329676 :question/problem "![](images/e2/e2f149fb4f32fb264e0af6898e7737d570ec90bbff31cf1256eb9bd2636ceab3.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. Given that $w=\\lambda u+\\mu v$, find $\\lambda $ and $\\mu .$" "![](images/e2/e2f149fb4f32fb264e0af6898e7737d570ec90bbff31cf1256eb9bd2636ceab3.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. Given that $\\mathbf{w}=\\lambda \\mathbf{u}+\\mu \\mathbf{v}$, find $\\lambda $ and $\\mu .$"] [:db/cas 17592186329676 :question/worked-solution "![](images/58/589f9f524a7c98da61ad6c5001b8d392d89073d5734cd70cfa750d153fe6f7b7.png)\n\nIn $△OUW$ shown above, we have \n\n- $OU=2|v|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{OU}$ has the opposite direction to $v$\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{OU}=-2v.$\n\nSimilarly:\n\n- $UW=4|u|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{UW}$ has the opposite direction to $u$\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{UW}=-4u.$\n\nAdditionally, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}w & =\\overset{\\to }{OU}+\\overset{\\to }{UW} \\\\  & =-2v-4u \\\\  & =-4u-2v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $w=\\lambda u+\\mu v=-4u-2v.$\n\nSince $u∦v,$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients, we get:\n\n- $\\lambda =-4$ and $\\mu =-2$" "![](images/58/589f9f524a7c98da61ad6c5001b8d392d89073d5734cd70cfa750d153fe6f7b7.png)\n\nIn $△OUW$ shown above, we have \n\n- $OU=2|\\mathbf{v}|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{OU}$ has the opposite direction to $\\mathbf{v}$\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{OU}=-2\\mathbf{v}.$\n\nSimilarly:\n\n- $UW=4|\\mathbf{u}|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{UW}$ has the opposite direction to $\\mathbf{u}$\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{UW}=-4\\mathbf{u}.$\n\nAdditionally, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{w} & =\\overset{\\to }{OU}+\\overset{\\to }{UW} \\\\  & =-2\\mathbf{v}-4\\mathbf{u} \\\\  & =-4\\mathbf{u}-2\\mathbf{v}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $\\mathbf{w}=\\lambda \\mathbf{u}+\\mu \\mathbf{v}=-4\\mathbf{u}-2\\mathbf{v}.$\n\nSince $\\mathbf{u}∦\\mathbf{v},$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients, we get:\n\n- $\\lambda =-4$ and $\\mu =-2$"] [:db/cas 17592186329678 :question/problem "![](images/04/040127594f639248736d4492f1853bcb1e6ef18c37db9242ebe466045713628a.png)\n\nConsider the diagram above, where $\\overset{\\to }{OU}=u,$ $\\overset{\\to }{OV}=v$ and $W$ divides $\\overset{―}{UV}$ in the ratio $UW:WV=2:7.$ Find $\\overset{\\to }{OW}.$" "![](images/04/040127594f639248736d4492f1853bcb1e6ef18c37db9242ebe466045713628a.png)\n\nConsider the diagram above, where $\\overset{\\to }{OU}=\\mathbf{u},$ $\\overset{\\to }{OV}=\\mathbf{v}$ and $W$ divides $\\overline{UV}$ in the ratio $UW:WV=2:7.$ Find $\\overset{\\to }{OW}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186329678 :question/worked-solution "From the diagram, we see that\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{OW}=\\overset{\\to }{OU}+\\overset{\\to }{UW}.\n$$\n\nWe're told that $\\overset{\\to }{OU}=u,$ so we just need to work out $\\overset{\\to }{UW}.$\n\nFirst, from $△OUV,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{UV} & =\\overset{\\to }{UO}+\\overset{\\to }{OV} \\\\  & =-\\overset{\\to }{OU}+\\overset{\\to }{OV} \\\\  & =-u+v \\\\  & =v-u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $UW:WV=2:7,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{UW}=\\frac{2}{9}\\overset{\\to }{UV}=\\frac{2}{9}(v-u).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{OW} & =\\overset{\\to }{OU}+\\overset{\\to }{UW} \\\\  & =u+\\frac{2}{9}(v-u) \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{9}u+\\frac{2}{9}v.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "From the diagram, we see that\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{OW}=\\overset{\\to }{OU}+\\overset{\\to }{UW}.\n$$\n\nWe're told that $\\overset{\\to }{OU}=\\mathbf{u},$ so we just need to work out $\\overset{\\to }{UW}.$\n\nFirst, from $△OUV,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{UV} & =\\overset{\\to }{UO}+\\overset{\\to }{OV} \\\\  & =-\\overset{\\to }{OU}+\\overset{\\to }{OV} \\\\  & =-\\mathbf{u}+\\mathbf{v} \\\\  & =\\mathbf{v}-\\mathbf{u}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $UW:WV=2:7,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{UW}=\\frac{2}{9}\\overset{\\to }{UV}=\\frac{2}{9}(\\mathbf{v}-\\mathbf{u}).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{OW} & =\\overset{\\to }{OU}+\\overset{\\to }{UW} \\\\  & =\\mathbf{u}+\\frac{2}{9}(\\mathbf{v}-\\mathbf{u}) \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{9}\\mathbf{u}+\\frac{2}{9}\\mathbf{v}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186329679 :question/problem "![](images/be/be4bd3ff63baa05d861cbad6a26c6b53b8b7f1fed64feb0c0465408c6e7ced2d.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. Given that $l=\\lambda j+\\mu k$, find $\\lambda $ and $\\mu .$" "![](images/be/be4bd3ff63baa05d861cbad6a26c6b53b8b7f1fed64feb0c0465408c6e7ced2d.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. Given that $\\mathbf{l}=\\lambda \\mathbf{j}+\\mu \\mathbf{k}$, find $\\lambda $ and $\\mu .$"] [:db/cas 17592186329679 :question/worked-solution "![](images/a2/a216faf7db26da01730a18607a298e442af47a7d45a7a69e6fd12cbfd2229360.png)\n\nIn $△OJL$ shown above, we have \n\n- $OJ=3|j|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{OJ}$ and $j$ have the opposite direction\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{OJ}=-3j.$\n\nSimilarly:\n\n- $JL=|k|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{JL}$ and $k$ have the same direction\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{JL}=k.$\n\nAdditionally, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l & =\\overset{\\to }{OJ}+\\overset{\\to }{JL} \\\\  & =-3j+k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $l=\\lambda j+\\mu k=-3j+k.$\n\nSince $j∦k,$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients, we get:\n\n- $\\lambda =-3$ and $\\mu =1$" "![](images/a2/a216faf7db26da01730a18607a298e442af47a7d45a7a69e6fd12cbfd2229360.png)\n\nIn $△OJL$ shown above, we have \n\n- $OJ=3|\\mathbf{j}|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{OJ}$ and $\\mathbf{j}$ have the opposite direction\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{OJ}=-3\\mathbf{j}.$\n\nSimilarly:\n\n- $JL=|\\mathbf{k}|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{JL}$ and $\\mathbf{k}$ have the same direction\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{JL}=\\mathbf{k}.$\n\nAdditionally, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{l} & =\\overset{\\to }{OJ}+\\overset{\\to }{JL} \\\\  & =-3\\mathbf{j}+\\mathbf{k}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $\\mathbf{l}=\\lambda \\mathbf{j}+\\mu \\mathbf{k}=-3\\mathbf{j}+\\mathbf{k}.$\n\nSince $\\mathbf{j}∦\\mathbf{k},$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients, we get:\n\n- $\\lambda =-3$ and $\\mu =1$"] [:db/cas 17592186338753 :question/problem "![](images/75/7578bb2e5b594161744d96d918fa75e0225e723138d010dba3ed635098e75ca2.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. Given that $c=a+\\lambda b,$ find $\\lambda .$" "![](images/75/7578bb2e5b594161744d96d918fa75e0225e723138d010dba3ed635098e75ca2.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. Given that $\\mathbf{c}=\\mathbf{a}+\\lambda \\mathbf{b},$ find $\\lambda .$"] [:db/cas 17592186338753 :question/worked-solution "To find $\\lambda ,$ we want to match the expression\n\n$$\nc=a+\\lambda b.\n$$\n\nWe are given that one of the components is $a.$ So, we begin with the vector $a,$ and then find the remaining vector needed to reach the point $C.$\n\n![](images/47/4797ffa54b3c2e4d177a6c3d527b1aaf3f1460d3c509bca86395e5f360314d25.png)\n\nNote that in $△OAC,$ we have:\n\n- $AC=3|b|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{AC}$ and $b$ have the same direction\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{AC}=3b.$\n\nAdditionally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c & =\\overset{\\to }{OA}+\\overset{\\to }{AC} \\\\  & =a+3b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $c=a+\\lambda b=a+3b.$\n\nSince $a∦b,$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients of $b,$ we get:\n\n$$\n\\lambda =3\n$$" "To find $\\lambda ,$ we want to match the expression\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{c}=\\mathbf{a}+\\lambda \\mathbf{b}.\n$$\n\nWe are given that one of the components is $\\mathbf{a}.$ So, we begin with the vector $\\mathbf{a},$ and then find the remaining vector needed to reach the point $C.$\n\n![](images/47/4797ffa54b3c2e4d177a6c3d527b1aaf3f1460d3c509bca86395e5f360314d25.png)\n\nNote that in $△OAC,$ we have:\n\n- $AC=3|\\mathbf{b}|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{AC}$ and $\\mathbf{b}$ have the same direction\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{AC}=3\\mathbf{b}.$\n\nAdditionally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{c} & =\\overset{\\to }{OA}+\\overset{\\to }{AC} \\\\  & =\\mathbf{a}+3\\mathbf{b}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $\\mathbf{c}=\\mathbf{a}+\\lambda \\mathbf{b}=\\mathbf{a}+3\\mathbf{b}.$\n\nSince $\\mathbf{a}∦\\mathbf{b},$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients of $\\mathbf{b},$ we get:\n\n$$\n\\lambda =3\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186338754 :question/problem "![](images/06/068eeaf4f0ac63aafca9ee09f06b94f6e52cbe00b4a625970881fe105e299a40.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. Given that $c=a+\\lambda b,$ find $\\lambda .$" "![](images/06/068eeaf4f0ac63aafca9ee09f06b94f6e52cbe00b4a625970881fe105e299a40.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. Given that $\\mathbf{c}=\\mathbf{a}+\\lambda \\mathbf{b},$ find $\\lambda .$"] [:db/cas 17592186338754 :question/worked-solution "To find $\\lambda ,$ we want to match the expression\n\n$$\nc=a+\\lambda b.\n$$\n\nWe are given that one of the components is $a.$ So, we begin with the vector $a,$ and then find the remaining vector needed to reach the point $C.$\n\n![](images/45/45a4735bf7710ff7c2699a9b42b10c34807d6162a3d7da38842ba87f13158788.png)\n\nNote that in $△OAC,$ we have:\n\n- $AC=\\frac{1}{2}|b|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{AC}$ and $b$ have the same direction\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{AC}=\\frac{1}{2}b.$\n\nAdditionally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c & =\\overset{\\to }{OA}+\\overset{\\to }{AC} \\\\  & =a+\\frac{1}{2}b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $c=a+\\lambda b=a+\\frac{1}{2}b.$\n\nSince $a∦b,$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients of $b,$ we get:\n\n$$\n\\lambda =\\frac{1}{2}\n$$" "To find $\\lambda ,$ we want to match the expression\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{c}=\\mathbf{a}+\\lambda \\mathbf{b}.\n$$\n\nWe are given that one of the components is $\\mathbf{a}.$ So, we begin with the vector $\\mathbf{a},$ and then find the remaining vector needed to reach the point $C.$\n\n![](images/45/45a4735bf7710ff7c2699a9b42b10c34807d6162a3d7da38842ba87f13158788.png)\n\nNote that in $△OAC,$ we have:\n\n- $AC=\\frac{1}{2}|\\mathbf{b}|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{AC}$ and $\\mathbf{b}$ have the same direction\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{AC}=\\frac{1}{2}\\mathbf{b}.$\n\nAdditionally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{c} & =\\overset{\\to }{OA}+\\overset{\\to }{AC} \\\\  & =\\mathbf{a}+\\frac{1}{2}\\mathbf{b}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $\\mathbf{c}=\\mathbf{a}+\\lambda \\mathbf{b}=\\mathbf{a}+\\frac{1}{2}\\mathbf{b}.$\n\nSince $\\mathbf{a}∦\\mathbf{b},$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients of $\\mathbf{b},$ we get:\n\n$$\n\\lambda =\\frac{1}{2}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186338756 :question/problem "![](images/4b/4b5f0399c2fc40b26dacb6b5c323bb54ba3ecbcf4b4375cdfecc59631140c0da.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. Given that $c=a+\\lambda b,$ find $\\lambda .$" "![](images/4b/4b5f0399c