(images/24/245a7b9af068b057e16a0c84971f5bb356a9fc56374cb3c17c4896d7a9b815ca.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. Given that $c=\\lambda a+b,$ find $\\lambda .$" "![](images/24/245a7b9af068b057e16a0c84971f5bb356a9fc56374cb3c17c4896d7a9b815ca.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. Given that $\\mathbf{c}=\\lambda \\mathbf{a}+\\mathbf{b},$ find $\\lambda .$"] [:db/cas 17592186338757 :question/worked-solution "To find $\\lambda ,$ we want to match the expression\n\n$$\nc=\\lambda a+b.\n$$\n\nWe are given that one of the components is $b.$ So, we begin with the vector $b,$ and then find the remaining vector needed to reach the point $C.$\n\n![](images/9b/9b99ad62bfedf6945020d345ef0bbc3531c58af1088698a78daf482cd4c14fe1.png)\n\nNote that in $△OBC,$ we have:\n\n- $BC=4|a|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{BC}$ and $a$ have the same direction\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{BC}=4a.$\n\nAdditionally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c & =\\overset{\\to }{OB}+\\overset{\\to }{BC} \\\\  & =b+4a.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $c=\\lambda a+b=b+4a.$\n\nSince $a∦b,$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients of $a,$ we get:\n\n$$\n\\lambda =4\n$$" "To find $\\lambda ,$ we want to match the expression\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{c}=\\lambda \\mathbf{a}+\\mathbf{b}.\n$$\n\nWe are given that one of the components is $\\mathbf{b}.$ So, we begin with the vector $\\mathbf{b},$ and then find the remaining vector needed to reach the point $C.$\n\n![](images/9b/9b99ad62bfedf6945020d345ef0bbc3531c58af1088698a78daf482cd4c14fe1.png)\n\nNote that in $△OBC,$ we have:\n\n- $BC=4|\\mathbf{a}|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{BC}$ and $\\mathbf{a}$ have the same direction\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{BC}=4\\mathbf{a}.$\n\nAdditionally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{c} & =\\overset{\\to }{OB}+\\overset{\\to }{BC} \\\\  & =\\mathbf{b}+4\\mathbf{a}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $\\mathbf{c}=\\lambda \\mathbf{a}+\\mathbf{b}=\\mathbf{b}+4\\mathbf{a}.$\n\nSince $\\mathbf{a}∦\\mathbf{b},$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients of $\\mathbf{a},$ we get:\n\n$$\n\\lambda =4\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186345312 :question/problem "![](images/c8/c800644be3cc0ad0688d52948a4a2cb813cbe14b1fe899abe5a1d92613a687bf.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. Given that $r=\\lambda p+\\mu q$, find $\\lambda $ and $\\mu .$" "![](images/c8/c800644be3cc0ad0688d52948a4a2cb813cbe14b1fe899abe5a1d92613a687bf.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. Given that $\\mathbf{r}=\\lambda \\mathbf{p}+\\mu \\mathbf{q}$, find $\\lambda $ and $\\mu .$"] [:db/cas 17592186345312 :question/worked-solution "![](images/90/90272fad8af526de6e169faba8daae29210c1f502c4959d0e1829e5ad65476f0.png)\n\nIn $△OPR$ shown above, we have \n\n- $OP=2|p|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{OP}$ and $p$ have the same direction\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{OP}=2p.$\n\nSimilarly:\n\n- $PR=4|q|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{PR}$ and $q$ have the same direction\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{PR}=4q.$\n\nAdditionally, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\overset{\\to }{OP}+\\overset{\\to }{PR} \\\\  & =2p+4q.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $r=\\lambda p+\\mu q=2p+4q.$\n\nSince $p∦q,$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients, we get:\n\n- $\\lambda =2$ and $\\mu =4$" "![](images/90/90272fad8af526de6e169faba8daae29210c1f502c4959d0e1829e5ad65476f0.png)\n\nIn $△OPR$ shown above, we have \n\n- $OP=2|\\mathbf{p}|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{OP}$ and $\\mathbf{p}$ have the same direction\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{OP}=2\\mathbf{p}.$\n\nSimilarly:\n\n- $PR=4|\\mathbf{q}|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{PR}$ and $\\mathbf{q}$ have the same direction\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{PR}=4\\mathbf{q}.$\n\nAdditionally, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{r} & =\\overset{\\to }{OP}+\\overset{\\to }{PR} \\\\  & =2\\mathbf{p}+4\\mathbf{q}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $\\mathbf{r}=\\lambda \\mathbf{p}+\\mu \\mathbf{q}=2\\mathbf{p}+4\\mathbf{q}.$\n\nSince $\\mathbf{p}∦\\mathbf{q},$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients, we get:\n\n- $\\lambda =2$ and $\\mu =4$"] [:db/cas 17592186364880 :question/problem "![](images/e1/e1391a86176b1453c05f6ab43f2b5360fac00287e621d8917a142116990688b2.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. Given that $c=\\lambda a+3b$, find $\\lambda .$" "![](images/e1/e1391a86176b1453c05f6ab43f2b5360fac00287e621d8917a142116990688b2.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. Given that $\\mathbf{c}=\\lambda \\mathbf{a}+3\\mathbf{b}$, find $\\lambda .$"] [:db/cas 17592186364880 :question/worked-solution "To find $\\lambda ,$ we want to match the expression\n\n$$\nc=\\lambda a+3b.\n$$\n\nWe are given that one of the components is $3b.$ So, we first draw the vector $3b$ from the origin. Then we complete the path to the point $C.$\n\n![](images/ea/eadc3cc79d5c0f9b58bd8afc6864dca8e5f794cf398921e2ad540b491819562f.png)\n\nLet $P$ be the endpoint of the vector $3b.$ Then\n\n$$\nc=\\overset{\\to }{OP}+\\overset{\\to }{PC}.\n$$\n\nIn $△OPC,$ we have:\n\n- $PC=|a|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{PC}$ and $a$ have the same direction\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{PC}=a.$\n\nAdditionally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c & =\\overset{\\to }{OP}+\\overset{\\to }{PC} \\\\  & =3b+a.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $c=\\lambda a+3b=a+3b.$\n\nSince $a∦b,$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients of $a,$ we get:\n\n$$\n\\lambda =1\n$$" "To find $\\lambda ,$ we want to match the expression\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{c}=\\lambda \\mathbf{a}+3\\mathbf{b}.\n$$\n\nWe are given that one of the components is $3\\mathbf{b}.$ So, we first draw the vector $3\\mathbf{b}$ from the origin. Then we complete the path to the point $C.$\n\n![](images/ea/eadc3cc79d5c0f9b58bd8afc6864dca8e5f794cf398921e2ad540b491819562f.png)\n\nLet $P$ be the endpoint of the vector $3\\mathbf{b}.$ Then\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{c}=\\overset{\\to }{OP}+\\overset{\\to }{PC}.\n$$\n\nIn $△OPC,$ we have:\n\n- $PC=|\\mathbf{a}|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{PC}$ and $\\mathbf{a}$ have the same direction\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{PC}=\\mathbf{a}.$\n\nAdditionally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{c} & =\\overset{\\to }{OP}+\\overset{\\to }{PC} \\\\  & =3\\mathbf{b}+\\mathbf{a}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $\\mathbf{c}=\\lambda \\mathbf{a}+3\\mathbf{b}=\\mathbf{a}+3\\mathbf{b}.$\n\nSince $\\mathbf{a}∦\\mathbf{b},$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients of $\\mathbf{a},$ we get:\n\n$$\n\\lambda =1\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186364895 :answer/value "\\overset{\\to }{OT}=\\frac{1}{2}a+b" "\\overset{\\to }{OT}=\\frac{1}{2}\\mathbf{a}+\\mathbf{b}"] [:db/cas 17592186364896 :answer/value "\\overset{\\to }{OT}=\\frac{1}{2}a+\\frac{1}{2}b" "\\overset{\\to }{OT}=\\frac{1}{2}\\mathbf{a}+\\frac{1}{2}\\mathbf{b}"] [:db/cas 17592186364897 :answer/value "\\overset{\\to }{OT}=\\frac{2}{3}a+\\frac{1}{3}b" "\\overset{\\to }{OT}=\\frac{2}{3}\\mathbf{a}+\\frac{1}{3}\\mathbf{b}"] [:db/cas 17592186364898 :answer/value "\\overset{\\to }{OT}=\\frac{1}{3}a+\\frac{2}{3}b" "\\overset{\\to }{OT}=\\frac{1}{3}\\mathbf{a}+\\frac{2}{3}\\mathbf{b}"] [:db/cas 17592186364899 :answer/value "\\overset{\\to }{OT}=a+\\frac{1}{2}b" "\\overset{\\to }{OT}=\\mathbf{a}+\\frac{1}{2}\\mathbf{b}"] [:db/cas 17592186365574 :question/problem "![](images/0f/0f9ee37fe5f1cf32ba470b24e5d27b4164bd00a0caeae0536ae48d4cc5836c6d.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. If $t=\\lambda a+\\mu b$, then$\\,\\lambda =$\n{{field-1}}\nand \n$\\mu =$\n{{field-2}}." "![](images/0f/0f9ee37fe5f1cf32ba470b24e5d27b4164bd00a0caeae0536ae48d4cc5836c6d.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. If $\\mathbf{t}=\\lambda \\mathbf{a}+\\mu \\mathbf{b}$, then$\\,\\lambda =$\n{{field-1}}\nand \n$\\mu =$\n{{field-2}}."] [:db/cas 17592186365574 :question/worked-solution "![](images/c9/c95e718a0c30e2be1f3678123eea0006b17fd781305c06a4f277488c2389337f.png)\n\nIn $△OXT$ shown above, we have:\n\n- $OX=\\frac{7}{4}\\cdot |a|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{OX}$ has the opposite direction to $a$\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{OX}=-\\frac{7}{4}a.$\n\nSimilarly:\n\n- $XT=2|b|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{XT}$ has the opposite direction to $b$\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{XT}=-2b.$\n\nAdditionally,\n\n$$\nt=\\overset{\\to }{OX}+\\overset{\\to }{XT}=-\\frac{7}{4}a-2b.\n$$\n\nConsequently, $t=\\lambda a+\\mu b=-\\frac{7}{4}a-2b.$\n\nSince $b∦a,$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients, we get:\n\n- $\\lambda =-\\frac{7}{4}$ and $\\mu =-2$" "![](images/c9/c95e718a0c30e2be1f3678123eea0006b17fd781305c06a4f277488c2389337f.png)\n\nIn $△OXT$ shown above, we have:\n\n- $OX=\\frac{7}{4}\\cdot |\\mathbf{a}|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{OX}$ has the opposite direction to $\\mathbf{a}$\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{OX}=-\\frac{7}{4}\\mathbf{a}.$\n\nSimilarly:\n\n- $XT=2|\\mathbf{b}|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{XT}$ has the opposite direction to $\\mathbf{b}$\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{XT}=-2\\mathbf{b}.$\n\nAdditionally,\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{t}=\\overset{\\to }{OX}+\\overset{\\to }{XT}=-\\frac{7}{4}\\mathbf{a}-2\\mathbf{b}.\n$$\n\nConsequently, $\\mathbf{t}=\\lambda \\mathbf{a}+\\mu \\mathbf{b}=-\\frac{7}{4}\\mathbf{a}-2\\mathbf{b}.$\n\nSince $\\mathbf{b}∦\\mathbf{a},$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients, we get:\n\n- $\\lambda =-\\frac{7}{4}$ and $\\mu =-2$"] [:db/cas 17592186387001 :question/problem "![](images/65/6560a7f05ae95638cbfe902dfa022b2d8de6b07c6e1e6f85051ccf87b92ec338.png)\n\nConsider the diagram above, where $\\overset{\\to }{OH}=h,$ $\\overset{\\to }{OI}=i$ and $J$ divides $\\overset{―}{HI}$ in the ratio $HJ:JI=5:3.$ Find $\\overset{\\to }{OJ}.$" "![](images/65/6560a7f05ae95638cbfe902dfa022b2d8de6b07c6e1e6f85051ccf87b92ec338.png)\n\nConsider the diagram above, where $\\overset{\\to }{OH}=\\mathbf{h},$ $\\overset{\\to }{OI}=\\mathbf{i}$ and $J$ divides $\\overline{HI}$ in the ratio $HJ:JI=5:3.$ Find $\\overset{\\to }{OJ}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186387001 :question/worked-solution "From the diagram, we see that\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{OJ}=\\overset{\\to }{OH}+\\overset{\\to }{HJ}.\n$$\n\nWe're told that $\\overset{\\to }{OH}=h,$ so we just need to work out $\\overset{\\to }{HJ}.$\n\nFirst, from $△OHI,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{HI} & =\\overset{\\to }{HO}+\\overset{\\to }{OI} \\\\  & =-\\overset{\\to }{OH}+\\overset{\\to }{OI} \\\\  & =-h+i \\\\  & =i-h.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $HJ:JI=5:3,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{HJ}=\\frac{5}{8}\\overset{\\to }{HI}=\\frac{5}{8}(i-h).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{OJ} & =\\overset{\\to }{OH}+\\overset{\\to }{HJ} \\\\  & =h+\\frac{5}{8}(i-h) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{8}h+\\frac{5}{8}i.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "From the diagram, we see that\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{OJ}=\\overset{\\to }{OH}+\\overset{\\to }{HJ}.\n$$\n\nWe're told that $\\overset{\\to }{OH}=\\mathbf{h},$ so we just need to work out $\\overset{\\to }{HJ}.$\n\nFirst, from $△OHI,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{HI} & =\\overset{\\to }{HO}+\\overset{\\to }{OI} \\\\  & =-\\overset{\\to }{OH}+\\overset{\\to }{OI} \\\\  & =-\\mathbf{h}+\\mathbf{i} \\\\  & =\\mathbf{i}-\\mathbf{h}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $HJ:JI=5:3,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{HJ}=\\frac{5}{8}\\overset{\\to }{HI}=\\frac{5}{8}(\\mathbf{i}-\\mathbf{h}).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{OJ} & =\\overset{\\to }{OH}+\\overset{\\to }{HJ} \\\\  & =\\mathbf{h}+\\frac{5}{8}(\\mathbf{i}-\\mathbf{h}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{8}\\mathbf{h}+\\frac{5}{8}\\mathbf{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186387003 :answer/value "\\overset{\\to }{OJ}=\\frac{1}{2}h+\\frac{1}{2}i" "\\overset{\\to }{OJ}=\\frac{1}{2}\\mathbf{h}+\\frac{1}{2}\\mathbf{i}"] [:db/cas 17592186387004 :answer/value "\\overset{\\to }{OJ}=\\frac{3}{4}h+\\frac{1}{4}i" "\\overset{\\to }{OJ}=\\frac{3}{4}\\mathbf{h}+\\frac{1}{4}\\mathbf{i}"] [:db/cas 17592186387005 :answer/value "\\overset{\\to }{OJ}=\\frac{5}{8}h+\\frac{3}{8}i" "\\overset{\\to }{OJ}=\\frac{5}{8}\\mathbf{h}+\\frac{3}{8}\\mathbf{i}"] [:db/cas 17592186387006 :answer/value "\\overset{\\to }{OJ}=\\frac{1}{8}h+\\frac{3}{4}i" "\\overset{\\to }{OJ}=\\frac{1}{8}\\mathbf{h}+\\frac{3}{4}\\mathbf{i}"] [:db/cas 17592186387007 :answer/value "\\overset{\\to }{OJ}=\\frac{3}{8}h+\\frac{5}{8}i" "\\overset{\\to }{OJ}=\\frac{3}{8}\\mathbf{h}+\\frac{5}{8}\\mathbf{i}"] [:db/cas 17592186390558 :question/problem "![](images/ba/ba82c6b68026b0f4339c1e4fa01c4b1aa1b7aee74b74176f81bba1c0e1de259f.png)\n\nConsider the diagram above, where $\\overset{\\to }{OR}=r,$ $\\overset{\\to }{OS}=s$ and $V$ divides $\\overset{―}{RS}$ in the ratio $RV:VS=6:5.$ Find $\\overset{\\to }{OV}.$" "![](images/ba/ba82c6b68026b0f4339c1e4fa01c4b1aa1b7aee74b74176f81bba1c0e1de259f.png)\n\nConsider the diagram above, where $\\overset{\\to }{OR}=\\mathbf{r},$ $\\overset{\\to }{OS}=\\mathbf{s}$ and $V$ divides $\\overline{RS}$ in the ratio $RV:VS=6:5.$ Find $\\overset{\\to }{OV}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186390558 :question/worked-solution "From the diagram, we see that\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{OV}=\\overset{\\to }{OR}+\\overset{\\to }{RV}.\n$$\n\nWe're told that $\\overset{\\to }{OR}=r,$ so we just need to work out $\\overset{\\to }{RV}.$\n\nFirst, from $△ORS,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{RS} & =\\overset{\\to }{RO}+\\overset{\\to }{OS} \\\\  & =-\\overset{\\to }{OR}+\\overset{\\to }{OS} \\\\  & =-r+s \\\\  & =s-r.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $RV:VS=6:5,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{RV}=\\frac{6}{11}\\overset{\\to }{RS}=\\frac{6}{11}(s-r).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{OV} & =\\overset{\\to }{OR}+\\overset{\\to }{RV} \\\\  & =r+\\frac{6}{11}(s-r) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{11}r+\\frac{6}{11}s.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "From the diagram, we see that\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{OV}=\\overset{\\to }{OR}+\\overset{\\to }{RV}.\n$$\n\nWe're told that $\\overset{\\to }{OR}=\\mathbf{r},$ so we just need to work out $\\overset{\\to }{RV}.$\n\nFirst, from $△ORS,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{RS} & =\\overset{\\to }{RO}+\\overset{\\to }{OS} \\\\  & =-\\overset{\\to }{OR}+\\overset{\\to }{OS} \\\\  & =-\\mathbf{r}+\\mathbf{s} \\\\  & =\\mathbf{s}-\\mathbf{r}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $RV:VS=6:5,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{RV}=\\frac{6}{11}\\overset{\\to }{RS}=\\frac{6}{11}(\\mathbf{s}-\\mathbf{r}).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{OV} & =\\overset{\\to }{OR}+\\overset{\\to }{RV} \\\\  & =\\mathbf{r}+\\frac{6}{11}(\\mathbf{s}-\\mathbf{r}) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{11}\\mathbf{r}+\\frac{6}{11}\\mathbf{s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186390560 :answer/value "\\overset{\\to }{OV}=\\frac{5}{6}r+\\frac{1}{6}s" "\\overset{\\to }{OV}=\\frac{5}{6}\\mathbf{r}+\\frac{1}{6}\\mathbf{s}"] [:db/cas 17592186390561 :answer/value "\\overset{\\to }{OV}=\\frac{6}{11}r+\\frac{5}{11}s" "\\overset{\\to }{OV}=\\frac{6}{11}\\mathbf{r}+\\frac{5}{11}\\mathbf{s}"] [:db/cas 17592186390562 :answer/value "\\overset{\\to }{OV}=r+\\frac{5}{6}s" "\\overset{\\to }{OV}=\\mathbf{r}+\\frac{5}{6}\\mathbf{s}"] [:db/cas 17592186390563 :answer/value "\\overset{\\to }{OV}=\\frac{5}{11}r+\\frac{6}{11}s" "\\overset{\\to }{OV}=\\frac{5}{11}\\mathbf{r}+\\frac{6}{11}\\mathbf{s}"] [:db/cas 17592186390564 :answer/value "\\overset{\\to }{OV}=\\frac{1}{11}r+s" "\\overset{\\to }{OV}=\\frac{1}{11}\\mathbf{r}+\\mathbf{s}"] [:db/cas 17592186403878 :question/problem "![](images/16/1649e6c4ef2874cda2a7d7428b54220ef2a1790ba4b197c755165c48add0c31e.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. Given that $c=\\lambda a+\\mu b$, find $\\lambda $ and $\\mu .$" "![](images/16/1649e6c4ef2874cda2a7d7428b54220ef2a1790ba4b197c755165c48add0c31e.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. Given that $\\mathbf{c}=\\lambda \\mathbf{a}+\\mu \\mathbf{b}$, find $\\lambda $ and $\\mu .$"] [:db/cas 17592186403878 :question/worked-solution "![](images/7c/7cf17440c27765efb34b6a01c12a76ad4e980fc540307e2218227153661db5dd.png)\n\nIn $△OAC$ shown above, we have:\n\n- $OA=2.5|a|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{OA}$ and $a$ have the same direction\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{OA}=2.5a.$\n\nSimilarly:\n\n- $AC=3|b|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{AC}$ and $b$ have the same direction\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{AC}=3b.$\n\nAdditionally, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c & =\\overset{\\to }{OA}+\\overset{\\to }{AC} \\\\  & =2.5a+3b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $c=\\lambda a+\\mu b=2.5a+3b.$ \n\nSince $a∦b,$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients, we get:\n\n$\\,$ $\\lambda =2.5$ and $\\mu =3$" "![](images/7c/7cf17440c27765efb34b6a01c12a76ad4e980fc540307e2218227153661db5dd.png)\n\nIn $△OAC$ shown above, we have:\n\n- $OA=2.5|\\mathbf{a}|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{OA}$ and $\\mathbf{a}$ have the same direction\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{OA}=2.5\\mathbf{a}.$\n\nSimilarly:\n\n- $AC=3|\\mathbf{b}|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{AC}$ and $\\mathbf{b}$ have the same direction\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{AC}=3\\mathbf{b}.$\n\nAdditionally, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{c} & =\\overset{\\to }{OA}+\\overset{\\to }{AC} \\\\  & =2.5\\mathbf{a}+3\\mathbf{b}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $\\mathbf{c}=\\lambda \\mathbf{a}+\\mu \\mathbf{b}=2.5\\mathbf{a}+3\\mathbf{b}.$ \n\nSince $\\mathbf{a}∦\\mathbf{b},$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients, we get:\n\n$\\,$ $\\lambda =2.5$ and $\\mu =3$"] [:db/cas 17592186420450 :answer/value "\\overset{\\to }{OT}=\\frac{1}{4}a+\\frac{3}{4}b" "\\overset{\\to }{OT}=\\frac{1}{4}\\mathbf{a}+\\frac{3}{4}\\mathbf{b}"] [:db/cas 17592186420451 :answer/value "\\overset{\\to }{OT}=\\frac{5}{8}a+\\frac{3}{8}b" "\\overset{\\to }{OT}=\\frac{5}{8}\\mathbf{a}+\\frac{3}{8}\\mathbf{b}"] [:db/cas 17592186420454 :answer/value "\\overset{\\to }{OT}=\\frac{3}{8}a+\\frac{5}{8}b" "\\overset{\\to }{OT}=\\frac{3}{8}\\mathbf{a}+\\frac{5}{8}\\mathbf{b}"] [:db/cas 17592186420458 :answer/value "\\overset{\\to }{OT}=\\frac{3}{4}a+\\frac{1}{4}b" "\\overset{\\to }{OT}=\\frac{3}{4}\\mathbf{a}+\\frac{1}{4}\\mathbf{b}"] [:db/cas 17592186420461 :answer/value "\\overset{\\to }{OT}=\\frac{7}{8}a+\\frac{1}{8}b" "\\overset{\\to }{OT}=\\frac{7}{8}\\mathbf{a}+\\frac{1}{8}\\mathbf{b}"] [:db/cas 17592186420465 :question/problem "![](images/52/52e25ccc5b59b3eecd914c5b141e443475a5de58299404528d27f1fc85db97ea.png)\n\nConsider the diagram above, where $\\overset{\\to }{OA}=a,$ $\\overset{\\to }{OB}=b$, and $T$ divides $\\overset{―}{AB}$ in the ratio $AT:TB=3:5.$ Find $\\overset{\\to }{OT}.$" "![](images/52/52e25ccc5b59b3eecd914c5b141e443475a5de58299404528d27f1fc85db97ea.png)\n\nConsider the diagram above, where $\\overset{\\to }{OA}=\\mathbf{a},$ $\\overset{\\to }{OB}=\\mathbf{b}$, and $T$ divides $\\overline{AB}$ in the ratio $AT:TB=3:5.$ Find $\\overset{\\to }{OT}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186420465 :question/worked-solution "From the diagram, we see that\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{OT}=\\overset{\\to }{OA}+\\overset{\\to }{AT}.\n$$\n\nWe're told that $\\overset{\\to }{OA}=a,$ so we just need to work out $\\overset{\\to }{AT}.$\n\nFirst, from $△OAB,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =\\overset{\\to }{AO}+\\overset{\\to }{OB} \\\\  & =-\\overset{\\to }{OA}+\\overset{\\to }{OB} \\\\  & =-a+b \\\\  & =b-a.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $AT:TB=3:5,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{AT}=\\frac{3}{8}\\overset{\\to }{AB}=\\frac{3}{8}(b-a).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{OT} & =\\overset{\\to }{OA}+\\overset{\\to }{AT} \\\\  & =a+\\frac{3}{8}(b-a) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{8}a+\\frac{3}{8}b.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "From the diagram, we see that\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{OT}=\\overset{\\to }{OA}+\\overset{\\to }{AT}.\n$$\n\nWe're told that $\\overset{\\to }{OA}=\\mathbf{a},$ so we just need to work out $\\overset{\\to }{AT}.$\n\nFirst, from $△OAB,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =\\overset{\\to }{AO}+\\overset{\\to }{OB} \\\\  & =-\\overset{\\to }{OA}+\\overset{\\to }{OB} \\\\  & =-\\mathbf{a}+\\mathbf{b} \\\\  & =\\mathbf{b}-\\mathbf{a}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $AT:TB=3:5,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{AT}=\\frac{3}{8}\\overset{\\to }{AB}=\\frac{3}{8}(\\mathbf{b}-\\mathbf{a}).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{OT} & =\\overset{\\to }{OA}+\\overset{\\to }{AT} \\\\  & =\\mathbf{a}+\\frac{3}{8}(\\mathbf{b}-\\mathbf{a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{8}\\mathbf{a}+\\frac{3}{8}\\mathbf{b}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186420466 :question/problem "![](images/8c/8cf7d0520b1d0654d5749651595acd48b7a08f2b2d55142b18ca63b2ded20f55.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. Given that $c=\\lambda a+2b$, find $\\lambda .$" "![](images/8c/8cf7d0520b1d0654d5749651595acd48b7a08f2b2d55142b18ca63b2ded20f55.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. Given that $\\mathbf{c}=\\lambda \\mathbf{a}+2\\mathbf{b}$, find $\\lambda .$"] [:db/cas 17592186420466 :question/worked-solution "To find $\\lambda ,$ we want to match the expression\n\n$$\nc=\\lambda a+2b.\n$$\n\nWe are given that one of the components is $2b.$ So, we first draw the vector $2b$ from the origin. Then we complete the path to the point $C.$\n\n![](images/9e/9edea1e3ad727d11d7bfac884f27c1b435233fa7df561dbe2a4f3af45ea16bfa.png)\n\nIn $△OBC$, we have:\n\n- $BC=3|a|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{BC}$ and $a$ have the same direction\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{BC}=3a.$ \n\nAdditionally, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c & =\\overset{\\to }{OB}+\\overset{\\to }{BC} \\\\  & =2b+3a.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $c=2b+\\lambda a=2b+3a.$ \n\nSince $a∦b,$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients of $a$, we get:\n\n$$\n\\lambda =3\n$$" "To find $\\lambda ,$ we want to match the expression\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{c}=\\lambda \\mathbf{a}+2\\mathbf{b}.\n$$\n\nWe are given that one of the components is $2\\mathbf{b}.$ So, we first draw the vector $2\\mathbf{b}$ from the origin. Then we complete the path to the point $C.$\n\n![](images/9e/9edea1e3ad727d11d7bfac884f27c1b435233fa7df561dbe2a4f3af45ea16bfa.png)\n\nIn $△OBC$, we have:\n\n- $BC=3|\\mathbf{a}|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{BC}$ and $\\mathbf{a}$ have the same direction\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{BC}=3\\mathbf{a}.$ \n\nAdditionally, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{c} & =\\overset{\\to }{OB}+\\overset{\\to }{BC} \\\\  & =2\\mathbf{b}+3\\mathbf{a}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $\\mathbf{c}=2\\mathbf{b}+\\lambda \\mathbf{a}=2\\mathbf{b}+3\\mathbf{a}.$ \n\nSince $\\mathbf{a}∦\\mathbf{b},$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients of $\\mathbf{a}$, we get:\n\n$$\n\\lambda =3\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186420467 :question/problem "![](images/4b/4bb7598c9ebae28be572508974cd025caa8f4a6c3313f025ff0f5ca55296334e.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. Given that $h=\\lambda f+\\mu g$, find $\\lambda $ and $\\mu .$" "![](images/4b/4bb7598c9ebae28be572508974cd025caa8f4a6c3313f025ff0f5ca55296334e.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. Given that $\\mathbf{h}=\\lambda \\mathbf{f}+\\mu \\mathbf{g}$, find $\\lambda $ and $\\mu .$"] [:db/cas 17592186420467 :question/worked-solution "![](images/43/43642acdb991827b4e1e16744e8a69507a383a1319ae07efde78480af4bab166.png)\n\nIn $△OFH$ shown above, we have \n\n- $OF=2|g|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{OF}$ and $g$ have the same direction\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{OF}=2g.$\n\nSimilarly:\n\n- $FH=3|f|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{FH}$ has the opposite direction to $f$\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{FH}=-3f.$\n\nAdditionally, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}h & =\\overset{\\to }{OF}+\\overset{\\to }{FH} \\\\  & =2g-3f \\\\  & =-3f+2g.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $h=\\lambda f+\\mu g=-3f+2g.$\n\nSince $f∦g,$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients, we get:\n\n- $\\lambda =-3$ and $\\mu =2$" "![](images/43/43642acdb991827b4e1e16744e8a69507a383a1319ae07efde78480af4bab166.png)\n\nIn $△OFH$ shown above, we have \n\n- $OF=2|\\mathbf{g}|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{OF}$ and $\\mathbf{g}$ have the same direction\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{OF}=2\\mathbf{g}.$\n\nSimilarly:\n\n- $FH=3|\\mathbf{f}|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{FH}$ has the opposite direction to $\\mathbf{f}$\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{FH}=-3\\mathbf{f}.$\n\nAdditionally, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{h} & =\\overset{\\to }{OF}+\\overset{\\to }{FH} \\\\  & =2\\mathbf{g}-3\\mathbf{f} \\\\  & =-3\\mathbf{f}+2\\mathbf{g}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $\\mathbf{h}=\\lambda \\mathbf{f}+\\mu \\mathbf{g}=-3\\mathbf{f}+2\\mathbf{g}.$\n\nSince $\\mathbf{f}∦\\mathbf{g},$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients, we get:\n\n- $\\lambda =-3$ and $\\mu =2$"] [:db/cas 17592186514754 :question/problem "![](images/b6/b6e5e69988b962627f39af49e25a904769a7de6bbebf8193e19d1540895ef992.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. If $r=\\lambda p+\\mu q$, then$\\,\\lambda =$\n{{field-1}}\nand \n$\\mu =$\n{{field-2}}." "![](images/b6/b6e5e69988b962627f39af49e25a904769a7de6bbebf8193e19d1540895ef992.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. If $\\mathbf{r}=\\lambda \\mathbf{p}+\\mu \\mathbf{q}$, then$\\,\\lambda =$\n{{field-1}}\nand \n$\\mu =$\n{{field-2}}."] [:db/cas 17592186514754 :question/worked-solution "![](images/6b/6be1c7aa854a75ea143bc6b0b70317128d3f83f7868db4eb1ad50b326d223b8c.png)\n\nIn $△OMR$ shown above, we have:\n\n- $OM=\\frac{1}{4}|q|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{OM}$ and $q$ have the same direction\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{OM}=\\frac{1}{4}q.$\n\nSimilarly:\n\n- $MR=\\frac{4}{3}|p|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{MR}$ has the opposite direction to $p$\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{MR}=-\\frac{4}{3}p.$\n\nAdditionally, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\overset{\\to }{OM}+\\overset{\\to }{MR} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}q-\\frac{4}{3}p \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{3}p+\\frac{1}{4}q.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $$\n\\displaystyle r=\\lambda p+\\mu q=-\\frac{4}{3}p+\\frac{1}{4}q.\n$$\n\nSince $p∦q,$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients, we get:\n\n- $\\lambda =-\\frac{4}{3}$ and $\\mu =\\frac{1}{4}$" "![](images/6b/6be1c7aa854a75ea143bc6b0b70317128d3f83f7868db4eb1ad50b326d223b8c.png)\n\nIn $△OMR$ shown above, we have:\n\n- $OM=\\frac{1}{4}|\\mathbf{q}|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{OM}$ and $\\mathbf{q}$ have the same direction\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{OM}=\\frac{1}{4}\\mathbf{q}.$\n\nSimilarly:\n\n- $MR=\\frac{4}{3}|\\mathbf{p}|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{MR}$ has the opposite direction to $\\mathbf{p}$\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{MR}=-\\frac{4}{3}\\mathbf{p}.$\n\nAdditionally, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{r} & =\\overset{\\to }{OM}+\\overset{\\to }{MR} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\mathbf{q}-\\frac{4}{3}\\mathbf{p} \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{3}\\mathbf{p}+\\frac{1}{4}\\mathbf{q}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $$\n\\displaystyle \\mathbf{r}=\\lambda \\mathbf{p}+\\mu \\mathbf{q}=-\\frac{4}{3}\\mathbf{p}+\\frac{1}{4}\\mathbf{q}.\n$$\n\nSince $\\mathbf{p}∦\\mathbf{q},$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients, we get:\n\n- $\\lambda =-\\frac{4}{3}$ and $\\mu =\\frac{1}{4}$"] [:db/cas 17592186514764 :question/problem "![](images/df/df3c05b58a8b6831ee666a527e80a506ed2b6c0a7c7046849689381c8afe2dce.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. If $r=\\lambda p+\\mu q$, then$\\,\\lambda =$\n{{field-1}}\nand \n$\\mu =$\n{{field-2}}." "![](images/df/df3c05b58a8b6831ee666a527e80a506ed2b6c0a7c7046849689381c8afe2dce.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. If $\\mathbf{r}=\\lambda \\mathbf{p}+\\mu \\mathbf{q}$, then$\\,\\lambda =$\n{{field-1}}\nand \n$\\mu =$\n{{field-2}}."] [:db/cas 17592186514764 :question/worked-solution "![](images/ea/ea6d05ad928486c371f9d38aa0c8a8e3edc671a2e64c0426557e454e11120705.png)\n\nIn $△OMR$ shown above, we have:\n\n- $OM=\\frac{10}{3}|q|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{OM}$ has the opposite direction to $q$\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{OM}=-\\frac{10}{3}q.$\n\nSimilarly:\n\n- $MR=\\frac{5}{3}|p|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{MR}$ and $p$ have the same direction\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{MR}=\\frac{5}{3}p.$\n\nAdditionally, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\overset{\\to }{OM}+\\overset{\\to }{MR} \\\\  & =-\\frac{10}{3}q+\\frac{5}{3}p \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{3}p-\\frac{10}{3}q.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $$\n\\displaystyle r=\\lambda p+\\mu q=\\frac{5}{3}p-\\frac{10}{3}q.\n$$\n\nSince $p∦q,$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients, we get:\n\n- $\\lambda =\\frac{5}{3}$ and $\\mu =-\\frac{10}{3}$" "![](images/ea/ea6d05ad928486c371f9d38aa0c8a8e3edc671a2e64c0426557e454e11120705.png)\n\nIn $△OMR$ shown above, we have:\n\n- $OM=\\frac{10}{3}|\\mathbf{q}|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{OM}$ has the opposite direction to $\\mathbf{q}$\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{OM}=-\\frac{10}{3}\\mathbf{q}.$\n\nSimilarly:\n\n- $MR=\\frac{5}{3}|\\mathbf{p}|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{MR}$ and $\\mathbf{p}$ have the same direction\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{MR}=\\frac{5}{3}\\mathbf{p}.$\n\nAdditionally, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{r} & =\\overset{\\to }{OM}+\\overset{\\to }{MR} \\\\  & =-\\frac{10}{3}\\mathbf{q}+\\frac{5}{3}\\mathbf{p} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{3}\\mathbf{p}-\\frac{10}{3}\\mathbf{q}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $$\n\\displaystyle \\mathbf{r}=\\lambda \\mathbf{p}+\\mu \\mathbf{q}=\\frac{5}{3}\\mathbf{p}-\\frac{10}{3}\\mathbf{q}.\n$$\n\nSince $\\mathbf{p}∦\\mathbf{q},$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients, we get:\n\n- $\\lambda =\\frac{5}{3}$ and $\\mu =-\\frac{10}{3}$"] [:db/cas 17592186514766 :question/problem "![](images/7c/7c577ee7f4171d488642f9c8f9c8e4204269b05620ed22eae91db72b1f931837.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. If $r=\\lambda p+\\mu q$, then$\\,\\lambda =$\n{{field-1}}\nand \n$\\mu =$\n{{field-2}}." "![](images/7c/7c577ee7f4171d488642f9c8f9c8e4204269b05620ed22eae91db72b1f931837.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. If $\\mathbf{r}=\\lambda \\mathbf{p}+\\mu \\mathbf{q}$, then$\\,\\lambda =$\n{{field-1}}\nand \n$\\mu =$\n{{field-2}}."] [:db/cas 17592186514766 :question/worked-solution "![](images/01/0148dd6296370b01a9decbee10a96765be9af88c2f1eb8b9137d59131082c36f.png)\n\nIn $△OPR$ shown above, we have:\n\n- $OP=|p|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{OP}$ and $p$ have the same direction\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{OP}=p.$\n\nSimilarly:\n\n- $PR=|q|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{PR}$ and $q$ have the same direction\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{PR}=q.$\n\nAdditionally, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\overset{\\to }{OP}+\\overset{\\to }{PR} \\\\  & =p+q.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $r=\\lambda p+\\mu q=p+q.$\n\nSince $p∦q,$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients, we get:\n\n- $\\lambda =1$ and $\\mu =1$" "![](images/01/0148dd6296370b01a9decbee10a96765be9af88c2f1eb8b9137d59131082c36f.png)\n\nIn $△OPR$ shown above, we have:\n\n- $OP=|\\mathbf{p}|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{OP}$ and $\\mathbf{p}$ have the same direction\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{OP}=\\mathbf{p}.$\n\nSimilarly:\n\n- $PR=|\\mathbf{q}|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{PR}$ and $\\mathbf{q}$ have the same direction\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{PR}=\\mathbf{q}.$\n\nAdditionally, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{r} & =\\overset{\\to }{OP}+\\overset{\\to }{PR} \\\\  & =\\mathbf{p}+\\mathbf{q}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $\\mathbf{r}=\\lambda \\mathbf{p}+\\mu \\mathbf{q}=\\mathbf{p}+\\mathbf{q}.$\n\nSince $\\mathbf{p}∦\\mathbf{q},$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients, we get:\n\n- $\\lambda =1$ and $\\mu =1$"] [:db/cas 17592186514769 :question/problem "![](images/d5/d590fd2fe368f5f7b460a2e0ec070d92c08b450184372a795e6823985a7df649.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. If $r=\\lambda p+\\mu q$, then$\\,\\lambda =$\n{{field-1}}\nand \n$\\mu =$\n{{field-2}}." "![](images/d5/d590fd2fe368f5f7b460a2e0ec070d92c08b450184372a795e6823985a7df649.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. If $\\mathbf{r}=\\lambda \\mathbf{p}+\\mu \\mathbf{q}$, then$\\,\\lambda =$\n{{field-1}}\nand \n$\\mu =$\n{{field-2}}."] [:db/cas 17592186514769 :question/worked-solution "![](images/2c/2cfab6eaeb4098a384c7807fcd34d7503d2b17d8188f9a6be2e387a4f57a3229.png)\n\nIn $△OPR$ shown above, we have:\n\n- 