\nSince $\\mathbf{p}∦\\mathbf{q},$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients, we get:\n\n- $\\lambda =-\\frac{15}{4}$ and $\\mu =\\frac{4}{3}$"] [:db/cas 17592186514788 :question/problem "![](images/11/1129eb74332cdea7d149aedf6b03abad182ed75d8d5d802ba15ff2503a816c55.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. If $r=\\lambda p+\\mu q$, then$\\,\\lambda =$\n{{field-1}}\nand \n$\\mu =$\n{{field-2}}." "![](images/11/1129eb74332cdea7d149aedf6b03abad182ed75d8d5d802ba15ff2503a816c55.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. If $\\mathbf{r}=\\lambda \\mathbf{p}+\\mu \\mathbf{q}$, then$\\,\\lambda =$\n{{field-1}}\nand \n$\\mu =$\n{{field-2}}."] [:db/cas 17592186514788 :question/worked-solution "![](images/2e/2e22361f45d4f9c98c2558c298fbcddfda78e205ab2ab39f9ee9dd7a534670a2.png)\n\nIn $△OMR$ shown above, we have:\n\n- $OM=\\frac{4}{3}|q|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{OM}$ has the opposite direction to $q$\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{OM}=-\\frac{4}{3}q.$\n\nSimilarly:\n\n- $MR=\\frac{11}{3}|p|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{MR}$ and $p$ have the same direction\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{MR}=\\frac{11}{3}p.$\n\nAdditionally, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\overset{\\to }{OM}+\\overset{\\to }{MR} \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{3}q+\\frac{11}{3}p \\\\  & =\\frac{11}{3}p-\\frac{4}{3}q.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $$\n\\displaystyle r=\\lambda p+\\mu q=\\frac{11}{3}p-\\frac{4}{3}q.\n$$\n\nSince $p∦q,$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients, we get:\n\n- $\\lambda =\\frac{11}{3}$ and $\\mu =-\\frac{4}{3}$" "![](images/2e/2e22361f45d4f9c98c2558c298fbcddfda78e205ab2ab39f9ee9dd7a534670a2.png)\n\nIn $△OMR$ shown above, we have:\n\n- $OM=\\frac{4}{3}|\\mathbf{q}|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{OM}$ has the opposite direction to $\\mathbf{q}$\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{OM}=-\\frac{4}{3}\\mathbf{q}.$\n\nSimilarly:\n\n- $MR=\\frac{11}{3}|\\mathbf{p}|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{MR}$ and $\\mathbf{p}$ have the same direction\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{MR}=\\frac{11}{3}\\mathbf{p}.$\n\nAdditionally, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{r} & =\\overset{\\to }{OM}+\\overset{\\to }{MR} \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{3}\\mathbf{q}+\\frac{11}{3}\\mathbf{p} \\\\  & =\\frac{11}{3}\\mathbf{p}-\\frac{4}{3}\\mathbf{q}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $$\n\\displaystyle \\mathbf{r}=\\lambda \\mathbf{p}+\\mu \\mathbf{q}=\\frac{11}{3}\\mathbf{p}-\\frac{4}{3}\\mathbf{q}.\n$$\n\nSince $\\mathbf{p}∦\\mathbf{q},$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients, we get:\n\n- $\\lambda =\\frac{11}{3}$ and $\\mu =-\\frac{4}{3}$"] [:db/cas 17592186514789 :question/problem "![](images/06/060c4e83c464cfcd60303e524b142bdcc60e9a05125361d84babcdc4bbc79b41.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. If $r=\\lambda p+\\mu q$, then$\\,\\lambda =$\n{{field-1}}\nand \n$\\mu =$\n{{field-2}}." "![](images/06/060c4e83c464cfcd60303e524b142bdcc60e9a05125361d84babcdc4bbc79b41.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. If $\\mathbf{r}=\\lambda \\mathbf{p}+\\mu \\mathbf{q}$, then$\\,\\lambda =$\n{{field-1}}\nand \n$\\mu =$\n{{field-2}}."] [:db/cas 17592186514789 :question/worked-solution "![](images/d7/d7d81b7870205f722a1cc3d23131e573ead2cdfbc66ffcef81d75f31eac6c458.png)\n\nIn $△OPR$ shown above, we have:\n\n- $OP=|p|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{OP}$ and $p$ have the same direction\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{OP}=p.$\n\nSimilarly:\n\n- $PR=2|q|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{PR}$ and $q$ have the same direction\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{PR}=2q.$\n\nAdditionally, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\overset{\\to }{OP}+\\overset{\\to }{PR} \\\\  & =p+2q.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $r=\\lambda p+\\mu q=p+2q.$\n\nSince $p∦q,$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients, we get:\n\n- $\\lambda =1$ and $\\mu =2$" "![](images/d7/d7d81b7870205f722a1cc3d23131e573ead2cdfbc66ffcef81d75f31eac6c458.png)\n\nIn $△OPR$ shown above, we have:\n\n- $OP=|\\mathbf{p}|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{OP}$ and $\\mathbf{p}$ have the same direction\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{OP}=\\mathbf{p}.$\n\nSimilarly:\n\n- $PR=2|\\mathbf{q}|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{PR}$ and $\\mathbf{q}$ have the same direction\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{PR}=2\\mathbf{q}.$\n\nAdditionally, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{r} & =\\overset{\\to }{OP}+\\overset{\\to }{PR} \\\\  & =\\mathbf{p}+2\\mathbf{q}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $\\mathbf{r}=\\lambda \\mathbf{p}+\\mu \\mathbf{q}=\\mathbf{p}+2\\mathbf{q}.$\n\nSince $\\mathbf{p}∦\\mathbf{q},$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients, we get:\n\n- $\\lambda =1$ and $\\mu =2$"] [:db/cas 17592186514792 :question/problem "![](images/c5/c562267ed2e131e42598905fbbd1aac6aa609b94070e39de3fae527cf792a94b.png)\n\nConsider the diagram above, where $\\overset{\\to }{OA}=a,$ $\\overset{\\to }{OB}=b$ and $T$ divides $\\overset{―}{AB}$ in the ratio $AT:TB=4:3.$ Find $\\overset{\\to }{OT}.$" "![](images/c5/c562267ed2e131e42598905fbbd1aac6aa609b94070e39de3fae527cf792a94b.png)\n\nConsider the diagram above, where $\\overset{\\to }{OA}=\\mathbf{a},$ $\\overset{\\to }{OB}=\\mathbf{b}$ and $T$ divides $\\overline{AB}$ in the ratio $AT:TB=4:3.$ Find $\\overset{\\to }{OT}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186514792 :question/worked-solution "From the diagram, we see that\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{OT}=\\overset{\\to }{OA}+\\overset{\\to }{AT}.\n$$\n\nWe're told that $\\overset{\\to }{OA}=a,$ so we just need to work out $\\overset{\\to }{AT}.$\n\nFirst, from $△OAB,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =\\overset{\\to }{AO}+\\overset{\\to }{OB} \\\\  & =-\\overset{\\to }{OA}+\\overset{\\to }{OB} \\\\  & =-a+b \\\\  & =b-a.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $AT:TB=4:3,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{AT}=\\frac{4}{7}\\overset{\\to }{AB}=\\frac{4}{7}(b-a).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{OT} & =\\overset{\\to }{OA}+\\overset{\\to }{AT} \\\\  & =a+\\frac{4}{7}(b-a) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{7}a+\\frac{4}{7}b.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "From the diagram, we see that\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{OT}=\\overset{\\to }{OA}+\\overset{\\to }{AT}.\n$$\n\nWe're told that $\\overset{\\to }{OA}=\\mathbf{a},$ so we just need to work out $\\overset{\\to }{AT}.$\n\nFirst, from $△OAB,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =\\overset{\\to }{AO}+\\overset{\\to }{OB} \\\\  & =-\\overset{\\to }{OA}+\\overset{\\to }{OB} \\\\  & =-\\mathbf{a}+\\mathbf{b} \\\\  & =\\mathbf{b}-\\mathbf{a}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $AT:TB=4:3,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{AT}=\\frac{4}{7}\\overset{\\to }{AB}=\\frac{4}{7}(\\mathbf{b}-\\mathbf{a}).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{OT} & =\\overset{\\to }{OA}+\\overset{\\to }{AT} \\\\  & =\\mathbf{a}+\\frac{4}{7}(\\mathbf{b}-\\mathbf{a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{7}\\mathbf{a}+\\frac{4}{7}\\mathbf{b}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186514793 :question/problem "![](images/6a/6a68fe1a254748519e02fc6f1404a19fc292420bb653d8dd5cc480ce5ad35bb2.png)\n\nConsider the diagram above, where $\\overset{\\to }{OA}=a,$ $\\overset{\\to }{OB}=b$ and $T$ divides $\\overset{―}{AB}$ in the ratio $AT:TB=15:13.$ Find $\\overset{\\to }{OT}.$" "![](images/6a/6a68fe1a254748519e02fc6f1404a19fc292420bb653d8dd5cc480ce5ad35bb2.png)\n\nConsider the diagram above, where $\\overset{\\to }{OA}=\\mathbf{a},$ $\\overset{\\to }{OB}=\\mathbf{b}$ and $T$ divides $\\overline{AB}$ in the ratio $AT:TB=15:13.$ Find $\\overset{\\to }{OT}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186514793 :question/worked-solution "From the diagram, we see that\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{OT}=\\overset{\\to }{OA}+\\overset{\\to }{AT}.\n$$\n\nWe're told that $\\overset{\\to }{OA}=a,$ so we just need to work out $\\overset{\\to }{AT}.$\n\nFirst, from $△OAB,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =\\overset{\\to }{AO}+\\overset{\\to }{OB} \\\\  & =-\\overset{\\to }{OA}+\\overset{\\to }{OB} \\\\  & =-a+b \\\\  & =b-a.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $AT:TB=15:13,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{AT}=\\frac{15}{28}\\overset{\\to }{AB}=\\frac{15}{28}(b-a).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{OT} & =\\overset{\\to }{OA}+\\overset{\\to }{AT} \\\\  & =a+\\frac{15}{28}(b-a) \\\\  & =\\frac{13}{28}a+\\frac{15}{28}b.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "From the diagram, we see that\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{OT}=\\overset{\\to }{OA}+\\overset{\\to }{AT}.\n$$\n\nWe're told that $\\overset{\\to }{OA}=\\mathbf{a},$ so we just need to work out $\\overset{\\to }{AT}.$\n\nFirst, from $△OAB,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =\\overset{\\to }{AO}+\\overset{\\to }{OB} \\\\  & =-\\overset{\\to }{OA}+\\overset{\\to }{OB} \\\\  & =-\\mathbf{a}+\\mathbf{b} \\\\  & =\\mathbf{b}-\\mathbf{a}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $AT:TB=15:13,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{AT}=\\frac{15}{28}\\overset{\\to }{AB}=\\frac{15}{28}(\\mathbf{b}-\\mathbf{a}).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{OT} & =\\overset{\\to }{OA}+\\overset{\\to }{AT} \\\\  & =\\mathbf{a}+\\frac{15}{28}(\\mathbf{b}-\\mathbf{a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{13}{28}\\mathbf{a}+\\frac{15}{28}\\mathbf{b}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186514799 :answer/value "\\overset{\\to }{OT}=-\\frac{13}{28}a+\\frac{15}{28}b" "\\overset{\\to }{OT}=-\\frac{13}{28}\\mathbf{a}+\\frac{15}{28}\\mathbf{b}"] [:db/cas 17592186514800 :answer/value "\\overset{\\to }{OT}=\\frac{4}{7}a+\\frac{3}{7}b" "\\overset{\\to }{OT}=\\frac{4}{7}\\mathbf{a}+\\frac{3}{7}\\mathbf{b}"] [:db/cas 17592186514801 :answer/value "\\overset{\\to }{OT}=\\frac{3}{5}a+\\frac{2}{5}b" "\\overset{\\to }{OT}=\\frac{3}{5}\\mathbf{a}+\\frac{2}{5}\\mathbf{b}"] [:db/cas 17592186514802 :answer/value "\\overset{\\to }{OT}=-\\frac{2}{5}a+\\frac{3}{5}b" "\\overset{\\to }{OT}=-\\frac{2}{5}\\mathbf{a}+\\frac{3}{5}\\mathbf{b}"] [:db/cas 17592186514803 :answer/value "\\overset{\\to }{OT}=\\frac{15}{28}a+\\frac{13}{28}b" "\\overset{\\to }{OT}=\\frac{15}{28}\\mathbf{a}+\\frac{13}{28}\\mathbf{b}"] [:db/cas 17592186514804 :answer/value "\\overset{\\to }{OT}=-\\frac{3}{7}a+\\frac{4}{7}b" "\\overset{\\to }{OT}=-\\frac{3}{7}\\mathbf{a}+\\frac{4}{7}\\mathbf{b}"] [:db/cas 17592186514806 :answer/value "\\overset{\\to }{OT}=\\frac{13}{28}a+\\frac{15}{28}b" "\\overset{\\to }{OT}=\\frac{13}{28}\\mathbf{a}+\\frac{15}{28}\\mathbf{b}"] [:db/cas 17592186514807 :answer/value "\\overset{\\to }{OT}=-\\frac{17}{30}a+\\frac{13}{30}b" "\\overset{\\to }{OT}=-\\frac{17}{30}\\mathbf{a}+\\frac{13}{30}\\mathbf{b}"] [:db/cas 17592186514811 :answer/value "\\overset{\\to }{OT}=a+\\frac{4}{7}b" "\\overset{\\to }{OT}=\\mathbf{a}+\\frac{4}{7}\\mathbf{b}"] [:db/cas 17592186514813 :answer/value "\\overset{\\to }{OT}=\\frac{17}{30}a-\\frac{13}{30}b" "\\overset{\\to }{OT}=\\frac{17}{30}\\mathbf{a}-\\frac{13}{30}\\mathbf{b}"] [:db/cas 17592186514814 :question/problem "![](images/8e/8ed7afdce3cd7857b315c498649f772dfc561d55dd5b91841e0481117e0a50ea.png)\n\nConsider the diagram above, where $\\overset{\\to }{OA}=a,$ $\\overset{\\to }{OB}=b$ and $T$ divides $\\overset{―}{AB}$ in the ratio $AT:TB=13:11.$ Express $\\overset{\\to }{OT}$ via the vectors $a$ and $b$.\n\n$\\,\\overset{\\to }{OT}=$\n{{field-1}}\n$a+$\n{{field-2}}\n$b$" "![](images/8e/8ed7afdce3cd7857b315c498649f772dfc561d55dd5b91841e0481117e0a50ea.png)\n\nConsider the diagram above, where $\\overset{\\to }{OA}=\\mathbf{a},$ $\\overset{\\to }{OB}=\\mathbf{b}$ and $T$ divides $\\overline{AB}$ in the ratio $AT:TB=13:11.$ Express $\\overset{\\to }{OT}$ via the vectors $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{b}$.\n\n$\\,\\overset{\\to }{OT}=$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\mathbf{a}+$\n{{field-2}}\n$\\mathbf{b}$"] [:db/cas 17592186514814 :question/worked-solution "From the diagram, we see that\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{OT}=\\overset{\\to }{OA}+\\overset{\\to }{AT}.\n$$\n\nWe're told that $\\overset{\\to }{OA}=a,$ so we just need to work out $\\overset{\\to }{AT}.$\n\nFirst, from $△OAB,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =\\overset{\\to }{AO}+\\overset{\\to }{OB} \\\\  & =-\\overset{\\to }{OA}+\\overset{\\to }{OB} \\\\  & =-a+b \\\\  & =b-a.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $AT