lly,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c & =\\overset{\\to }{OP}+\\overset{\\to }{PC} \\\\  & =b-a.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $c=\\lambda a+b=-a+b.$\n\nSince $a∦b,$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients of $a,$ we get:\n\n$$\n\\lambda =-1\n$$" "To find $\\lambda ,$ we want to match the expression\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{c}=\\lambda \\mathbf{a}+\\mathbf{b}.\n$$\n\nWe are given that one of the components is $\\mathbf{b}.$ So, we first draw the vector $\\mathbf{b}$ from the origin. Then we complete the path to the point $C.$\n\n![](images/b2/b2f5f00c7c968d98dd37e2e038b65d00b7cf5accb4aaf2bf2b801f15d3a27082.png)\n\nLet $P$ be the endpoint of the vector $\\mathbf{b}.$ Then\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{c}=\\overset{\\to }{OP}+\\overset{\\to }{PC}.\n$$\n\nIn $△OPC,$ we have:\n\n- $PC=|\\mathbf{a}|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{PC}$ and $\\mathbf{a}$ have opposite directions\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{PC}=-\\mathbf{a}.$\n\nAdditionally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{c} & =\\overset{\\to }{OP}+\\overset{\\to }{PC} \\\\  & =\\mathbf{b}-\\mathbf{a}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $\\mathbf{c}=\\lambda \\mathbf{a}+\\mathbf{b}=-\\mathbf{a}+\\mathbf{b}.$\n\nSince $\\mathbf{a}∦\\mathbf{b},$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients of $\\mathbf{a},$ we get:\n\n$$\n\\lambda =-1\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186572666 :question/problem "![](images/2e/2ea6acbf6574e970a798100729de9a92874bca90b8153db9883daa1cd6d98c2a.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. Given that $c=\\lambda a+2b$, find $\\lambda .$" "![](images/2e/2ea6acbf6574e970a798100729de9a92874bca90b8153db9883daa1cd6d98c2a.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. Given that $\\mathbf{c}=\\lambda \\mathbf{a}+2\\mathbf{b}$, find $\\lambda .$"] [:db/cas 17592186572666 :question/worked-solution "To find $\\lambda ,$ we want to match the expression\n\n$$\nc=\\lambda a+2b.\n$$\n\nWe are given that one of the components is $2b.$ So, we first draw the vector $2b$ from the origin. Then we complete the path to the point $C.$\n\n![](images/3c/3c31e9b0d926de6a3f8c9f35bd816349d6488c8b98864d8576fdbfc395bbd92d.png)\n\nLet $P$ be the endpoint of the vector $2b.$ Then\n\n$$\nc=\\overset{\\to }{OP}+\\overset{\\to }{PC}.\n$$\n\nIn $△OPC,$ we have:\n\n- $PC=3|a|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{PC}$ and $a$ have the same direction\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{PC}=3a.$\n\nAdditionally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c & =\\overset{\\to }{OP}+\\overset{\\to }{PC} \\\\  & =2b+3a.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $c=\\lambda a+2b=3a+2b.$\n\nSince $a∦b,$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients of $a,$ we get:\n\n$$\n\\lambda =3\n$$" "To find $\\lambda ,$ we want to match the expression\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{c}=\\lambda \\mathbf{a}+2\\mathbf{b}.\n$$\n\nWe are given that one of the components is $2\\mathbf{b}.$ So, we first draw the vector $2\\mathbf{b}$ from the origin. Then we complete the path to the point $C.$\n\n![](images/3c/3c31e9b0d926de6a3f8c9f35bd816349d6488c8b98864d8576fdbfc395bbd92d.png)\n\nLet $P$ be the endpoint of the vector $2\\mathbf{b}.$ Then\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{c}=\\overset{\\to }{OP}+\\overset{\\to }{PC}.\n$$\n\nIn $△OPC,$ we have:\n\n- $PC=3|\\mathbf{a}|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{PC}$ and $\\mathbf{a}$ have the same direction\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{PC}=3\\mathbf{a}.$\n\nAdditionally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{c} & =\\overset{\\to }{OP}+\\overset{\\to }{PC} \\\\  & =2\\mathbf{b}+3\\mathbf{a}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $\\mathbf{c}=\\lambda \\mathbf{a}+2\\mathbf{b}=3\\mathbf{a}+2\\mathbf{b}.$\n\nSince $\\mathbf{a}∦\\mathbf{b},$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients of $\\mathbf{a},$ we get:\n\n$$\n\\lambda =3\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186572695 :question/problem "![](images/22/22f309ffc9ff6ae4a43a5faae12e4ed952d65aec70f87a7e71f64594f0c61f92.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. If $c=\\frac{4}{3}a+\\lambda b,$ then$\\,\\lambda =$\n{{field-1}}." "![](images/22/22f309ffc9ff6ae4a43a5faae12e4ed952d65aec70f87a7e71f64594f0c61f92.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. If $\\mathbf{c}=\\frac{4}{3}\\mathbf{a}+\\lambda \\mathbf{b},$ then$\\,\\lambda =$\n{{field-1}}."] [:db/cas 17592186572695 :question/worked-solution "To find $\\lambda ,$ we want to match the expression\n\n$$\nc=\\frac{4}{3}a+\\lambda b.\n$$\n\nWe are given that one of the components is $\\frac{4}{3}a.$ So, we first draw the vector $\\frac{4}{3}a$ from the origin. Then we complete the path to the point $C.$\n\n![](images/b8/b8db5015e22e55282e180b1d81f7a2e1ca544dfe0f468dc4dedb6283bac2da5c.png)\n\nLet $P$ be the endpoint of the vector $\\frac{4}{3}a.$ Then\n\n$$\nc=\\overset{\\to }{OP}+\\overset{\\to }{PC}.\n$$\n\nIn $△OPC,$ we have:\n\n- $PC=\\frac{1}{4}|b|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{PC}$ and $b$ have the same direction\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{PC}=\\frac{1}{4}b.$\n\nAdditionally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c & =\\overset{\\to }{OP}+\\overset{\\to }{PC} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}a+\\frac{1}{4}b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $$\n\\displaystyle c=\\frac{4}{3}a+\\lambda b=\\frac{4}{3}a+\\frac{1}{4}b.\n$$\n\nSince $a∦b,$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients of $b,$ we get:\n\n$$\n\\lambda =\\frac{1}{4}.\n$$" "To find $\\lambda ,$ we want to match the expression\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{c}=\\frac{4}{3}\\mathbf{a}+\\lambda \\mathbf{b}.\n$$\n\nWe are given that one of the components is $\\frac{4}{3}\\mathbf{a}.$ So, we first draw the vector $\\frac{4}{3}\\mathbf{a}$ from the origin. Then we complete the path to the point $C.$\n\n![](images/b8/b8db5015e22e55282e180b1d81f7a2e1ca544dfe0f468dc4dedb6283bac2da5c.png)\n\nLet $P$ be the endpoint of the vector $\\frac{4}{3}\\mathbf{a}.$ Then\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{c}=\\overset{\\to }{OP}+\\overset{\\to }{PC}.\n$$\n\nIn $△OPC,$ we have:\n\n- $PC=\\frac{1}{4}|\\mathbf{b}|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{PC}$ and $\\mathbf{b}$ have the same direction\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{PC}=\\frac{1}{4}\\mathbf{b}.$\n\nAdditionally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{c} & =\\overset{\\to }{OP}+\\overset{\\to }{PC} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}\\mathbf{a}+\\frac{1}{4}\\mathbf{b}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $$\n\\displaystyle \\mathbf{c}=\\frac{4}{3}\\mathbf{a}+\\lambda \\mathbf{b}=\\frac{4}{3}\\mathbf{a}+\\frac{1}{4}\\mathbf{b}.\n$$\n\nSince $\\mathbf{a}∦\\mathbf{b},$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients of $\\mathbf{b},$ we get:\n\n$$\n\\lambda =\\frac{1}{4}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186572704 :question/problem "![](images/69/695c825905b268e40b7c77c681c608fcedfed4d0c5150f76224c4e60eb8ab045.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. Given that $c=\\lambda a+2b$, find $\\lambda .$" "![](images/69/695c825905b268e40b7c77c681c608fcedfed4d0c5150f76224c4e60eb8ab045.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. Given that $\\mathbf{c}=\\lambda \\mathbf{a}+2\\mathbf{b}$, find $\\lambda .$"] [:db/cas 17592186572704 :question/worked-solution "To find $\\lambda ,$ we want to match the expression\n\n$$\nc=\\lambda a+2b.\n$$\n\nWe are given that one of the components is $2b.$ So, we first draw the vector $2b$ from the origin. Then we complete the path to the point $C.$\n\n![](images/7d/7d388cdd1db5cfe59b447c3fc27b597fc4e42397966935592f56c1c9f4a598e6.png)\n\nLet $P$ be the endpoint of the vector $2b.$ Then\n\n$$\nc=\\overset{\\to }{OP}+\\overset{\\to }{PC}.\n$$\n\nIn $△OPC,$ we have:\n\n- $PC=|a|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{PC}$ and $a$ have the same direction\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{PC}=a.$\n\nAdditionally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c & =\\overset{\\to }{OP}+\\overset{\\to }{PC} \\\\  & =2b+a.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $c=\\lambda a+2b=a+2b.$\n\nSince $a∦b,$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients of $a,$ we get:\n\n$$\n\\lambda =1\n$$" "To find $\\lambda ,$ we want to match the expression\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{c}=\\lambda \\mathbf{a}+2\\mathbf{b}.\n$$\n\nWe are given that one of the components is $2\\mathbf{b}.$ So, we first draw the vector $2\\mathbf{b}$ from the origin. Then we complete the path to the point $C.$\n\n![](images/7d/7d388cdd1db5cfe59b447c3fc27b597fc4e42397966935592f56c1c9f4a598e6.png)\n\nLet $P$ be the endpoint of the vector $2\\mathbf{b}.$ Then\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{c}=\\overset{\\to }{OP}+\\overset{\\to }{PC}.\n$$\n\nIn $△OPC,$ we have:\n\n- $PC=|\\mathbf{a}|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{PC}$ and $\\mathbf{a}$ have the same direction\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{PC}=\\mathbf{a}.$\n\nAdditionally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{c} & =\\overset{\\to }{OP}+\\overset{\\to }{PC} \\\\  & =2\\mathbf{b}+\\mathbf{a}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $\\mathbf{c}=\\lambda \\mathbf{a}+2\\mathbf{b}=\\mathbf{a}+2\\mathbf{b}.$\n\nSince $\\mathbf{a}∦\\mathbf{b},$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients of $\\mathbf{a},$ we get:\n\n$$\n\\lambda =1\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186572708 :question/problem "![](images/5d/5dea155246de4f35697780db7ebf1802cdf5c2899fe42fa494993be2f65dd7f3.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. Given that $h=\\lambda f+\\mu g$, find $\\lambda$ and $\\mu .$" "![](images/5d/5dea155246de4f35697780db7ebf1802cdf5c2899fe42fa494993be2f65dd7f3.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. Given that $\\mathbf{h}=\\lambda \\mathbf{f}+\\mu \\mathbf{g}$, find $\\lambda$ and $\\mu .$"] [:db/cas 17592186572708 :question/worked-solution "![](images/99/9905448ad59cb2f68f5def832811eb34056305d1a535f23387f4403a340cb8f8.png)\n\nIn $△OFH$ shown above, we have \n\n- $OF=\\frac{1}{2}|f|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{OF}$ has the opposite direction to $f$\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{OF}=-\\frac{1}{2}f.$\n\nSimilarly:\n\n- $FH=2|g|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{FH}$ has the opposite direction to $g$\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{FH}=-2g.$\n\nAdditionally, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}h & =\\overset{\\to }{OF}+\\overset{\\to }{FH} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}f-2g.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $h=\\lambda f+\\mu g=-\\frac{1}{2}f-2g.$\n\nSince $f∦g,$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients, we get:\n\n- $\\lambda =-\\frac{1}{2}$ and $\\mu =-2$" "![](images/99/9905448ad59cb2f68f5def832811eb34056305d1a535f23387f4403a340cb8f8.png)\n\nIn $△OFH$ shown above, we have \n\n- $OF=\\frac{1}{2}|\\mathbf{f}|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{OF}$ has the opposite direction to $\\mathbf{f}$\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{OF}=-\\frac{1}{2}\\mathbf{f}.$\n\nSimilarly:\n\n- $FH=2|\\mathbf{g}|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{FH}$ has the opposite direction to $\\mathbf{g}$\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{FH}=-2\\mathbf{g}.$\n\nAdditionally, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{h} & =\\overset{\\to }{OF}+\\overset{\\to }{FH} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\mathbf{f}-2\\mathbf{g}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $\\mathbf{h}=\\lambda \\mathbf{f}+\\mu \\mathbf{g}=-\\frac{1}{2}\\mathbf{f}-2\\mathbf{g}.$\n\nSince $\\mathbf{f}∦\\mathbf{g},$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients, we get:\n\n- $\\lambda =-\\frac{1}{2}$ and $\\mu =-2$"] [:db/cas 17592186572725 :question/problem "![](images/67/6715bda9432c2ba3e61c31f436cd6b7608312da499bb4372944d25db6cf76090.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. Given that $c=\\lambda a+\\mu b$, find $\\lambda$ and $\\mu .$" "![](images/67/6715bda9432c2ba3e61c31f436cd6b7608312da499bb4372944d25db6cf76090.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. Given that $\\mathbf{c}=\\lambda \\mathbf{a}+\\mu \\mathbf{b}$, find $\\lambda$ and $\\mu .$"] [:db/cas 17592186572725 :question/worked-solution "![](images/89/8997fda665e4bba248dd19f5b8fe0d2a2524df341042271a0f8426e0ce152ecd.png)\n\nIn $△OAC$ shown above, we have:\n\n- $OA=3|a|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{OA}$ and $a$ have opposite directions\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{OA}=-3a.$\n\nSimilarly:\n\n- $AC=2|b|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{AC}$ and $b$ have opposite directions\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{AC}=-2b.$\n\nAdditionally, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c & =\\overset{\\to }{OA}+\\overset{\\to }{AC} \\\\  & =-3a-2b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $c=\\lambda a+\\mu b=-3a-2b.$ \n\nSince $a∦b,$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients, we get:\n\n- $\\lambda =-3$ and $\\mu =-2$" "![](images/89/8997fda665e4bba248dd19f5b8fe0d2a2524df341042271a0f8426e0ce152ecd.png)\n\nIn $△OAC$ shown above, we have:\n\n- $OA=3|\\mathbf{a}|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{OA}$ and $\\mathbf{a}$ have opposite directions\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{OA}=-3\\mathbf{a}.$\n\nSimilarly:\n\n- $AC=2|\\mathbf{b}|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{AC}$ and $\\mathbf{b}$ have opposite directions\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{AC}=-2\\mathbf{b}.$\n\nAdditionally, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{c} & =\\overset{\\to }{OA}+\\overset{\\to }{AC} \\\\  & =-3\\mathbf{a}-2\\mathbf{b}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $\\mathbf{c}=\\lambda \\mathbf{a}+\\mu \\mathbf{b}=-3\\mathbf{a}-2\\mathbf{b}.$ \n\nSince $\\mathbf{a}∦\\mathbf{b},$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients, we get:\n\n- $\\lambda =-3$ and $\\mu =-2$"] [:db/cas 17592186582353 :question/problem "![](images/cb/cbd0ffe0fb9e879f2a93557da1aef08622c9e048e14d83c4f4619cf5a1cd47bb.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. Given that $w=\\lambda u+\\mu v$, find $\\lambda$ and $\\mu .$" "![](images/cb/cbd0ffe0fb9e879f2a93557da1aef08622c9e048e14d83c4f4619cf5a1cd47bb.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. Given that $\\mathbf{w}=\\lambda \\mathbf{u}+\\mu \\mathbf{v}$, find $\\lambda$ and $\\mu .$"] [:db/cas 17592186582353 :question/worked-solution "![](images/fb/fba4b74fde44c862267cd2c58852f4f829f5b07ead948b0c18d79e94f7d1c0fc.png)\n\nIn $△OUW$ shown above, we have \n\n- $OU=2|u|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{OU}$ and $u$ have the same direction\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{OU}=2u.$\n\nSimilarly:\n\n- $UW=3|v|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{UW}$ has the opposite direction to $v$\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{UW}=-3v.$\n\nAdditionally, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}w & =\\overset{\\to }{OU}+\\overset{\\to }{UW} \\\\  & =2u-3v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $w=\\lambda u+\\mu v=2u-3v.$\n\nSince $u∦v,$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients, we get:\n\n- $\\lambda =2$ and $\\mu =-3$" "![](images/fb/fba4b74fde44c862267cd2c58852f4f829f5b07ead948b0c18d79e94f7d1c0fc.png)\n\nIn $△OUW$ shown above, we have \n\n- $OU=2|\\mathbf{u}|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{OU}$ and $\\mathbf{u}$ have the same direction\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{OU}=2\\mathbf{u}.$\n\nSimilarly:\n\n- $UW=3|\\mathbf{v}|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{UW}$ has the opposite direction to $\\mathbf{v}$\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{UW}=-3\\mathbf{v}.$\n\nAdditionally, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{w} & =\\overset{\\to }{OU}+\\overset{\\to }{UW} \\\\  & =2\\mathbf{u}-3\\mathbf{v}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $\\mathbf{w}=\\lambda \\mathbf{u}+\\mu \\mathbf{v}=2\\mathbf{u}-3\\mathbf{v}.$\n\nSince $\\mathbf{u}∦\\mathbf{v},$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients, we get:\n\n- $\\lambda =2$ and $\\mu =-3$"] [:db/cas 17592186582357 :question/problem "![](images/eb/eb43aa9d35e08f19fff74f030fc5246428b83f7bce907a890a3b8dfa62571dc2.png)\n\nConsider the diagram above, where $\\overset{\\to }{OC}=c,$ $\\overset{\\to }{OR}=r,$ and $X$ divides $\\overset{―}{CR}$ in the ratio $CX:XR=6:11.$ Express $\\overset{\\to }{OX}$ via the vectors $c$ and $r.$\n\n$\\,\\overset{\\to }{OX}=$\n{{field-1}}\n$c+$\n{{field-2}}\n$r$" "![](images/eb/eb43aa9d35e08f19fff74f030fc5246428b83f7bce907a890a3b8dfa62571dc2.png)\n\nConsider the diagram above, where $\\overset{\\to }{OC}=\\mathbf{c},$ $\\overset{\\to }{OR}=\\mathbf{r},$ and $X$ divides $\\overline{CR}$ in the ratio $CX:XR=6:11.$ Express $\\overset{\\to }{OX}$ via the vectors $\\mathbf{c}$ and $\\mathbf{r}.$\n\n$\\,\\overset{\\to }{OX}=$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\mathbf{c}+$\n{{field-2}}\n$\\mathbf{r}$"] [:db/cas 17592186582357 :question/worked-solution "From the diagram, we see that\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{OX}=\\overset{\\to }{OC}+\\overset{\\to }{CX}.\n$$\n\nWe're told that $\\overset{\\to }{OC}=c,$ so we just need to work out $\\overset{\\to }{CX}.$\n\nFirst, from $△OCR,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{CR} & =\\overset{\\to }{CO}+\\overset{\\to }{OR} \\\\  & =-\\overset{\\to }{OC}+\\overset{\\to }{OR} \\\\  & =-c+r \\\\  & =r-c.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $CX:XR=6:11,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{CX}=\\frac{6}{17}\\overset{\\to }{CR}=\\frac{6}{17}(r-c).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{OX} & =\\overset{\\to }{OC}+\\overset{\\to }{CX} \\\\  & =c+\\frac{6}{17}(r-c) \\\\  & =\\frac{11}{17}c+\\frac{6}{17}r.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "From the diagram, we see that\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{OX}=\\overset{\\to }{OC}+\\overset{\\to }{CX}.\n$$\n\nWe're told that $\\overset{\\to }{OC}=\\mathbf{c},$ so we just need to work out $\\overset{\\to }{CX}.$\n\nFirst, from $△OCR,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{CR} & =\\overset{\\to }{CO}+\\overset{\\to }{OR} \\\\  & =-\\overset{\\to }{OC}+\\overset{\\to }{OR} \\\\  & =-\\mathbf{c}+\\mathbf{r} \\\\  & =\\mathbf{r}-\\mathbf{c}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $CX:XR=6:11,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{CX}=\\frac{6}{17}\\overset{\\to }{CR}=\\frac{6}{17}(\\mathbf{r}-\\mathbf{c}).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{OX} & =\\overset{\\to }{OC}+\\overset{\\to }{CX} \\\\  & =\\mathbf{c}+\\frac{6}{17}(\\mathbf{r}-\\mathbf{c}) \\\\  & =\\frac{11}{17}\\mathbf{c}+\\frac{6}{17}\\mathbf{r}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186105463 :question/worked-solution "The vector equation of the straight line passing through $P$ and parallel to $\\mathbf{v}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r} = \\mathbf{p} + t \\mathbf{v},\n$$\n\nwhere $t \\in (-\\infty, \\infty)$ and $\\mathbf{p}$ is the position vector of $P$.\n\nIn our case, the position vector of the point $P$ is $\\mathbf{p} = {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}3 \\\\ - 1 \\\\ 7\\end{array} \\right]}.$\n\nUsing the formula for the vector equation of a line, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}+t\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix},\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The vector equation of the straight line passing through $P$ and parallel to $\\mathbf{v}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r} = \\mathbf{p} + t \\mathbf{v},\n$$\n\nwhere $t \\in (-\\infty, \\infty)$ and $\\mathbf{p}$ is the position vector of $P$.\n\nIn our case, the position vector of the point $P$ is $\\mathbf{p} = {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}3 \\\\ - 1 \\\\ 7\\end{array} \\right]}.$\n\nUsing the formula for the vector equation of a line, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{r} & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}+t\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix},\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186321386 :answer/value "r=⟨\\sqrt{3},\\,\\frac{11}{2},\\,-7⟩+t⟨1,\\,1,\\,-2⟩,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" "\\mathbf{r}=⟨\\sqrt{3},\\,\\frac{11}{2},\\,-7⟩+t⟨1,\\,1,\\,-2⟩,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )"] [:db/cas 17592186321580 :answer/value "r=⟨1,\\,1,\\,-2⟩+t⟨\\sqrt{3},\\,\\frac{11}{2},\\,-7⟩,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" "\\mathbf{r}=⟨1,\\,1,\\,-2⟩+t⟨\\sqrt{3},\\,\\frac{11}{2},\\,-7⟩,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )"] [:db/cas 17592186321704 :answer/value "r=⟨\\frac{11}{2},\\,\\sqrt{3},\\,-7⟩+t⟨-1,\\,1,\\,-2⟩,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" "\\mathbf{r}=⟨\\frac{11}{2},\\,\\sqrt{3},\\,-7⟩+t⟨-1,\\,1,\\,-2⟩,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )"] [:db/cas 17592186321954 :answer/value "r=⟨1,\\,1,\\,-2⟩+t⟨-7,\\,\\frac{11}{2},\\,\\sqrt{3}⟩,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" "\\mathbf{r}=⟨1,\\,1,\\,-2⟩+t⟨-7,\\,\\frac{11}{2},\\,\\sqrt{3}⟩,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )"] [:db/cas 17592186322964 :answer/value "r=⟨1,\\,1,\\,-2⟩+⟨-7,\\,\\frac{11}{2},\\,\\sqrt{3}⟩" "\\mathbf{r}=⟨1,\\,1,\\,-2⟩+⟨-7,\\,\\frac{11}{2},\\,\\sqrt{3}⟩"] [:db/cas 17592186106291 :question/problem "Find a vector equation of the straight line passing through $Q(\\sqrt{3},\\,\\frac{11}{2},\\,-7)$ and parallel to $v=⟨1,\\,1,\\,-2⟩.$" "Find a vector equation of the straight line passing through $Q(\\sqrt{3},\\,\\frac{11}{2},\\,-7)$ and parallel to $\\mathbf{v}=⟨1,\\,1,\\,-2⟩.$"] [:db/cas 17592186106291 :question/worked-solution "The vector equation of the straight line passing through $Q$ and parallel to $v$ is given by\n\n$$\nr=q+tv,\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )$ and $q$ is the position vector of $Q.$ \n\nIn our case, the position vector of the point $Q$ is $q=⟨\\sqrt{3},\\,\\frac{11}{2},\\,-7⟩.$ \n\nUsing the formula for the vector equation of a line, we have\n\n$$\nr=⟨\\sqrt{3},\\,\\frac{11}{2},\\,-7⟩+t⟨1,\\,1,\\,-2⟩,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$" "The vector equation of the straight line passing through $Q$ and parallel to $\\mathbf{v}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}=\\mathbf{q}+t\\mathbf{v},\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )$ and $\\mathbf{q}$ is the position vector of $Q.$ \n\nIn our case, the position vector of the point $Q$ is $\\mathbf{q}=⟨\\sqrt{3},\\,\\frac{11}{2},\\,-7⟩.$ \n\nUsing the formula for the vector equation of a line, we have\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}=⟨\\sqrt{3},\\,\\frac{11}{2},\\,-7⟩+t⟨1,\\,1,\\,-2⟩,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186321366 :answer/value "r=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}+t\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" "\\mathbf{r}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}+t\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )"] [:db/cas 17592186321846 :answer/value "r=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}" "\\mathbf{r}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186321952 :answer/value "r=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}+t\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix},\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" "\\mathbf{r}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}+t\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix},\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )"] [:db/cas 17592186322312 :answer/value "r=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\en