atrix},\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )"] [:db/cas 17592186491586 :answer/value "r=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}+t\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix},\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" "\\mathbf{r}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}+t\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix},\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )"] [:db/cas 17592186491776 :answer/value "r=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}+t\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix},\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" "\\mathbf{r}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}+t\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix},\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )"] [:db/cas 17592186492050 :question/worked-solution "The vector equation of the straight line passing through $H$ and parallel to $v$ is given by\n\n$$\nr=h+tv,\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )$ and $h$ is the position vector of $H.$ \n\nWe can find a direction vector by computing the displacement vector between the given points $H(1,-2,3)$ and $S(4,-2,1),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\overset{\\to }{HS} \\\\  & =⟨4,-2,1⟩-⟨1,-2,3⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3,0,-2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vector equation of the line is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}+t\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix},\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The vector equation of the straight line passing through $H$ and parallel to $\\mathbf{v}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}=\\mathbf{h}+t\\mathbf{v},\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )$ and $\\mathbf{h}$ is the position vector of $H.$ \n\nWe can find a direction vector by computing the displacement vector between the given points $H(1,-2,3)$ and $S(4,-2,1),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{v} & =\\overrightarrow{HS} \\\\  & =⟨4,-2,1⟩-⟨1,-2,3⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3,0,-2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vector equation of the line is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{r} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}+t\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix},\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186648083 :answer/value "r=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}+t\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" "\\mathbf{r}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}+t\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )"] [:db/cas 17592186648204 :answer/value "r=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}+t\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" "\\mathbf{r}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}+t\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )"] [:db/cas 17592186648379 :answer/value "r=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}+t\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" "\\mathbf{r}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}+t\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )"] [:db/cas 17592186648432 :answer/value "r=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}+t\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" "\\mathbf{r}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}+t\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )"] [:db/cas 17592186648439 :answer/value "r=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}+t\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" "\\mathbf{r}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}+t\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )"] [:db/cas 17592186648628 :question/worked-solution "The vector equation of the straight line passing through $K$ and parallel to $v$ is given by\n\n$$\nr=k+tv,\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )$ and $k$ is the position vector of $K.$ \n\nWe can find a direction vector by computing the displacement vector between the given points $K(1,-4,2)$ and $L(1,-2,3),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\overset{\\to }{KL} \\\\  & =⟨1,-2,3⟩-⟨1,-4,2⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,2,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vector equation of the line is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}+t\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The vector equation of the straight line passing through $K$ and parallel to $\\mathbf{v}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}=\\mathbf{k}+t\\mathbf{v},\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )$ and $\\mathbf{k}$ is the position vector of $K.$ \n\nWe can find a direction vector by computing the displacement vector between the given points $K(1,-4,2)$ and $L(1,-2,3),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{v} & =\\overrightarrow{KL} \\\\  & =⟨1,-2,3⟩-⟨1,-4,2⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,2,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vector equation of the line is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{r} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}+t\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186105465 :question/worked-solution "First, we find the vector equation of the line, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =p+tv \\\\ r & =⟨1,2,0⟩+t⟨-4,1,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t \\in (-\\infty, \\infty).$\n\nSince $A$ lies on the line, we know that there must be a real number $t$ such that\n\n$$\n\\overrightarrow{OA} = \\langle 1, 2, 0 \\rangle + t \\langle -4, 1, 1 \\rangle\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{PA} & =\\overset{\\to }{OA}-\\overset{\\to }{OP} \\\\  & =(⟨1,2,0⟩+t⟨-4,1,1⟩)-⟨1,2,0⟩ \\\\  & =t\\,⟨-4,\\,1,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-4t,\\,t,\\,t⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $PA=6\\sqrt{2},$ we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}PA & =6\\sqrt{2} \\\\ |\\overset{\\to }{PA}| & =6\\sqrt{2} \\\\ |⟨-4t,\\,t,\\,t⟩| & =6\\sqrt{2} \\\\ \\sqrt{(-4t)^{2}+{t}^{2}+{t}^{2}} & =6\\sqrt{2} \\\\ \\sqrt{18{t}^{2}} & =6\\sqrt{2} \\\\ \\pm 3t\\sqrt{2} & =6\\sqrt{2} \\\\ t & =\\pm 2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, putting the values $t=\\pm 2$ into the equation for the line, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\overset{\\to }{O{A}_{1}}=⟨1,2,0⟩+2⟨-4,1,1⟩=⟨-7,4,2⟩, \\\\  & \\overset{\\to }{O{A}_{2}}=⟨1,2,0⟩-2⟨-4,1,1⟩=⟨9,0,-2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the points are $A_1(-7, 4, 2)$ and $A_2(9, 0, -2).$" "First, we find the vector equation of the line, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{r} & =\\mathbf{p}+t\\mathbf{v} \\\\ \\mathbf{r} & =⟨1,2,0⟩+t⟨-4,1,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t \\in (-\\infty, \\infty).$\n\nSince $A$ lies on the line, we know that there must be a real number $t$ such that\n\n$$\n\\overrightarrow{OA} = \\langle 1, 2, 0 \\rangle + t \\langle -4, 1, 1 \\rangle\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overrightarrow{PA} & =\\overrightarrow{OA}-\\overrightarrow{OP} \\\\  & =(⟨1,2,0⟩+t⟨-4,1,1⟩)-⟨1,2,0⟩ \\\\  & =t\\,⟨-4,\\,1,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-4t,\\,t,\\,t⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $PA=6\\sqrt{2},$ we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}PA & =6\\sqrt{2} \\\\ |\\overrightarrow{PA}| & =6\\sqrt{2} \\\\ |⟨-4t,\\,t,\\,t⟩| & =6\\sqrt{2} \\\\ \\sqrt{(-4t)^{2}+{t}^{2}+{t}^{2}} & =6\\sqrt{2} \\\\ \\sqrt{18{t}^{2}} & =6\\sqrt{2} \\\\ \\pm 3t\\sqrt{2} & =6\\sqrt{2} \\\\ t & =\\pm 2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, putting the values $t=\\pm 2$ into the equation for the line, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\overrightarrow{O{A}_{1}}=⟨1,2,0⟩+2⟨-4,1,1⟩=⟨-7,4,2⟩, \\\\  & \\overrightarrow{O{A}_{2}}=⟨1,2,0⟩-2⟨-4,1,1⟩=⟨9,0,-2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the points are $A_1(-7, 4, 2)$ and $A_2(9, 0, -2).$"] [:db/cas 17592186106197 :question/problem "Consider the straight line that passes through $P(3,1,-1)$ and is parallel to $v=⟨2,1,3⟩.$ Find all the possible points $A$ on that line such that $AP=\\sqrt{14}.$" "Consider the straight line that passes through $P(3,1,-1)$ and is parallel to $\\mathbf{v}=⟨2,1,3⟩.$ Find all the possible points $A$ on that line such that $AP=\\sqrt{14}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186106