 number $t$ such that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{OY} & =⟨3,\\,2,\\,1⟩+t⟨1,\\,3,\\,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{PY} & =\\overset{\\to }{OY}-\\overset{\\to }{OP} \\\\  & =(⟨3,\\,2,\\,1⟩+t⟨1,\\,3,\\,0⟩)-⟨3,\\,2,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =t⟨1,\\,3,\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨t,\\,3t,\\,0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $PY=\\sqrt{160},$ we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}YP & =\\sqrt{160} \\\\ |\\overset{\\to }{PY}| & =\\sqrt{160} \\\\ |⟨t,\\,3t,\\,0⟩| & =\\sqrt{160} \\\\ \\sqrt{{t}^{2}+(3t)^{2}+{0}^{2}} & =\\sqrt{160} \\\\ \\sqrt{10{t}^{2}} & =\\sqrt{160} \\\\ \\pm \\sqrt{10}t & =4\\sqrt{10} \\\\ t & =\\pm 4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, putting the values $t=\\pm 4$ into the equation for the line, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\overset{\\to }{O{Y}_{1}}=⟨3,\\,2,\\,1⟩+4⟨1,\\,3,\\,0⟩=⟨7,14,1⟩, \\\\  & \\overset{\\to }{O{Y}_{2}}=⟨3,\\,2,\\,1⟩-4⟨1,\\,3,\\,0⟩=⟨-1,-10,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the points are ${Y}_{1}(7,14,1)$ and ${Y}_{2}(-1,-10,1).$" "First, we find the vector equation of the line, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{r} & =\\mathbf{p}+t\\mathbf{v} \\\\ \\mathbf{r} & =⟨3,\\,2,\\,1⟩+t⟨1,\\,3,\\,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nSince $Y$ lies on the line, we know that there must be a real number $t$ such that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overrightarrow{OY} & =⟨3,\\,2,\\,1⟩+t⟨1,\\,3,\\,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overrightarrow{PY} & =\\overrightarrow{OY}-\\overrightarrow{OP} \\\\  & =(⟨3,\\,2,\\,1⟩+t⟨1,\\,3,\\,0⟩)-⟨3,\\,2,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =t⟨1,\\,3,\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨t,\\,3t,\\,0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $PY=\\sqrt{160},$ we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}YP & =\\sqrt{160} \\\\ |\\overrightarrow{PY}| & =\\sqrt{160} \\\\ |⟨t,\\,3t,\\,0⟩| & =\\sqrt{160} \\\\ \\sqrt{{t}^{2}+(3t)^{2}+{0}^{2}} & =\\sqrt{160} \\\\ \\sqrt{10{t}^{2}} & =\\sqrt{160} \\\\ \\pm \\sqrt{10}t & =4\\sqrt{10} \\\\ t & =\\pm 4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, putting the value