ine is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}=\\mathbf{p}+t\\mathbf{v}\\,⟹\\,\\mathbf{r}=\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}+t\\begin{bmatrix}-8 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nand the vector equation of the  line segment $\\overline{PQ}$ is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}+t\\begin{bmatrix}-8 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,t\\in [0,1].\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\na+b+c=0+1+1=2.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186281394 :question/problem "The equation below gives a parametrization of the line segment $\\overset{―}{PQ},$ where $P(3,2,-1)$ and $Q(0,1,-3).$\n\n$$\nr(t)=a\\,i+b\\,j-(2t+1)\\,k,\\,t\\in [0,c]\n$$\n\nWhat is $a+b+c?$" "The equation below gives a parametrization of the line segment $\\overline{PQ},$ where $P(3,2,-1)$ and $Q(0,1,-3).$\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(t)=a\\,\\mathbf{i}+b\\,\\mathbf{j}-(2t+1)\\,\\mathbf{k},\\,t\\in [0,c]\n$$\n\nWhat is $a+b+c?$"] [:db/cas 17592186281394 :question/worked-solution "We need to find a direction vector that is parallel to the given line. We can use the vector $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\overset{\\to }{PQ} \\\\  & =\\overset{\\to }{OQ}-\\overset{\\to }{OP} \\\\  & =(j-3\\,k)-(3\\,i+2\\,j-k) \\\\  & =-3\\,i-j-2\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the vector equation of the line is\n\n$$\nr=p+tv\\,⟹\\,r=(3\\,i+2\\,j-k)+t(-3\\,i-j-2\\,k)\n$$\n\nand the parametrization of the line segment $\\overset{―}{PQ}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =(3\\,i+2\\,j-k)+t(-3\\,i-j-2\\,k) \\\\  & =(3-3t)\\,i+(2-t)\\,j-(2t+1)\\,k,\\,t\\in [0,1].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\na+b+c=(3-3t)+(2-t)+1=6-4t.\n$$" "We need to find a direction vector that is parallel to the given line. We can use the vector $\\overrightarrow{PQ}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{v} & =\\overrightarrow{PQ} \\\\  & =\\overrightarrow{OQ}-\\overrightarrow{OP} \\\\  & =(\\mathbf{j}-3\\,\\mathbf{k})-(3\\,\\mathbf{i}+2\\,\\mathbf{j}-\\mathbf{k}) \\\\  & =-3\\,\\mathbf{i}-\\mathbf{j}-2\\,\\mathbf{k}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the vector equation of the line is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}=\\mathbf{p}+t\\mathbf{v}\\,⟹\\,\\mathbf{r}=(3\\,\\mathbf{i}+2\\,\\mathbf{j}-\\mathbf{k})+t(-3\\,\\mathbf{i}-\\mathbf{j}-2\\,\\mathbf{k})\n$$\n\nand the parametrization of the line segment $\\overline{PQ}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{r}(t) & =(3\\,\\mathbf{i}+2\\,\\mathbf{j}-\\mathbf{k})+t(-3\\,\\mathbf{i}-\\mathbf{j}-2\\,\\mathbf{k}) \\\\  & =(3-3t)\\,\\mathbf{i}+(2-t)\\,\\mathbf{j}-(2t+1)\\,\\mathbf{k},\\,t\\in [0,1].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\na+b+c=(3-3t)+(2-t)+1=6-4t.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186281395 :question/problem "The equation below gives a parametrization of the line segment $\\overset{―}{PQ},$ where $P(-1,1,3)$ and $Q(0,6,-1).$\n\n$$\nr(t)=a\\,i+(5t+1)\\,j+b\\,k,\\,t\\in [c,1]\n$$\n\nWhat is $a+b+c?$" "The equation below gives a parametrization of the line segment $\\overline{PQ},$ where $P(-1,1,3)$ and $Q(0,6,-1).$\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(t)=a\\,\\mathbf{i}+(5t+1)\\,\\mathbf{j}+b\\,\\mathbf{k},\\,t\\in [c,1]\n$$\n\nWhat is $a+b+c?$"] [:db/cas 17592186281395 :question/worked-solution "We need to find a direction vector that is parallel to the given line. We can use the vector $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\overset{\\to }{PQ} \\\\  & =\\overset{\\to }{OQ}-\\overset{\\to }{OP} \\\\  & =(6\\,j-k)-(-i+j+3\\,k) \\\\  & =i+5\\,j-4\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the vector equation of the line is\n\n$$\nr=p+tv\\,⟹\\,r=(-i+j+3\\,k)+t(i+5\\,j-4\\,k)\n$$\n\nand the parametrization of the line segment $\\overset{―}{PQ}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =(-i+j+3\\,k)+t(i+5\\,j-4\\,k) \\\\  & =(t-1)\\,i+(5t+1)\\,j+(3-4t)\\,k,\\,t\\in [0,1].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\na+b+c=(t-1)+(3-4t)+0=2-3t.\n$$" "We need to find a direction vector that is parallel to the given line. We can use the vector $\\overrightarrow{PQ}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{v} & =\\overrightarrow{PQ} \\\\  & =\\overrightarrow{OQ}-\\overrightarrow{OP} \\\\  & =(6\\,\\mathbf{j}-\\mathbf{k})-(-\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j}+3\\,\\mathbf{k}) \\\\  & =\\mathbf{i}+5\\,\\mathbf{j}-4\\,\\mathbf{k}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the vector equation of the line is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}=\\mathbf{p}+t\\mathbf{v}\\,⟹\\,\\mathbf{r}=(-\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j}+3\\,\\mathbf{k})+t(\\mathbf{i}+5\\,\\mathbf{j}-4\\,\\mathbf{k})\n$$\n\nand the parametrization of the line segment $\\overline{PQ}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{r}(t) & =(-\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j}+3\\,\\mathbf{k})+t(\\mathbf{i}+5\\,\\mathbf{j}-4\\,\\mathbf{k}) \\\\  & =(t-1)\\,\\mathbf{i}+(5t+1)\\,\\mathbf{j}+(3-4t)\\,\\mathbf{k},\\,t\\in [0,1].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\na+b+c=(t-1)+(3-4t)+0=2-3t.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186281398 :question/problem "The equation below gives a parametrization of the line segment $\\overset{―}{PQ},$ where $P(-4,0,-3)$ and $Q(-6,2,-5).$\n\n$$\nr(t)=-a\\,i+2t\\,j-b\\,k,\\,t\\in [0,c]\n$$\n\nWhat is $a+b+c?$" "The equation below gives a parametrization of the line segment $\\overline{PQ},$ where $P(-4,0,-3)$ and $Q(-6,2,-5).$\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(t)=-a\\,\\mathbf{i}+2t\\,\\mathbf{j}-b\\,\\mathbf{k},\\,t\\in [0,c]\n$$\n\nWhat is $a+b+c?$"] [:db/cas 17592186281398 :question/worked-solution "We need to find a direction vector that is parallel to the given line. We can use the vector $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\overset{\\to }{PQ} \\\\  & =\\overset{\\to }{OQ}-\\overset{\\to }{OP} \\\\  & =(-6\\,i+2\\,j-5\\,k)-(-4\\,i-3\\,k) \\\\  & =-2\\,i+2\\,j-2\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the vector equation of the line is\n\n$$\nr=p+tv\\,⟹\\,r=(-4\\,i-3\\,k)+t(-2\\,i+2\\,j-2\\,k)\n$$\n\nand the parametrization of the line segment $\\overset{―}{PQ}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =(-4\\,i-3\\,k)+t(-2\\,i+2\\,j-2\\,k) \\\\  & =-(2t+4)\\,i+2t\\,j-(2t+3)\\,k,\\,t\\in [0,1].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\na+b+c=(2t+4)+(2t+3)+1=4t+8.\n$$" "We need to find a direction vector that is parallel to the given line. We can use the vector $\\overrightarrow{PQ}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{v} & =\\overrightarrow{PQ} \\\\  & =\\overrightarrow{OQ}-\\overrightarrow{OP} \\\\  & =(-6\\,\\mathbf{i}+2\\,\\mathbf{j}-5\\,\\mathbf{k})-(-4\\,\\mathbf{i}-3\\,\\mathbf{k}) \\\\  & =-2\\,\\mathbf{i}+2\\,\\mathbf{j}-2\\,\\mathbf{k}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the vector equation of the line is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}=\\mathbf{p}+t\\mathbf{v}\\,⟹\\,\\mathbf{r}=(-4\\,\\mathbf{i}-3\\,\\mathbf{k})+t(-2\\,\\mathbf{i}+2\\,\\mathbf{j}-2\\,\\mathbf{k})\n$$\n\nand the parametrization of the line segment $\\overline{PQ}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{r}(t) & =(-4\\,\\mathbf{i}-3\\,\\mathbf{k})+t(-2\\,\\mathbf{i}+2\\,\\mathbf{j}-2\\,\\mathbf{k}) \\\\  & =-(2t+4)\\,\\mathbf{i}+2t\\,\\mathbf{j}-(2t+3)\\,\\mathbf{k},\\,t\\in [0,1].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\na+b+c=(2t+4)+(2t+3)+1=4t+8.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186281400 :question/problem "The equation below gives a parametrization of the line segment $\\overset{―}{PQ},$ where $P(1,-1,1)$ and $Q(2,-3,0).$\n\n$$\nr(t)=a\\,i-(2t+1)\\,j+b\\,k,\\,t\\in [c,1]\n$$\n\nWhat is $a+b+c?$" "The equation below gives a parametrization of the line segment $\\overline{PQ},$ where $P(1,-1,1)$ and $Q(2,-3,0).$\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(t)=a\\,\\mathbf{i}-(2t+1)\\,\\mathbf{j}+b\\,\\mathbf{k},\\,t\\in [c,1]\n$$\n\nWhat is $a+b+c?$"] [:db/cas 17592186281400 :question/worked-solution "We need to find a direction vector that is parallel to the given line. We can use the vector $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\overset{\\to }{PQ} \\\\  & =\\overset{\\to }{OQ}-\\overset{\\to }{OP} \\\\  & =(2\\,i-3\\,j)-(i-j+k) \\\\  & =i-2\\,j-k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the vector equation of the line is\n\n$$\nr=p+tv\\,⟹\\,r=(i-j+k)+t(i-2\\,j-k)\n$$\n\nand the parametrization of the line segment $\\overset{―}{PQ}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =(i-j+k)+t(i-2\\,j-k) \\\\  & =(t+1)\\,i-(2t+1)\\,j+(1-t)\\,k,\\,t\\in [0,1].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\na+b+c=(t+1)+(1-t)+0=2.\n$$" "We need to find a direction vector that is parallel to the given line. We can use the vector $\\overrightarrow{PQ}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{v} & =\\overrightarrow{PQ} \\\\  & =\\overrightarrow{OQ}-\\overrightarrow{OP} \\\\  & =(2\\,\\mathbf{i}-3\\,\\mathbf{j})-(\\mathbf{i}-\\mathbf{j}+\\mathbf{k}) \\\\  & =\\mathbf{i}-2\\,\\mathbf{j}-\\mathbf{k}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the vector equation of the line is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}=\\mathbf{p}+t\\mathbf{v}\\,⟹\\,\\mathbf{r}=(\\mathbf{i}-\\mathbf{j}+\\mathbf{k})+t(\\mathbf{i}-2\\,\\mathbf{j}-\\mathbf{k})\n$$\n\nand the parametrization of the line segment $\\overline{PQ}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{r}(t) & =(\\mathbf{i}-\\mathbf{j}+\\mathbf{k})+t(\\mathbf{i}-2\\,\\mathbf{j}-\\mathbf{k}) \\\\  & =(t+1)\\,\\mathbf{i}-(2t+1)\\,\\mathbf{j}+(1-t)\\,\\mathbf{k},\\,t\\in [0,1].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\na+b+c=(t+1)+(1-t)+0=2.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186281402 :question/problem "The equation below gives a parametrization of the line segment $\\overset{―}{PQ},$ where $P(4,3,5)$ and $Q(6,3,1).$\n\n$$\nr(t)=(2t+4)\\,i+a\\,j+b\\,k,\\,t\\in [0,c]\n$$\n\nWhat is $a+b+c?$" "The equation below gives a parametrization of the line segment $\\overline{PQ},$ where $P(4,3,5)$ and $Q(6,3,1).$\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(t)=(2t+4)\\,\\mathbf{i}+a\\,\\mathbf{j}+b\\,\\mathbf{k},\\,t\\in [0,c]\n$$\n\nWhat is $a+b+c?$"] [:db/cas 17592186281402 :question/worked-solution "We need to find a direction vector that is parallel to the given line. We can use the vector $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\overset{\\to }{PQ} \\\\  & =\\overset{\\to }{OQ}-\\overset{\\to }{OP} \\\\  & =(6\\,i+3\\,j+k)-(4\\,i+3\\,j+5\\,k) \\\\  & =2\\,i-4\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the vector equation of the line is\n\n$$\nr=p+tv\\,⟹\\,r=(4\\,i+3\\,j+5\\,k)+t(2\\,i-4\\,k)\n$$\n\nand the parametrization of the line segment $\\overset{―}{PQ}$ is\n\n$$\n\\be