nd{bmatrix}+t\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ b\\end{bmatrix},\\,t\\in [0,c]\n$$\n\nWhat is the value of $a+b+c?$"] [:db/cas 17592186637570 :question/worked-solution "We need to find a direction vector that is parallel to the given line. We can use the vector $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\overset{\\to }{PQ} \\\\  & =\\overset{\\to }{OQ}-\\overset{\\to }{OP} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -3 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the vector equation of the line is\n\n$$\nr=p+tv\\,⟹\\,r=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+t\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nand the vector equation of the  line segment $\\overset{―}{PQ}$ is\n\n$$\nr(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+t\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix},\\,t\\in [0,1].\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\na+b+c=(-2)+(-2)+1=-3.\n$$" "We need to find a direction vector that is parallel to the given line. We can use the vector $\\overrightarrow{PQ}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{v} & =\\overrightarrow{PQ} \\\\  & =\\overrightarrow{OQ}-\\overrightarrow{OP} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -3 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the vector equation of the line is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}=\\mathbf{p}+t\\mathbf{v}\\,⟹\\,\\mathbf{r}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+t\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nand the vector equation of the  line segment $\\overline{PQ}$ is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+t\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix},\\,t\\in [0,1].\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\na+b+c=(-2)+(-2)+1=-3.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186107157 :tutorial/content "Suppose we are given a point $P$ with position vector $\\mathbf{p},$ and another vector $\\mathbf{v}.$ \n\n![](images/b1/b1bb8d0cbe5dcbb24e224e8efc77e32db7a1657b245666aebd7b18107ee41149.png)\n\nThe **vector equation** of the line that is parallel to $\\mathbf{v}$ and passes through $P$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=p+tv,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vector $\\mathbf{v}$ is often called the **direction vector** of the line, while the vector $\\mathbf{r}$ denotes the position vector of an arbitrary point on the line.\n\nFor example, if\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{v}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix},\n\t\\qquad\n\t\\mathbf{p}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nthe vector equation of the line  that is parallel to $\\mathbf{v}$ and passes through $P$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}+t\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix},\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Suppose we are given a point $P$ with position vector $\\mathbf{p},$ and another vector $\\mathbf{v}.$ \n\n![](images/b1/b1bb8d0cbe5dcbb24e224e8efc77e32db7a1657b245666aebd7b18107ee41149.png)\n\nThe **vector equation** of the line that is parallel to $\\mathbf{v}$ and passes through $P$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{r}=\\mathbf{p}+t\\mathbf{v},\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vector $\\mathbf{v}$ is often called the **direction vector** of the line, while the vector $\\mathbf{r}$ denotes the position vector of an arbitrary point on the line.\n\nFor example, if\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{v}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix},\n\t\\qquad\n\t\\mathbf{p}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nthe vector equation of the line  that is parallel to $\\mathbf{v}$ and passes through $P$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{r} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}+t\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix},\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186107159 :tutorial/content "![](images/44/443bbe1d201f1919fa2fb7ce3b632715cdc0e8dc77ff94bdc89f2cff7402b772.png)\n\nSuppose we wish to find a vector equation of the line *segment* whose endpoints are $P(3,-1,7)$ and $Q(3,4,4).$ \n\nTo do this, we need to find the vector equation of the corresponding line and then restrict the domain of the parameter $t.$\n\nFirst, we need to find a direction vector parallel to the given line. We can use the vector $\\overrightarrow{PQ}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\overset{\\to }{PQ} \\\\  & =\\overset{\\to }{OQ}-\\overset{\\to }{OP} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the vector equation of the line $\\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{PQ}$ is \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r} = \\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix} + t\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}\n=\n\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 5t-1 \\\\ 7-3t\\end{bmatrix}, \\qquad t\\in (-\\infty,\\infty).\n$$\n\nThis equation describes every point on the line $\\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{PQ}.$ \n\nTo restrict this to points on the segment $\\overline{PQ}$ only, we set $t\\in [0,1].$ Thus, the vector equation of the line segment $\\overline{PQ}$ is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(t) =\n\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 5t-1 \\\\ 7-3t\\end{bmatrix},\n\\qquad t \\in [0,1].\n$$\n\nWriting the equation of a line segment in this way is sometimes called **parametrizing** the line segment since each point on the segment corresponds to a single parameter $t.$\n\n- Notice that $t=0$ gives us the position vector of $P{:}$\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(0) =\n\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 5(0)-1 \\\\ 7-3(0)\\end{bmatrix}\n=\n\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix} = \\mathbf p\n$$\n\n- Similarly, $t=1$ gives us the position vector of $Q{:}$\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(1) =\n\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 5(1)-1 \\\\ 7-3(1)\\end{bmatrix}\n=\n\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}  = \\mathbf q\n$$\n\nSince increasing $t$ moves our vector $\\mathbf r (t)$ from $P$ to $Q,$ we say that the line segment $\\overline{PQ}$ is **traversed** *from* the point $P$ *to* the point $Q$ under this parametrization." "![](images/44/443bbe1d201f1919fa2fb7ce3b632715cdc0e8dc77ff94bdc89f2cff7402b772.png)\n\nSuppose we wish to find a vector equation of the line *segment* whose endpoints are $P(3,-1,7)$ and $Q(3,4,4).$ \n\nTo do this, we need to find the vector equation of the corresponding line and then restrict the domain of the parameter $t.$\n\nFirst, we need to find a direction vector parallel to the given line. We can use the vector $\\overrightarrow{PQ}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{v} & =\\overrightarrow{PQ} \\\\  & =\\overrightarrow{OQ}-\\overrightarrow{OP} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the vector equation of the line $\\overleftrightarrow{PQ}$ is \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r} = \\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix} + t\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}\n=\n\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 5t-1 \\\\ 7-3t\\end{bmatrix}, \\qquad t\\in (-\\infty,\\infty).\n$$\n\nThis equation describes every point on the line $\\overleftrightarrow{PQ}.$ \n\nTo restrict this to points on the segment $\\overline{PQ}$ only, we set $t\\in [0,1].$ Thus, the vector equation of the line segment $\\overline{PQ}$ is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(t) =\n\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 5t-1 \\\\ 7-3t\\end{bmatrix},\n\\qquad t \\in [0,1].\n$$\n\nWriting the equation of a line segment in this way is sometimes called **parametrizing** the line segment since each point on the segment corresponds to a single parameter $t.$\n\n- Notice that $t=0$ gives us the position vector of $P{:}$\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(0) =\n\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 5(0)-1 \\\\ 7-3(0)\\end{bmatrix}\n=\n\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix} = \\mathbf p\n$$\n\n- Similarly, $t=1$ gives us the position vector of $Q{:}$\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(1) =\n\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 5(1)-1 \\\\ 7-3(1)\\end{bmatrix}\n=\n\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}  = \\mathbf q\n$$\n\nSince increasing $t$ moves our vector $\\mathbf r (t)$ from $P$ to $Q,$ we say that the line segment $\\overline{PQ}$ is **traversed** *from* the point $P$ *to* the point $Q$ under this parametrization."]]